      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>Royal Travellers.—Unlike Queen Victoria, who revelled in Arctic temperatures, Queen Alexandra pre- fers her apartments to be warm, so also does the Princess of Wales, who, from a child has always felt extreme cold exceedingly. Her Royal Highness too, suffers very much in extreme heat, and all this together with the fact that the Princess is a very indifferent sailor, has greatly marred her pleasure when upon her many voyages and travels. Queen Alexandra is a much better sailor and traveller generally than the Princess of Wales, and it is only when yachting in really rough weather, that Her Majesty occasionally succumbs to distressing indisposition. Miss Dorothy Drew.—Some years ago, the world heard a good deal about the comings and goings of Miss Dorothy Drew. That was in the time of her grandfather, the late Mr. W. E. Gladstone, when as a child, she used to run about bare-footed, and was the constant companion of Mr. Gladstone in his latter years. She is now a fair young lady of nineteen years, and was present with the Hawarden party on Mr. Churchill's platform at one of the meetings at the Manchester election. Hawarden Castle belongs to Mr. William Charles Gladstone, now in his twenty-third year, and it would thus appear by their support of Mr. Winston Churchill that both he and his cousin, Miss Dorothy Drew, follow in the political faith of their distinguished grandfather. Over-indulging " Children.—Children a century ago, were kept well in the background as may he gathered from John Ruskin's account of his own boyhood, which he gives in "Prxterita." "I was never allowed," he writes, " to come down to dessert until much later in life, when I was able to crack nuts neatly. I was then permitted to crack other people's nuts for them, but never to have any myself. Now-a-days parents are inclined to go to the other extreme, and appear to be hurt if one does not always share in the enthesinsm with which they fuss over their children, study their whims and fancies, and never think of checking acts of dis- obedience. But the spoilt child is never a loveable one, and although one would not wish to see a return to the harsh methods of Ruskin's time, still the over-petting and over-indulgence of children is a mistake, from which they are sure to suffer in character in after years. Sir Henry and Lady Carnpbell-Bannerman's French Tastes.—The late Sir Henry and Lady Campbell- Bannerman had both a singular affection for things French and Belmont Castle, their Scottish home in Perthshire, beers testimony in many ways to their tastes in this direction. The old building was destroyed by fire in 1884, and Sir Henry' who came into possession of the estate, rebuilt the Castle, which is a fine mansion, standing in beautiful, but not very extensive grounds. The decoration of the rooms was mostly done by French artists, and some of the apartments are modelled after the style of the Palace of Versailles, and also contain, a great many French works of art. Sir Henry and Lady Campbell-Bannerman both spoke French fluently and I frequently conversed in that language in private life, whilst Lady Campbell-Bannerman bad a great affection for French bull dogs, which became the favourite pets at Belmont Castle. Close by is the village of Mergle, which boasts great antiquity, and here in the church- yard, Sir Henry was this week laid to rest beside his wife, amidst the innumerable manifestations of public sympathy and regard. ` Marry in May and you'll rue the Day."—There are many old proverbs about the evil consequences of marrying in May, and of all ancient superstitions none survives more strongly than the objection to be wedded in the " unlucky month." a his is seen at once in the falling off in the number of marriages announced in the newspapers, the list in the "'times " being usually only about one-fourth, or one-fifth as long in May, as in June. That this prejudice is an absurdity most people are prepared to admit, but it has come down to us from very ancient times. The church in its early days recognised many of .the old pagan customs, and until medireval times marriages were forbidden between certain festivals. Some of the old church registers put these roles into rhyme : Light days from Easter says you may ; Rogation bids thee to contain, But Trinity sets thee free again. It would appear that what wria merely custom, became in time superstition, and Ours perpetuated a " close season "for weddings during May. Summer Fabrics.—Printed cottons are in the greatest profusion in shopland, the hest closely resembling in pattern our great-great grandmother's dimity chair coverings and casement curtains. Made up in quaint, picturesque styles, and worn with suitable headgear, these fabrics should score a success, but the girl who builds her own gowns, or can only afford the cheap— and often uncompetent dressmaker—would do better to go back to the plain, uncrushable linens for useful morning gowns this coming summer. Thee there are the batieties and organdies, with woven borders, which also need great skill in making up successfully. The underdress too for these materials is so important, and this means that although the batistss and organdies themselves may he inexpensive, the bill mounts up con- siderably 'ere the dress is complete. The Yoke.—The white, cream, or ecru openwork yoke will be a feature of all summer blouses, a state of affairs likely to cause much rejoicing, as few ideas in the modiste world are so becoming. Then it affords con- siderable scope to the home worker, who can inexpen- sively work up her own yokes and at the same time appear exclusive. Short lengths of lace, embroideries, edge, which may be made either of the material an ribbons, obtained for a mere trifle at the sales, can or of white linen or pique. The tunic fastens often be made up into really beautiful yokes by skilful down the front in double-breasted style, and is fingers. Transparent effects being no loneer deemed ornamented with six large buttons, the buttons correct for day wear these yokes will be lined either being flanked on either side by small, machine- with culpe-de-chine or silk muslin, stitched box-pleats. At the long waist-line the Care of the Hands.—Whetber it is due to our ever- tunic is held in by a neat little belt of the mate- growing love of outdoor sport, or because we take less rial, machine-stitched round the edges, which fas care of them than forrnerly,the fact remains that beauti tens in front. Neat little sleeves of very ml hands are comparatively rarely seen nowadays. No moderate size, set into two or three tucks at the doubt it is vary difficult to enjoy outdoor games and wrist, complete this pretty little model. yet keep one's hands nice, still we ought to do our best. THE COAT AND SKIRT. • A capital plan is to always hare upon the washstand, a The severe coat and skirt is very much a thing cut lemon, and after washing the bands a little of the of the past, except for sporting purposes. says juice should be squeezed over them, well rubbed in, and the queen. The coats of to-day ere indeed van- allowed to dry. The hands should then be rinsed in ous. Some are cut with a short Basque in front, cleaned water. At night, again after well washing the ending in a tassel, and a long one at the back,' hands in warm water and soap, an application of equal slit up the side to half the depth, with a flap parts of rosewater, lemon juice, and glycerine should be pocket at the back, tassels falling from the elbow used, and loose gloves, out of which the palms have sleeves. Quito different is the long-skirted coat been cut, drawn on. cut up to the hips at the side and laced and so Photography Without a Camera.—There are few more arc the sleeves on the out.,-1 +l, Ti • -1-e ...42 arm. -els popular and entertaining hobbies than amateur garment flies open in the front, with collar and revers of velvet. It photography. The facilities for following it have been is a style in which b