      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>TRE SWEETLY SIMPLE STYLE.</head>
        <p>TRE SWEETLY SIMPLE STYLE.</p>
        <p>If, as the fashion prophets predict, muslin iS be fashionable this summer for gowns, hats, and even outdoor jackets, then must we certainly Eel') a change come over tho girl of the period. To be effective in this dainty and essentially feminine material, with which are associated rosebuds and blue ribbons and those sort of things, wearers must be sweetly simple, a little helpless, anything and everything, in short, but big and independent and "all there." But we shall manage it, declares "Miranda" in the Lady's Pictorial. The English girl is equal to anything that is asked of her by Madame la Mode, and if sweet simplicity is to be her role this season w• shall find her filling it to perfection. And if she does she will effect a welcome change.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LACE HATS TO BE WORN.</head>
        <p>LACE HATS TO BE WORN.</p>
        <p>Literally hundreds of yards of fine white Valenciennes lace, gathered up into little flounces and frills innumerable, have been used by the milliners in the decoration of some of the daintiest and prettiest of the new spring hats, and there seems every prospect that during the summer hats which are made entirely of lace will enjoy a most remarkable vogue. In size they will be nowise behind the winter season, for they will be immense, and the crown will be still higher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>NATTY LITTLE TIINIC</head>
        <p>A NATTY LITTLE TIINIC</p>
        <p>Very pretty as well as most useful and practi- cal is the natty little tunic pictured in our illus- tration. It might be carried out with great suc- cess in brown, navy, or green serge, or, if liked, in a pretty indistinct tweed. In place of the more usual sailor collar the neck is finished by a narrow roll collar, machine-stitched at the</p>
