        <p>Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR INPSIEGIEZTON. 'NI</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>yes -I g carriage, and the train was -slowing down before entering a big, bustling station, and few minutes later they were being driven through busy streets, on their way to a hotel and breakfast. She laughed gaily when, by-and-bye, she seated herself at the head of the table to pour out Percy's coffee. It was all so new and delightful. She felt already some of the importance and dignity of a young matron. "I wonder what poor dear uncle is thinking of me now?" she said, as she sipped her coffee. "I suppose he is saying—well—very naughty things, and sending people in all direc- tions to look for me." Percy laughed, too, but</p>
        <p>less whole-heartedly. He knew something of "poor dear uncle's" disposition, and recognised Jessica's description as not overdrawn. "It doesn't seem a bit like a wedding," she said plaintively, as they walked towards the registry office. "It is great fun, and awfully exciting, and—and you are a dear boy, and I am awfully fond of you, but—it--it must be nice to have a real wedding, and heaps of new clothes and presents and bridesmaids and things." "Jessica, you aren't regrettins• ? " Percy said, tenderly, As he drew her little hand through his arm, and looked down at her with sadly reproach- ful eyes. She never could resist that look, and she pressed his arm loving!y. "Not a bit, you dear old thing, only—oh, darling, look at those cakes, real wedding-cakes. We must have one, Percy," and she drew up before firs shop, and gazed in at the window. 'Do get me a cake, dear. It will be such fun to send pieces to all the girls, and to uncle." "I think I wouldn't send any to your uncle," he advised, following his excited bride into the shop. Jessica, having made her choice, wandered round, looking at the dainty things in which her soul delighted. The girl who served her took the bill to Percy. "One guinea, please, sir' Percy wheeled around quickly, his face flushed, his manner embarrassed. "Darling, have you any change ?" fumbling in his pockets, "I-1 haven't enough with me, could you—until—" "Not change enough? 'said Jessica, in surprise, reluctantly drawing out her purse. "Oh, how stupid. We shall want so much. Yes, I have a guinea," and she took two coins from her purse, and placed in the girl's hand. Percy, still rather upset by the incident, strolled out of the shop, and waited outside, while Jessica stayed to buy some little boxes, and give instructions that all should be sent at once to the Royal Hotel. "I want it to be there when we get back," she was saying, as she came out of the door. But Percy did not hear her. He was engaged in con- versation with a policeman, a very alsorbing con- versation, apparently. "Don't you know your way, dear?" she said laughingly, as she joined him. "I am afraid I must trouble the gentleman to tome with me, madam," said the blue-coated pardian of the law. "I am sorry to upset your plans, but--" "But they can't be upset," cried Jessica, a faint alarm mingling with her surprise., "We ought to be at the--"</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, May 2, 1908</p>
