        <p>"I cannot go home," she cried, wildly. Her strength was gone and her nerves were shattered by all she had gone through, and the prospect which lay before her. A girl alone, in a strange place, with s-ircely any money or clothes, no friends, no hope. "I can't go back. What would they say? How could I face them? I must do something to keep myself till you are free, and then--" "It is a pity to waste so much time," he inter- posed, cruelly. "Percy!" Her voice rose in a shrill cry of dismay. "Oh, you—you don't know what you are saying—you can't. Darling, you love me still ? " . .</p>
        <p>. _ Her voice broke into a tender, almost childish pleading. It, and her pitiful question, touched the man, touched him more than he was willing to be touched, and he turned away impatiently. He was saved from answering by the gaoler's voice announcing time was up. "Percy, she implored. 1 "Of—of course,' he answered, impatiently. "I , am thinking of what is best for you. I can't ask you to wait." The last Words were forced from him by the look 1 on her face. But she turned away without another sound. Dr. Hepburn looked grave. The problem was a ! difficult one to solve. Here was a girl, almost a child, beautiful, friendless, practically thrown on ' his hands. "For the time, at any rate," he said, gravely, "I must take you to my home—that is, if you will allow me. You see, you cannot stay here: it is against. regulations. My carriage is outside. Will you let me take you home to my•sister ? " Miss Millicent Hepburn, sitting waiting by the fire in their comfortable drawing-room, glanced up continually at the clock on the mantelpiece. "Oh, dear," she sighed, "how late Paul is. Dinner will be spoilt again, and that will be the third time this week." Miss Hepburn was a plump, placid little body, reminding one of a gentle dove-coloured duck. Even her conviction with regard to the dinner did not cause her to look more than placidly perturbed. But the sound of wheels dashing up and stopping outside the house made her start to her feet, and brought a look of determination to her face. "If that is another patient, I will speak myself, and say Paul cannot go out again to-nigh. What is the use of his overworking as he does? He'll kill , himself, and then the boy will be left without any parents at all." Strong in her resolution, she stepped out into the ball. She had not heard a knock or ring, hut she was determined to be ready. To her astonish- ment, Paul himself opened the door. "Ah, are you there? " h 3 said, in a tone of relief, and taking her by the arm, drew her into the nearest room, and poured his tale into her astonished ears. "Of course, it was the only thing you could do," she said, in a tone that made Paul bless her for the best of women. "Now, do get her in as quickly as possible," and she rang the'bell. "A friend has arrived unexpectedly," she said to the maid. "Tell Ann to light the fire and gas in the spare room at once." And when poor, weary, miserable Jessica entered . - of Aced</p>
        <p>The porter's voice grew indistinct, his voice seemed to die farther and farther away in the distance; a cold, heavy blackness settled down on her brain, her heart beat wildly, then seemed to stop ; her eyelids closed heavily, she tried to raise them, but all was black and cold. _1 mighty rushing sounded in her ears ; surely this was death, this cold numbness, this falling, falling, as into eternity. She stretched out her arms, and groped wildly—then all was peace. When at last, with a long, sobbing sigh, life returned to her, and sh€l opened her eyes, she found another pair of eyes looking anxiously into</p>
        <p>heis. "Percy," she said, involuntarily shrinking away. "No, Paul," said a voice. She thought of that other night when the same kind eyes were watching her, the same voice was encouraging her to be brave. With an effort, she collected her senses, and the truth came back to her. "Is he really gone?" she asked. "Really. Poor child, lam afraid you have a great trouble to face again." "Really gone?" she repeated, not heeding the rest of his sentence, and she looked up at him with eyes full of unmistakable relief. "Oh, thank God, thank God ! " and unable any longer to control .herself, she sobbed hysterically from pure joy and relief. Paul Hepburn dropped heavily into a chair beside the old sofa. "Do you—Jessica, do you mean that? For Heaven's sake, child, don't keep ins in suspense! Are you really glad he is gone? "Glad! she cried, and there was no need to say more. "The thought of—of this day has nearly killed me, and—there seemed no escape." "And now you need not leave us," he said, rather irrelevantly, and his voice trembled. Her eyes fell, and she coloured nervously as she tried to raise herself from where she was lying. Oh—l—oh, I must go," she stammered. "Jessica, listen. You must not go; you must stay.' I want you, and David wants you. My darling," bursting into sudden passion, 'you must not leave me. If you can find it in your heart to stay, for mercy's sake, do so. Can you, dear? Can you?" "I —I think I could not have found it in my heart to go," she said, softly, with a deep sigh of ineffable relief.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>LADY COLEBROOKE'S HOBBIES.</p>
        <p>Like the Duchesses of Rutland and Suther- land, Lady Colebrooke is famous alike, says the ratio-, for her beauty and her artistic talent and accomplishments. Her skill as a political hostess is well known, but as an adept at various arts and crafts she has somewhat hidden her light under a bushel. At her Scottish home at Abing- ton in Lanarkshire Lady Colebrook° has had a perfectly - appointed carpenter's shop fitted up wherein to indulge ai her hobby for wood-carv- ing. Not only has she turned out some ex- tremely clever pieces of work herself, but she Las taught the village girls how to carve pretty articles for their homes. Most of her intimate friends possess some specimen of her handiwork in carved wood. Lady Colebrooke is also a clever sculptor and has exhibited her work both in marble and plaster. Her husband shares with her a keen perception and love of art and things • beautiful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>VERY SMART HAT.</head>
        <p>A VERY SMART HAT.</p>
        <p>Pictured in the accompanying sketch is a par- ticularly fascinating and smart little hat, just the thing for regular wear during the early sum- mer. The hat is quite small, almost small enough, indeed, to be called a toque, but has a decided brim, which, I think, entitles it to be classed amongst the hats. A fine and very supple straw of a pretty Leghorn shade is used</p>
