      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>•'W/hat are your most expensive habits?" e"Tholte my wife wears."</p>
        <p>Elsie: "Laura's health seems greatly im- proved." Ads: "Yes; she has heard that her doctor is engaged."</p>
        <p>Seeds: "You say he left no money!" Bags: "No. You see, he lost his health getting wealthy, and then lost his wealth try- ing to get healthy."</p>
        <p>Grubb: "I've lis.(l an offer to go to worx for a London wholesale house. What would you do if yoh were in my shoes?" Friend (after a careful inspection) : "I think I should black 'em."</p>
        <p>Jenkins: "I've tried at least twenty kinds Qt health foods, but none of them has done me any good." Beatty: "What are you troubled with?" Jenkins: "An appetite.'</p>
        <p>Mr. Cunning: "Did you spend all that money I gave you this morning?" Mrs. Cun- ning: "Yes, my dear. If I'd spent only part of it I would have had to wait at the bargain counter for my change, and then I wouldn't have been home in time for dinner."</p>
        <p>Standard IV. was lately being, put through its paces in General Knowledge. "What do the letters B.C. with a number stand for?" Bright Youth: "Before Christ." "Right. Now, what do the letters B.A. stand for?" Another Bright Youth (promptly) : "Before Adam."</p>
        <p>An applicant for the post of mistress in a country school was asked: "What is your position with regard to the whipping of chil- dren ?" She replied: My usual position is on a chair with the child held firmly across my knees, face downwards !"</p>
        <p>"When you get your groceries to-day," said the butcher to his wife, "don't so to that little grocer next door to my shop.'w "Why not?" she demanded. "Because he sent in yesterday and borrowed an old pair of scales.</p>
        <p>"Well," said the bridegroom-to-be, "I sup- pose you'll be sorry, Willie, when the time comes for your sister's wedding." "Not much !" replied the small brother. "It will gimme an excuse to throw pa's slippers away."</p>
        <p>Customer (hesitatingly) : "I suppose—er —you have some—er—suitable books for a man—er—about to be married?" Book- seller: "Certainly, sir. Here, John, show this gentleman some of our account books— largest size."</p>
        <p>Very Fat Gentleman (to street boyj: "Sonny, can you tell me the quickest way I can get to the station?" Very Naughty Boy (after looking him up and down carefully): "I should say you'd better lay down an' roll over 'bout twice."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Whoopler : "You tell me, Herr Vo- gleschnitzel, that my daughter can never be- come a singer! Is there no hope for her?" Herr Vogleschnitzel : "Veil, madam, you mighclt put her on a diet of canary-seed al- retty, undt see vat dot viii do mit her."</p>
        <p>Lady (in registry office) : "As there are only my husband and myself in the family, I think you ought to be willing to come for less than you ask. There are only two persons to cook for." Domestic: "But, mum, when I'm wid you there 'ud be three."</p>
        <p>Sensitive Golfer (who has foozled) : "Did you laugh at me, boy?" Caddie: "No, sir; I was laughin' at anither man." Sensitive Golfer: "And what's funny about him?" Caddie: "He plays gawf awfa' like you sir !"</p>
        <p>Elocutionist (beginning to recite Longer- low's famous poem) : "Listen, my children, and you shall hear Of the midnight ride of Paul Revere—" Impatient Auditor • "What make of bicycle did he use?"</p>
        <p>"Well," said the groom, with a sigh of re- lief. when the ceremony was concluded, "I'm glad that trouble's over." "Over!" re- marked his married friend, "Why, it's only just begun."</p>
        <p>Teacher: "What is the meaning of the word 'excavate'!" Small Fupil: "It means to hollow out." Teacher: "Correct. Now form a sentence in which the word is pro- perly used." Small Pupil: "Stick a pin in a boy and he will excavate." A fine specimen of the Taurus hibernicus made its appearance in Bow-street police-court recently. 'You ducked your head,' said the magistrate to Patrick Lane, who charged Jeseph Kavanagh with shooting at him in the Strand. "Faith, and I did, your worship. It's better to be a coward for five minutes than to be dead' all your lifetime."</p>
        <p>French Maid (to inquiring friend): "Oui, madame is ill, but ze doctor hut pronounce it something very trifling, very small." Friend: "Oh, I am so relieved, for I was really anxious about her. What does the doctor. say the trouble is.Z" French Maid: "Let me recall. It was something very leetle. Oh! I have it now. Ze doctor says zat madame has ze small- pox."</p>
        <p>-7 A Scotch Minister and his servant, who were coming home from a wedding, began to consider the state into which their potations at the wedding feast has left them. "Sandy," said the minister, "just stop a minute here till I go ahead'. Maybe I don't walk very steady, and the good wife might remark something not just right." He walked ahead of the servant for a short distance, and then asked: "How is it? Am I walking straight?" "Oh, ay," answered Sandy, thickly, "ye're a' recbt—but who's that who's with you?"</p>
        <p>The great opera singer, Mine. Grisi, who was married to Signor Mario, the tenor. was singing in St. Petersburg. The emperor, the father of the present one, gave Mme. Grisi permi.sion to walk in his private park. One morning the em- peror met Mme. Grisi, accompanied by two little girls. The emperor saluted her, and said: "Are these two Grisettes?" " No, your majesty," replied Mme. Grisi, " they are two Marionettes."</p>
        <p>A fire occurred on premises rented by Jews in London. The circumstances were somewhat suspicious, and an agent of the insurance com- pany was sent to investigate. He interviewed the senior partner, and also the junior partner, as to the probable cause of the fire.. This was</p>
        <p>his repori : " I find that the senior partner thinks it was caused by an arc light on the second floor; the junior partner thintre it was caused by an incandescent light on the first floor —but my opinion is that it was caused by an Israelite in the basement !" Bishop Joseph Wilmer, of Louisiana, and Bishop Richard Wilmer, of Alabama, are cousins and intimate friends. In their travels in Italy one summer, the Bishop of Alabama was pointing out with enthusiasm certain archi- tectural beauties. The Bishop of Louisiana was bored. He said : " It's all very fine, Cousin Dick, but, nevertheless, a rich field, fragrant with the odour of new mown hay, would please me better." And the Bishop of 'Alabama re- plied : "Cousin Joe, there's not a donkey in all Italy that would not agree with you." •</p>
        <p>The manager of the Doemup laundry had ad- vertised for a man. Early next morning, a mild- eyed young char appeared, and referred to the advertiseent. You think you can fill the bill, do you?" asked the proprietor. " I dunno, boss. I've druv a laundry waggon; but I never druv one o' them other things.' "What other things?" And the applicant handed him a clip- ping of the ad., which read: " Wanted—A man to drive laundry waggon and solicit." A college president, noted for the extreme correctness of his language, and his closeness in money matters, once bought a pair of ready- made trousers and had them sent home. The bill rend: " To J. A. Thompsott, debtor, one pair pants, five dollars." The pedagogue called on Mr. Thompson and said " pants' was incor- rect: it shoula be "trousers." Said the clothier: " I've been in the clothing business twenty years and I've always sold two grades of goods, pants and trousers. Trousers are everything over five dollars in price; pants everything hve dollars and under. It's pants I sold you, Ind it's pants you'll pay furq"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Annual Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>The annral sale of work in connection with this Church was held at the Conservative Club, Sankey- street, on Monday afternoon when there was a very large attendance. The sale us opened by M rs. Legh, of High Legh Hall, who was accompanied by Mr. R. H. More. Amongst those present were the Mayor and Meyoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst), the Rev. W. and Mrs. Bracecamp, the Rev. E. E. and Mrs. Peters„ the Rev. J. H. Bankes, the Rev. H. V. and Mrs. Pigot„ Mr. Chas. White, and Messrs. J. H. Smethurst and John Rigby, Vicar's and people's warden respectively. THE STALL-HOLDERS. The following is a list of the stall-holders : Working Party Stall.—Mrs. Lord, Mrs. Gibson Smith,. Miss D. Reid, and Mrs. Worrell. Misses Geddes's Stall.—Misses Geddes, Miss H. Burge,s, and Miss V'estwell. Vicarage Stall.—Mrs. Bracecamp, Mrs. Lindley, Mrs.. Locker Mrs. Jolley, Mrs. Smith, and Mrs. Collin. Sweet Stall.--Miss D. Bracecamp and Miss M. Bennett._</p>
        <p>Cl-;C-ii=-Stall.—Mrs. Wilkes and Mrs. Cox. ITBible Class Stall.—Mrs. Binks and Miss Picton. Day School Stall.—Misses Finney and Miss Fowler. Flower Stall —Mrs. Rigby, Mrs. Ayres, and Miss M. Greenough.</p>
        <p>RefresSment Stall.—Mrs. Beesley, Mrs. Bunch, Mrs. Powis, Mrs. White, Miss Houghton, Misses Smethurst, Miss Gittens, Mrs. Wainwright, Miss Fisher and Miss Mac Neil.</p>
        <p>The VICAR (the Rev. W. Bmcecamp) said it was a natter of delight to them to and so many friends from outside parishes helping them on that interesting occasion. They were obliged to appeal to the public every year for financial assistance because their endow- ment fund was very small, and they were only sure of getting £llB ; everything else in connection with the church had to be found. It was, therefore, most encouraging to find that they had such a great band of workers who did their utmost from year to year to raise the amount required for parochial purposes (Hear, hear, and applause.) He hoped that all present would do their best to make the sale a greater success than ever. They were greatly indebted to Mrs. Legh for her presence. It was exceedingly kind of her to come from High Legh. She was well known in the neighbourhood and her work was much appreciated. Mrs. LEGH, who WRS received with applause, said she had taken a great deal of interest in Warrington since she came here with General Sir Redvers Buller. (Applause.) On that occasion, although she was not the star " herself, she was next to the star, and she had a very pleasant drive through the principal streets of the town and was pleased to find that that great and noble hero-.- (applause)—was so much appreciated. The General had a most enthusiastic reception, and no doubt all present would be sorry to -iear that since his visit to Warrington he had been seriously ill. She was glad to find however, that the latest accounts of him were much more satisfactory. (Applause.) She had much pleasure in declaring the sale of work open. (Applause.) Mr. J. R.SMETHURST,iu proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs.Legh, alluded to "the noble band of lady workers" in the parish which he said was the most noble any , parish could possess. Mr. RIGBY seconded.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in supporting, said the endowment fund had grown very wonderfully from £BO a year to over £l2O, and he did not care how soon it reached £320, so that they could declare all the seats free. The resolution was thed put and carried unanimously. The total proceeds amounted to £316 15s. 6d. which. is an increase of £26 on last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>STARTLED into St. Vitus' Dance.</head>
        <p>STARTLED into St. Vitus' Dance.</p>
        <p>This Nervous Girl suffered Tortures until her Nerves were strengthened by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>AND THOUSANDS SAY SO. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills enrich weak blood and so feed the exhausted nerves; they are invaluable for both sexes, and have cured Anwmia, Indigestion, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Neuralgia, Locomotor Ataxy, Paralysis, Nervous Disorders, and Ladies' Ailments. Only the genuine pills cure, those always bearing the full name (seven words), Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Obtainable of all dealers, or direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, EC., post free, 2s. Od. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes. Avoid all substitutes.</p>
        <p>The effect of a shock ,•tcte, upon a nervous system already weakened may AA „tip develop with most evil results ; this is trafed clearly in the account given by a young _ woman residing near Wrexham. The victim 44r" of a fright, St. Vitus' 4.01•••• )te. Dance developed to such a degree that her life was despaired of; how her nervous system was \ • strongly fortified by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills and a cure effected is •' here reprinted in her own words : " Two years ago," said Miss Emil, .r...iAms, MISS EMILY WI,LLIAMq, of cured of St. Vitus' Dance Brynterion,Brynissa-road, by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.Drynteg,Wrexham"While I was in the garden a hen flew at me, the attack being so sudden that I was quite startled and frightened. After going indoors my limbs shook violently and I fell into hysterics. "From that time onwards I felt so nervous and, excitable that every noise made me jump and tremble. My hands were too shaky for me to convey food to my mouth, and I could not remain still for more than a few seconds. Then fits of 'trembling came over me so violent that a doctor ordered me to bed; a few days later such convulsive movements of St. Vitus' Dand seized my limbs that I had to be strapped down. The jerkings in my limbs continued for some months and I became almost as helpless as a baby. I could take little solid food, for my stomach was too weak to digest it and I was so nervous that I could not bear to be left alone. " Apart from the awful pains that ricked my head and body I was Iv retchedly weak ; try as I would to keep my jerking limbs still I could not ; violent twitchings ran all over my body, my head shook and the muscles of my face would contract causing hideous grimaces. " One day a lady called and drew my mother's- attention to a case like mine that had been cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. She persuaded us to try them. I began to take these pills, and in time the effect was almost magical. Not at once, but steadily, an improvement in my health was noticed. I could pick up a cup without dropping it, and after three or four boxes my appotite improved, and I was able to enjoy light meals. As my cure progressed, I became aware of a gradual strengthening of my whole body, and we were all overjoyed to find that my convulsive movements were disappearing. So ray health improved until Dr. Williams' Pink Pills had positively cured me of St.Vitus' Dance. lam now nineteen, and have never had a relapse."</p>
