      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>Result Grazed</p>
        <p>Cried with Cruel, Torturing Pa —Flesh Rotted Away to the B(</p>
        <p>MbR. Joseph J. Loveday, of 40, Cradock Ro.• . Saltley, Birmingham, has told a "Birming- ham Echo" reporter how Zam-Buk, by curing him of an ulcerated leg, averted the necessity of amputation, and ended 16 years' terrible suffering. "I am now just turned 40 years of age," he told the reporter. "After leaving the Coldstream Guard. I started to work at the Saltley Gas Works. One day I knocked the skin off my shin, but at the time did not pay much attention to it, thinking it would soon get better. I was wearing coloured socks the time, and some of the dye must have got into the sore, for a few days later my leg began to fester and pain me very much, the sore and started to spread. I got some ordinary ointment, but the wound got worse. "At last I was compelled to consult a doctor. I was under his care for many weeks, and then he aa'vissi me to go to the hospital and have my le( taken of I could not bring myself to agree to losing my kg, although the wound by this time had spread to about six inches deep and three inches wide, and the pain it gave me was excruciating. It was fearfully painful even to stand upright. Then the wound in the leg healed up a little, but broke out in the ankle. I was now obliged to stay away from work, and call another doctor. He treated me in vain and</p>
