      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Warrington Workman's Compensation Award.</head>
        <p>Warrington Workman's Compensation Award.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiiess Will, K.C., Mr. Stewart Brown, barrister, Liverpool (instructed by Mr. H. C. G. Day, of Liverpool) applied on behalf of Messrs. Rylands Bros. Ltd., wire manufacturers, Church-street, for a review of an order under the Workmen's Compensation Act with regard to Enoch Sankey, 9, Wellington-street, who sustained injury whilst in their employ. Mr. Cuthbert Smitn, barrister, Liverpool (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) appeslad for Selksy. Mr. Brown stated that Sankey was t7rmerly employed as a wire labourer at the applicant's works. His employment consisted of tying up bundles of wire, and whilst doing so on July 12th of last year he received an injury to his right eye, the sight of which had been destroyed. An agreement was come tJ, under which Sankey was to receive 1 Is. sd. a week es compensation. . Sankey WB9 ea mined by D. Fox on October 28th who expressed the opinion that he WB9 fit to rumple his employment, and on January 7th he was e•ram' ied by Dr. Livesey, of Liverpool, who gave a similar expi °salon of opinion. Subsequently Mr. Steel wrote to the effect that Sanhey's doctor was of opinion that he was not able to resume his old work.</p>
        <p>His Honour said he took it that the only question to be decided was as to wheti)er Sankey was able to resume his old woi k. _</p>
        <p>IMr. Brown said that was practically the question that bad to he dc 3ided, and lie admitted that Sankey had not Ibeen offered other work, but he had been requestc I to . Isunie his old employment. His Floncur alluded to a recent decision in the Court of Appal, which as be read it was to the effect Oat it was not enough for a County Court Judge to, my 1 whether a man was unable to go back to his old work, ' but the Judge must go on to decide whether the man I could do other work and what was the yOue of that I work.</p>
        <p>Mc.Will tam Webb,mrtagrr of the netting department at Messrs.Rylands Bros., said Sanl ey was employed in the warehouse and had nothing to do with machinery. Lie firm turned out thousanas of bundles of wire netting weekly. He had never known of a case of injury to the eye happening in the warehouse before the present case. He saw no reason why Sant.,.ey should not do his work perfectly well with one eye. He actually offered to retro a. to work two or thee months ago eld he was as fit to work now es ever be was.' Two men wbo were I partially blind in one eye were employed by the firm at 1 wire wr no. ing a-id two youths who were positively blind in one eve wee wot king at ma lhinerv. Dr. E. J. Fox said that when he saw Sankey in October he was fit to work. WiLniss had insnentsd the place wile) e the at3ident occuived and had seen the work done. SaoLev was able to do the work. " That is not an opinion" said the dodo,: " it is a fac*...' He knew of one case of a ma^. blind in one eye, work- ing at wire weaving and of rioliber whe.e a man worked in a wire warehouse at work similar to Sankey's. Dr. Livcsey, of Liverpool, said he examined Srikey in Janua,v and agreed that be wrs able to follow his employment. He, howevet, ought to pro' )ct his eye with wire goggles and he was not 95 elficient to do b's work rs he was berore.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cuthbert Smith submitted it bad lacln proved that the work at which Sookev V4i.4 employed wee i dangerous sod he would in the iisk of losing his oLber eye. Before his Honour could reduce the award he ' must be satiski that some sui:able occupation was offered to the man but it 1-131'not b. 3n shwa a that the man was able with safety to himself to do the work suggested. Enoch Sankey was then called and raid he could not do the work with safety to himself. He had previously 1 3ea struck in the face with we. He denied having offered to return to tie same work, but admitted rsitiug Mr. Webb if he would find him some.h:ng to do, Dr. Ferguson said he examined Sa-3key in Januaif and Mach. The men might t 3 able to do the work but the risk would ::;3 so g.sat that it would not be right to r‘dt him to do it. Wire goggles would inter- fere slill more with his vision.</p>
        <p>E3s Honour said he W 139 satisfied tbst the sp.'nging of toe wire forward was apt to come from the .;ght side oa which the man was blind, and he would itie,efore Ina the risk of being wounded. He was not fit to do his old work, and PI award must be made in Sankey's favour with costs. The c--..se had been very fairly con- ducted on behalf of Messrs. Rylands Bros,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>At the Socialist Hall on Monday the members of the Earlestown Glee Society (Prize Chorus) presented their conductor, Mr. Hughes, with a silver mounted ebony baton, suitably inscribed, and their accompe list, Miss D. Pro flit, with an ebony fitted lady's companion ease, with silver initials, as a small token of esteem, for services rendered to the choir. Mr. Paton made the presentation to Mr. goalies, and Mrs. Wright presented the case to Miss PrOlfit, which came as a surprise to both parties, which they suitably acknowledged. The rest of the evening was spent in a social marmer.</p>
        <p>KILLED BY COMPLICATED NEW PIT[ An inquest on the body of Sergeant Tenlesor !Lad Scots Grards, who woe shot on Ash licaaes on Wednes- day as the result of an ac ident, was held on laulaNy at Aldershot Hospital.—An official, who gave evidence, syid the new magazine rifle was most compl:c-dcd, cad difacolt for a recruit to leala to hsre.e.</p>
