      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Licensing Bill</head>
        <p>The Licensing Bill</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN SUPPORT.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY MEETING AT BOLD-STREET CHURCH. Sti. ing Addrbsses and Unanimous</p>
        <p>Support.</p>
        <p>At the close of the usual evening service at Bold- strr t Wc leyan Cnurch on Sunday a unit(o meeting of -leyrn Methodists of the Warrington Circuit was held to support the Government Licensing Bill. There WE'S a very large attendance, the Rev. Sidney Pitt (supetintendent minister) presiding. Accompanying him on the rostrum were the Revs. W. Mellor, T. Miller and C. Harvey Cook, Messrs. C. E. Parker, E. J. Guest, and J. Upson. The CHA.amAN commenced by reading a circular from the President of the Conference stating that a meeting of the Committee of Privileges and the Temperance Com- mittee of the denom:nation had been held and called upon Wesleyan Methodists throughout the country to give the measure their utmost support and to hold meetings and pass resolutions in favour of it The Temperance Committee of the Liverpool District also , asked them to hold a meeting in support of the Bill in every chapel. The Cenference itself in its las minutes resolved : " ne Conference reaffirms its condemnation of the Licensing Act of 1904 and urges that no effort shall be spared to secure at the trliest possible moment the reversal of the most mischievous provisiors of that Act by the restoration of the full powers of the' local licersing authority and by fixing a time limit after which all compensation shall cr se." Addressing his fellow Wesleyan Methodists, continued Mr. Pitt,with a full sense of the responsibility of the position he had taken, he asked them: What would they think of themselves—what would others think of them--if, after those utterances on the part of their highest Conuexional authority, they allowed themselves to be silent in the crisis at which this nation had arrived ? It was well known that they had net been accustomed to do that sort of thing—they had never done it before, not even over the Education Bill, closely as that measure affects 1 their denominational position—and he hoped they might never have to take such a step again. But he i asked : Could they, with any self-respect, allow them- I salves to be silent when at last a Government of this land had been found with sufficient courage to take the risk and to do the very things which they as a denomina- tion had been urging the Government to do? This was not merely a matter of temperance or of party politics, for, as their revered ex-President, Dr. Pope, pointed out early in the controversy, it was their question before it was a Government or 9 political questiomand were they to drop it because the Government had come to the very line which they as a denomination had adopted? It was not merely a question of social reform There was a vast, far-reaching, unscrupulous, g eedy monopoly, and tke question was whether that monopoly was going to govein the country? It wry a national question in which as good citizens—and vs Wesleyan Methodists they had always claimed to be good citizens —they were involved. Messrs. Rowntree and .‘.-Jherwell some years ago made a calculation by which they showed that in the position of parties both in the constituencies, supposing that evety on-licensed house could influence two votes—and that was a very small estimate—the electoral power of the public-houses we, such that they could turn a minority of 150 in the House of Commons into a majority of 150. lnat wee to say, they were very near indeed to governing the country. " I have sevetal tin:lcl in this pulpit" con- tinued Mr. Pitt, "made reference to the opinions of Mr. R. J. Campbell. lam glad for once to he able to quo' him in a favourable sense. He has recently said the question is whether or not we are to get the hand of the drink monopoly away from the throat of the Brit ;sh nation. That is what it has come to. I do not know a place —I don't know whether you know one —wher the hand of the drink interest is tighter and harder on the throat of the people than it is in Warrington—(hear, heat,)—so that there is probably no place, at arty rate there are very few places in the coantry where it is more necessary to try this issue as to where the Government of the country is to lie. Wreat has been</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>HAPPENING DURING- IHE PAST FEW DAYS</head>
        <p>HAPPENING DURING- IHE PAST FEW DAYS</p>
        <p>hea proved up to the hilt what Messrs. Rowntree end Sherwell have shown. It has proved up to the hilt the vest, unscrupulous electoral power which is wielded by the drink interest—(hear, hear)—and in the presence of Lois threatening power, are we to be silent? (" No.") Every public-houte is a committee room. Every publican is a political agent—sadly against the will in some cases. (Hear, hear.) P only the publicr is knew and if only they de-e speah they would work with all their might for this Bill. We are sE raking w.th manner of sympathy of the public .ns. Iney s ,nd to ga'n by this Bill and if by the mercy of God they could be delivered from their trade they would s' ,nd to gain I more than all. I would remind them of what they know—that the life of the publ'ean and the bar tende str ids at the bottom as an insurable value. I have hi ;n told by an actuarirl authority that they tried to run an insurancs society of their ori,a and to get better ratr.' and they corld not do it. Nobody or very few suffer more than the publicans themselves, but in spite of it all, every publican is a political agent, every bar- n-aid is a canvasser. If out of consideration for the feelings of those whom we love and respect we were to be silent now, then we may efface ourselves or sllow ourselves to be for ever effacsd from any position of inluence in the guidance and government of LW^ land. Would John Wesley be silent ? (" No.") Mr. Pitt (incited from one of John Wesley's 53sermora vvHch are the standards of doctrine of tbe Wesley,' r Connexion, the following statement about thu. who traded in spirits "All who sell them in the r 3mmon way t 3 any that will buy are po"soners general. They murder his Majesty's subjects by wholr 'ale, neither dc s their eye pity nor spare. Ithey drive them to bell like sheep, and what is their gain? Is it not the blood of these men ? \Nilo then would envy their large estatss and sumptuous p-laces ? The cruse of God isin the midst of them. The curse of God cleaves to the 5t0nr1.,..: the timbe , the fir _sauna of them. be curse of God is th,ir garden^ their walks, their groves." Fear them not my breth .3u, concluded Mr. Pit' They will scare you if they can but fear "rem not. I will forewma you Whom ye shall fear. Fear Has that made "rem, and will judge them and call them to accoe 1 Fear Him that made us aud will judge us Bad ea" us ton sonar "Fear Him ye saiva and ye will then have not's' 1g else • to fear." (Applause.) Mc. E. J. G (YOST moved : " That this meating approves of the Licensing Bill now I afore tbe House of Commons as a nr sura of justice long due to the people and as a measure which is not only lest to tie trade but tempers justic) with mercy. Weile thanking the Government in power for coriageously bringing forward this Bill it recognizes the fact that it ,nnot he looked upon as a mi grits of party politics but as the outcome of a feeling of desire .for socie' reform which bag br al gross :ng in all churches and "a all pa..jea for many years. Although ON ineeF,ig realizes that the Bill might be strengthened on the side of temperance -eform, it accepta it and pledges itself to support the Goverament in carrying through this measure by all legitimate means." For a short time, said Mr. Guest, 1,3 wondered whether they were doing unite right to bold a meet'ig of that kndouthoe ncred preeses on that sacred day, but when be coolide-ed that it was a enestion of righteousne-^ versus rarighteousness and that those houses of prayer were built nor, simply to bring in the wreckage of society, to "rescue the perish- ing. care for the dying," but that they were to be 99 beacon lights to warn of danger, as break waters to give shell .r end safety, he realized that Neveation was I ).,ter than care and in belping forward the Licensing Bill they were doing the right ttriag. There wes no question that the drieir trade as it was carried on to-day worked for uarighteotp,erss and esil all Arm.' the line. He did not say that they as Gne-ches bad all the rghieousteas that they ought to have. Thera wrs a good deal of admix- ture of selFshrsss and of vid:ong, it might be. He did not say that the public-home end the trade in strong drink wrs all evil, and that the tendency was all vi...ong. He could quite understand that it was not the wish of the trade that those evils should result but he was su_e that the trade might nsrae 7a those places a great deal tar then it did. (EJc", heae.) We were all more or less selesh, rid I-a &amp;nor Yred r'l people who were in trade tried to make Usti°, -fel, but when " the t.ade" realized the er,;l effe ;15 of the traffic he thought they needed a sr 3cial me..-nre of Divine grac-; to enable them to cai on their businr-s rightly and with the leest possible harm. The Licensing Bill was goieg to help the trade to r ,/ on their business in a tatter 9nd more respectable manner. Wnen it was carefully considered, it was not in any way a measure of to anybody. It was certainly justict to the p3ople, rid he thought it was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>AIISOLUTE JUSTICE TO THE TRADE.</head>
        <p>AIISOLUTE JUSTICE TO THE TRADE.</p>
        <p>_ 'They as Nonconformists ought to re'ly to the Cove, -1- ment on that question. (Hear, hear.) They forced the hands of the Government to a large extent to bring the manure forward and the Govetoment had acted courageously in introducing the Bill. He sometimes wished a Conservative Government had brought it forward. (Laughter.) He was sure if they had don' the Conservatives, or many of them, would have sup poited them—be did not say all—(laughter)—and he was sure the Nonconformists would all have support( the Conservative Government on the question, he was sorry that now they had brought the issue ton bead and they had an opportunity of accomplishing a great reform, some of those who had 1 3en talking about tsmpelence in days gone by were falling out of the ranks. They want* to rally all the forces of refoLn to carry the Bill througb, He was sorry for some who could not join with them on the question. He could realize that there were some who were so socially mixed up with the brewing ititrest end also received financial e;d in support of the objets which they had in view, that they found it very difficult to speak out. They had not the spirit of martyrs. " But we," M. Guest went on "have notl2'ing to fear. We have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SEQUEL TO A MILL ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A MILL ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Warrington Girl's Claim For Compensation.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., Arm Kelly, 65, Foundry-street, brought an application under the Work- men's Compensation Act for compensation for injuries sustained whilst following her employment at Messrs. Armitage and Rigby's, Cockhedge Mille, Mr. Cuthbe.., Smith, barrister (in.-Aruct d by Mr. T. S. Steel) appeared for the applicant and Mr. Adshead Elliott, barrister (instruct2d by Mr. John Taylor of Blackburn), for the respondents..</p>
        <p>Mr. Cuthbert §mith said applicant was engaged as a tenter of a carding ITI 3hine at Cockhedge Mills. The criving strap came off one of the carding machines and wjaile she wrs essisting to replace it her arm WM caught. The girl said her average weekly earnings were 3s. 6d. The only question for the Judge to decide was whether the accident arose out of and in the course of her employment. The girl wr assistant to Barbara Smith who was a card tenter having charge of seven machines. Applic-nt had four machines of her own and when they were not work'ng had to help Smith. Aden a strap came off a !mu c dlr.' a "grinder," if he was anywhere wi,hin read', ought to replace it but on the occasion =n question he was nowhere to be sam and at Smith's request Kelly went to put the strap on. She sac down while she did so and as she wes getting up the strap broke and wrapped itself round her any carsiag a compound fracture of tb2. left elbow and laceration of the air. She was not no capable of working. It might be argued that she had no right to put the st Ap on but he understood that if a strap was not immediately iepla :rid everything started running to waste.</p>
        <p>Applic--at gave eviden a bearing out this statement and in cros-examirntion denied that when she was engaged by the fi she was told she had not on any account to touch r chi-mu or straps. She knew it was her duty when a strap c mae off to report it to the " gr,nder "if he a. a;.l the room. Tbe a was usually one" grinder " e ,rdf oom. She denied having been wa a. 1. -vii tire_ •wi en found at the back of me ;hi ti-it Ino ight to ko there. If the " geinder w, • not in room all the girls replaced the saps themselves. Mr. Elliott said applic it's duties were most clearly defined when she was engaged rad the overlooker gave her strict irsi, actions that on no account must she touch the st al •or m inery. When a strap came off it as her duty to tell the Josh Lead, en crier' spoke to engr ling applicant and I -lung her not to touch t ay dangerous part of a machine or handle a "mamas Bot ry, g :nder rid stripper in the employ of the if iv said it was eiL'ler his or another man's duty to put st•aps on. He bad I'll o -ion to warn Kelly for going behind rracbitr - where she had no right to be. Charles Sagar, al.o a so ipper and grinder, gave similar evidence, and said he had caught Kelly putting a belt on and told her he would boa her r •.rs if he cmght her doing so again. M.. Pone. barrister, addressed his Honour for the re- 'wader:its, M.,. Elliott having had to leave to catch a train, and contended that the girl wts incr• •ding her ditty in replacing tbe step. and was therefore respon- sible for what occurred. Thee wra no eviden e tint the rule bad bran systematically disregarded. His Honour said he wr opinion that the accident arose out of the duties of the applic mt. There was no notice in the mill to the effect that the girls were not to touch machine. y, rid on baba Icing the evidence he was inclined to believe the applicant's account that she was not wai Jed. fie found rs a fact that the con- duct of the girls in replacing straps was winked at. There were times when neither of the "grinders " was esent, and the girls were left to do them best under are ci,curnsl laces. He awarded applicant compensa- tion at tha rate of 3s. GO a week from the date of the a :•cident, with costs.</p>
      </div>
