      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HINTS FOR THE HOME.</head>
        <p>HINTS FOR THE HOME.</p>
        <p>Bacon rinds, after being scalded and scraped, should„ be saved for flavouring stocks and stews.</p>
        <p>A teoeupful of salt to every bucket of water will prevent dye running or fading from coloured articles.</p>
        <p>Meat may be kept fresh by brushing the joint all over with a mixture of one table- spoonful of vinegar to two of )Natef.</p>
        <p>For removing stains and grease spots from clothing, lady motorists, it is stated, find pure petrol such as is consumed by the en- gine of the motor very effective.</p>
        <p>No Polish should ever be used for lac- quered articles; if once applied the metal polish will spoil the surface of the lacquer. Lacquered goods should be rubbed up with a leather, and, if dirty, washed with a little warm water and soap.</p>
        <p>A Stimulant.—An excellent stimulant is made of coffee and egg. Separate the yolk of the egg from the white and beat the latter until it is a stiff froth. Boil and sweeten the coffee, and boiling milk to taste, then pour it gently on the yolk, beating it well. Lastly, stir into it the white of egg, and serve at once.</p>
        <p>Warts can be removed by an apiiication of the milk-like juice of spurge, "chick-weed" as it is commonly called. Saturate the wart with it two or three times daily, and in time it will disappear.</p>
        <p>Rust may be removed from nickel plating by covering the spots with mutton tallow and letting it stand for several days. If this i treatment s followed by a rubbing with pow- dered rotten-stone and then by a thorough washing with strong ammonia, succeeded by clear water, and a final polishing with dry whiting, stubborn cases will yield.</p>
        <p>Victoria Pudding.—Line a dish with puff pastry, put a layer of jam at the bottom, take the yokes of two eggs, two ounces but- ter, half teacupful milk, dessertspoonful cornflower, dissolve them on the fire until thickened, lay this over the jam; beat the whites of the eggs to a stiff froth, stir in two ounces sifted sugar, put it. over the top and bake half an hour in a moderate oven.</p>
        <p>A good way to whiten and improve the complexion is as follows: Take two tea- spoonfuls of flowers of sulphur and mix it in a eu-pful of milk. Let this stand for three hours so that the sulphur will settle, then ap- ply the milk to the face, leave it on for a minute, then wash it off in warm water.</p>
        <p>To Boil Salmon.—Select a fresh, firm fish, wash and prepare it for dressing. Lay the salmon on a strainer, scatter salt over it very thickly, then cover with cold water. Let this heat gently, and only simmer allowing three- quarters a an hour slow boiling for a fish of eiolt or nine pounds. Brush the skin over with salad oil just before serving.</p>
        <p>For Fruit Salad.—Boil half a pound of white sugar with a little water till you have a syrup. Peel some ripe fruit—apples, pears, bananas, etc., and add to the syrup in a howl. Add half an ounce of pistachio nuts. blanched and chopped. and half an ounce of desiecated cocoanut. When the salad is cold, add two tablespoonfuls of liqueur or brandy and serve</p>
        <p>In order to know whether a 'cake is ready a knitting pin or a sharp-pointed knife should be run into the centre. If it can be withdrawn without any resistance and is clean and dry, the cake is done and can be removed at once. The tin should be gently tapped at the bottom, and tile cake rolled on to a sieve, where it can cool slowly.</p>
        <p>For split* seams on mackintosh use this cement. Dissolve some fine shreds of pure indiarubber in naphtha or sulphide of car- bon, to form a stiff paste. Apply a little of the cement to each edge that is to be joined, bring the edges together, and place a weight over them till they are hard.</p>
        <p>Lemon Buns.—Mix two ounces of ground rice with six ounces of flour, one teaspoonful of baking powder and the granted rind of half a lemon. Rub in lightly two ounces of lard or butter. Beat up an egg into a gill of milk. Stir into the ingredients, beat for a few minutes, put into greased bun tins and bake in a quick ovcf, for nearly a quarter of an hour.</p>
        <p>Here is a good lacquer for brasswork. Tho- roughy clean the brass in the usual way, then with a small camel-hair pencil apply a thin, smooth coating of seccotine. This will last for 'months in a dry atmosphere, and give a rich gold colour to the brass. I have tested this on brass mountings of an overmantel, which is more or less exposed to smoke and steam; it retains its brightness after many months.</p>
        <p>Gooseberry Cream.—Stew a pound and a half of green gooseberries, with plenty of sugar, and rub them through a sieve. Then add the yolks of five eggs, and beat well. Place in a sauce- pan on the fire, and continue to beat until the mixture is nearly boiling. Then remove the pan quickly from the fire and continue whisking, adding, when cool, the stiffly beaten whites of decorate the top of each with whipped cream.</p>
        <p>Delicious Cold Aweet.—Soak in a liasin two ounces of ratafias, one ounce of sliced citron, with a wineglass of sherry. Then take one pint of milk, in a little of which mix smoothly one and a half ounces of cornflour. Put the remainder in a saucepan with one ounce of castor sugar. When hot pour in the corn- flour mixture, boil eight minutes, stirring all the time; then stir in a soaked mixture; and pour in a melted mould. When cold, turn out and serve plain, or with whipped cream.</p>
        <p>Of late years china and glassware have so far replaced the silver formerly used in the dining-room that its cleaning and polishing is not the task it used to be; yet, some silver is used by nearly all housekeepers, but the articles will not add to the appearance of the table unless they are kept shiningly clean. The most expensive silverware will soon be- come dull and lusterless if not cared for pro- perly, and it may be kept bright without such frequent polishings by washing often i in a strong lather of white ivory soap and warm, sc,ft water, and then rubbing dry with a soft, dry cloth, and polishing with chamois leather.</p>
        <p>Caen Mackerel.—Clean fish of even size till you have sufficient for the dish. Make some leery savoury forcemeat, using plenty of chopped parsley and herbs. Season all with pepper and sat and fill the insides of the fish. Tic up with a string and lay in a baking dish. Season again with pepper and salt, and dot butter all over the fish; also a squeeze of freshly-eut lemon. Bake in a moderate oven. Serve with some good brown gravy.</p>
        <p>Broiled Fish.—Split open and clean a good- sized haddock or mackerel, and steep it in the following mixture fpr an hour. Three table- spoonsful of oil, two of vinegar, half a small onion minced, half a teaspoonful of mixed herbs; pepper and salt. Take Up the fish, drain, then flour it and broil over a clear fire —serve with this piquante sauce. Make a quarter of a pint of good melted butter sauce, and stir into it a tablespoonful of finely- chopped piccalilli.</p>
        <p>To make sponges soft and white wash them with warm water containing a little tartaric acid. Then rinse in plenty of cold water so that the acid is completely eliminated. Do not pt much tartaric acid or the sponges will be spoilt.</p>
        <p>Fruit Salad.—Green gooseberries may be mixed with other fruits, so as to make a season- able fruit salad. Stew a pint and a half of gooseberries in water, with plenty ef sugar, and the thin rind of half a lemon. Make a syrup of water and loaf sugar, and flavour with half a glass of maraschino and the juice of one orange. Rave ready four bananas, peeled and sliced, and add to them the syrup with the gooseberries, a quarter of a pound of grapes— skinned and stoned—and one orange peeled and fooly</p>
