      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MAY 2nd, 1908.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>AREVOLUTION in Paraffin Oil Lighting.—The lixoelslor Incandescent Paraffin burner produces with an Incandescent mantle a brilliant white light of 80 candle.. Power : it consumes half the oil and gives double the light; no smell or smoke ; the safest lamp extant ; burner, mantle, and chimney (small size) fis. 6d. ; burner, mantle and chimney (large size recommended), is. 6d. ; postage 4d. extra; agents wanted.—VlNE &amp; Co., 33, Stamford et, London, B.S.</p>
        <p>The int..in object of Mr. COMING TO AsQUITH'S speech on the CLOSE Licensing Bill on Tuesday QUARTERS. was the clearing away of misapprehension. He first reiterated his former description of the objects of the Bill. The first of these is to improve, primarily, by a reduction in the number of licenses, but also by other salutary changes in the law, the condi- tions under which the liquor traffic is at present carried on. The second object is to recover for the State, with due regard to existing interests, that control which was given away by Mr. BA.Lroun in 1904. There was some incredulous laughter on the Opposition side of the House when the PRIME MINISTER spoke of paying due regard to existing interests, and his qualification of the phrase may be said to be the keynote of his speech. There would, he said, be due—but not undue—regard to existing interests. That surely is an admirable 1 and clear definition of the policy of the Govern- ment. It desires to dual fairly with the licensed interest, but it will yield up to it nothing to which it is not entitled. Its object is not persecution or spoliation, but the establishment of just rela- tions between the licensed interests and the State.</p>
        <p>The very statement of such a proposition implies that the Government is open to reconviction in regard to certain features of its Bill. Having no desire to do injustice, it will listen tolerantly to any reasonable representations on the subject. In the meantime, the PRIME MINISTER asks the trade and the public to look at the Bill in a calm and attentive spirit. Hitherto it has been the subject of gross misrepresentation. Mr. ASQUITH told the House of Commons that the only serious criticism of the Government's proposals he had come scroll'</p>
        <p>_..sim.•••I••=•MINIIM</p>
