        <p>was that it was no use suppressing public-houses I if they did not at the same time provide against the epspringing in their places of bogus clubs. Now, it must be obvious that any Gwenment intending to deal honestly with this question must be as much opposed to the estab- lishment of bogus clubs as to the retention of such public-houses as are either unnecessary or so ill- managed as to be injurious to the public morals and the public interest. What the Parma Mirusran says, in fact, is that both the Govern- ment and himself are keenly alive to the danger of these clubs, and are anxiously prepared to listen to suggestions which might prevent the develop- ment of the apprehended evil. Anyone who has given any attention at all to the proposals made in the Licensing Bill must be aware that what it proposes in regard to clubs has given rise to a seriously hostile agitation. To the argument contained in the amend- ment for which Mr. CAVE stands sponsor (which is that the Bill proposes nothing which is likely to promote the cause of temper- ance), the PRIME MINISTER rqakes a really clinch- ing reply. A reduction in thei number of licenses was one of the objects of Mr. BALFOUR'S Bill. With what purpose, Mr. Asßurru inquired, did the hon. gentlemen opposite support the Bill of 1904, which proposed a reduction in the number of licensed-houses, if they did not believe that a reduction would make for temperance ? Much of the Bill is, of course, an extension of Balfourian principles. Even the phrase, "monopoly value," COMM from that measure. There is a melancholy abundance of confused thought on this subject, said the PRIME MINISTER. The monopoly value in the case of a free license is the difference between the value of the house when it is unlicensed and when it is licensed. It is, in fact, just what the licensing authority gives to the licensee for nothing. In many cases it represents thousands of pounds. Lord GREY, for example, was offered ten thousand pounds for a licensed house which was not worth one-twientieth of that amount, the day before the licence was granted. Mr. ASQUITH concluded a most interesting and closely-reasoned speech by putting to Mr. CAVE, the author of the motion for the rejection of the Bill, some searching questions which that gentle- man did not find it judicious to supply with answers.</p>
        <p>Last Friday Mr. Winston MR. CHURCHILL. Churchill was in the throes</p>
        <p>of his Titanic struggle to hold North-West Manchester for the cause of progress against the forces of reaction and monopoly. His defaat was engraved on the pages of history on Saturday, and more than half a nation mourned. Yesterday the indomitable Mr. Churchill re-entered the lists, smiling and ready as ever for the fray, to grapple with the same foes on the battleground of Dundee. We would that he could have a fair field and no favour,but we note that the suffragists have already " queered the pitch " and are ready with insidious lobs "to again endeavour to keep Mr. Churchill out. Their's is not a noble part,and 'tis indeed queer consistency which impels them to oppose a friend and risk setting back the clock of reform and progress. One cannot entirely dis- sociate such tactics from the charge of seeking cheap notoriety. Meanwhile, Mr. Churchill stands confident, and undismayed either by the shrieking of the suffragists or the clamour of the brewers, for has he not said in pregnant phrase : "There is in the heart of every political reverse the dynamic impulse of a future triumph. You must turn the emotions of defeat to the process of recovery, so that the very hour of disaster may become the seed- time of victory."</p>
        <p>The defeat of Mr. Churchill Nouru-WEST was a disaster, but the fact MANCHESTER. that it was by no means • unexpected lessens its bitterness from the Liberal standpoint. The brilliant young President of the Board of Trade showed a bold front against odds which might have daunted the most imperturbable and case hardened politician in the country, and he came so near defeating the hetrogeneous conglo neration of foes arrayed against him that one regrets the early close of the contest. By sheer argumentative power he was beating down the opposition, and in another few days he might have accomplished the miraculous fea,t of holding an acknowleged Tory seat for the Liberal Government in the face of a combination of interests which drew the sinews of war from all parts of the country, and which were determined to stick at nothing to bring about his downfall. They succeeded, and in the howls of jubilation forgot that they had deprived Man- chester and Lancashire of the inestimable privilege of direct representation by one of the ablest members of the Government and the head of the department which is most closely identified with liable the National commerce. Our loss is Ii to be Dundee's gain The canny ScoW are not so likely Ito be turned from the straight path by clamour and misrepresentation as were the more fickle and emotional electors of North-West Manchester. ,</p>
        <p>• The most curious sequel to this historic election , is the scramble amongst the wolves for the bits. The-..e surely never was such competition to claim credit for a crime as when a Ministerial candidate has been defeated at a by-election. The corres- pondence-bag of every newspaper has been bulging with the letters of claimants for the honour of having killed Mr. Churchill at Manchester. "Alone we did it," say the brewers; " on the contrary, it was we," cry the Tariff Reformers; and" what nonsense they talk, it was all our doing," cry the Suffragists. "Not at all," put in the clergy, "It was we who drew off the votes, because of your detestable Education Bill " ; and " but for us," say the Catholics, "the whole Irish vote would have gone for Churchill and carried him in." And finally comes the Coal Consumer's League, which is quite positive that " dear coal" did the murder. Really, between them all we are not sure that the best and simplest plan is not that of the "London Observer," which says quite frankly," we will make it mean what we wish it to mean." Since the Liberals, it argues, claimed the 1906 victory for Free Trade, they cannot com- plain if we claim the 1908 victory for Tariff Reform, though it was "due in part to other than Fiscal issues." That is practically what all the Protectionist newspapers do in the teeth of the ' fact that their candidate laboured his utmost to prove that Tariff Reform was not the issue at this election, and that Free Traders might give him a , vote without prejudice to that cause. The claim 'of the "Morning Post" that Mr. Churchill's defeat sent "thedeath-knell of Free Trade ring- ing through the land" merely provokes a smile. Let Free Trade be the issue pure and simple and all Lancashire would once again pronounce solidly in its favour. •,.et '</p>
        <p>Warrington has now an THE INFIRMARY.—A Infirmary almost second FINAL APPEAL. to none for a town, of similar size, and the towns- people are justly proud of the fact. Unfor- tunately, however, tho buildiug is not free from debt and, generous as the response has already been, it is necessary for one more effort to be made to raise the balance of £615 10s. still required to defray the whole of the cost of enlarging the institution, and bringing it into line with the requirements of the town. The Committee have issued a final appeal pointing out that 01,13 grand total of subscriptions to date is £15,384 10s. but, as has already been ' explained, in order to carry out certain works in connection with the buildings which were deemed necessary and expedient, the total cost of the undertaking is £l6,ooolllThe Board of Management earnestly appeal tollany who have:not:arhscribed</p>
      </div>
