        <p>completing this good work, and any increase in the donations of those who have already so hand- somely responded to the appeal will be much appreciated. Toe district is already deriving great advantage from the additional accommodation , afforded by the extension,the number of in-patients receiving attention having increased by at least 25 per cent. It must not be forgotten that this increase involves a corresponding increase in the cost of maintenance,and it will be necessary that the list of annual subscriptions should be substantially augmented. We feel sure that Warrington people will rise to the occasion and that the work of this noble institution will not be hampered by straitened resources</p>
        <p>Emissaries of St. Thomas's</p>
        <p>THE SPURIOUs Church have this week been PARENTS' LEAGUE. worrying Stockton Heath householders for signatures to a form supporting the " Parents' League," the mushroom-like organization which sprang up suddenly after the introduction of Mr. McKenna's Education Bill. The League aims at securing the right of the parent to have his child taught the religion he desires in school hours at the State expense. It also professes independence of party politics or religious denomination, and one League leaflet ingenuously concludes in large letters : "Let Liberals and Conservatives unite and demand that every child should be brought up in the religion of its parents." Doubtless many signatures would be obtained from the unthinking. Those who paused to reflect would see with half an eye that the leaders of the Parents' League only desired one religion to be taught—that of the Established Church. The Church is making as great a to-do about the "rights of the parent" as the brewers are about the rights of the workingman As a matter of fact, neither cares one jot for the rights of anybody but itself, though each uses ' specious appeals to enlist public support for selfish ends. This precious Parents' League is nothing but a piece of denominational trickery, and its pro- moters ought to be severely heckled whenever they come out into the open. We deny in toto the right of parents to have their children taught denomina- tional religion at other people's expense. Tax- payers and rate-payers are not all parents I Fortunately, too, all parents do not unite in making such an absurd demand, and we hope each and every parent will look below the surface before succumbing to the blandishments of such a hybrid growth as the aforesaid Paints' League, which issues printed matter without the name of a single person—president, secretary, or treasurer—upon it as a guarantee of good faith. The State can have no concern with denominational teaching, and the demand of those who would foist their religion upon the elementary. schools must be resisted to the uttermost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The cuckoo was heard at Reddish Hall, Grappenhall, on Wednesday. * * * County Court work is booming at Warrington. At the last two courts there have been all-day sittings and so great is the pressure of business that the judge has fixed a special court for Monday, May 25th, at which some cases adjourned f rom last Thursday will be heard.</p>
        <p>* The new Warrington Post Office in Springfield- street, which is nearing completion, is a handsome building, and has been generally admired. It is expected that the Postmaster-General (Right Hon. Sydney Buxton, M.P.) will perform the openfng ceremony towards the end of this month. * * * It is rather curious that of the four elections which Mr. Winston Churchill has now fought he has /stood in two as a Conservative and two as a Liberal ; he has won once as a Conservative and once as a Liberal ; he has lost once as a Conservative and once as a Liberal; and all four elections have been in Lancashire. Further whereas at Oldham Mr. Runciman first beat him and then he beat Mr. Runciman ; at Manchester he first beat Mr. Joynson-Hicks and now has been beaten by him.</p>
        <p>* * * The Wesleyans of Warrington are showing an admirable public spirit on the licensing question. At a meeting held in Bold-street Church on Sunday evening, attended by fully 1,500 people, a resolution in support of the Government Bill was carried unanimously and with great enthusiasm. The speeches were of a high order, emphasising the superiority of human rights to property rights and the absolute justice of the Bill to the public and to the trade. The Wesleyans are to be congratulated upon giving such a strong lead in a great moral cause which pre- eminently concerns religious .communities.</p>
        <p>* * Saturday, May 23rd, will be a red-letter day for Warrington, for it will witness the holding of the first , Warrington Musical Festival at the Parr Hall. We hear that the entries are excellent and the towns- people are promised a fine musical treat. The chief choral competition for mixed voices should be worth coming a long way to bear, for eight leading choirs have entered and there will be two beautiful test pieces "He, watching over Israel " (Mendlessohn) and " Moonlight " (Eaton Faning). Other leading features will be the male voice choral competition, church or chapel choir contest, and children's choral competition. * The good wishes of Warrington Liberals will be extended to Mr. Francis Neilson in his fight in the Newport (Shropshire) division to fill the vacancy occasioned by the death of Colonel Kenyon Slaney,M.P. Mr. Neilson, who made a gallant fight in the con- stituency at the general election, is well-known in Warrington where he rendered splendid service to the progressive cause by his powerful oratory. In return Mr. Joseph Howes has gone to help him in his present campaign. Mr. Neilson has been nursing the Shropshire constituency for the last five years. He resides in the division and is a Shropshire man. * * An inquisitive newspaper correspondent writes to ask for an estimate of the total cost of the little expedi- tion of Alderman Salvidge and his two hundred colleagues from Liverpool to North-West Manchester on Friday, and also the source of the supply of the sinews of war. In reply, it may be pointed out that two hundred return railway fares at 4s. 6d. come to £45; two hundred half-day's wages at 35., £3O; and two hundred and thirty men's drinks, four at 2d. each, £5; total, :£BO. An inkling of the source of supply is supplied by another correspondent, who is a brewery shareholder, and is indignant at being asked to submit to a pro rata levy on his holding towards a " trade " defence fund.</p>
        <p>* * Quick wits get the last words.—Dianc. Opposition and inaccessibility are the sauce piquante of life—take them away and it becomes flat, weari- some and disappointing. —Rita. The woman who wants a vote has given up all thoughts of getting anything better out of life. It's a sort of twentieth century taking the veil.—rna Garvey. Give a modern young woman a decent social position, with enough money for her wants, an average amount of assurance, and she becomes so fastidious in the choice of a mate, that no man is good enough for her till she is too old to be good enough for any man.—Marion Crawford. Pretty speeches make very sickly conversation.— G.B. Shaw.</p>
        <p>First catch your reputation and then see if you can loep up with it.—iliner.</p>
        <p>* * * Everybody had a word of praise for the smartness with which the old shops at Market-gate were demol- ished and the site cleared. Shop-keepers and regular passers-by had visions of a broad, clean thoroughfare free from scaffolding and hoardings for the first time in twenty years. But, though it took only about a week to remove the old buildings it has taken four times as long to get the street levelled and paved and the job is not done yet. The delay in laying a few yards of setts has been positively painful. Time seems to have been filled in by the making of a foundation solid enough for a bank. Anyhow the setts were down a few days ago, and the wood blocks for the Market-gate " circus " have now arrived on the scene. We regret that we cannot given much promise of the widened street being in full use for some time yet, however. As soon as the Trinity Church side is finished the traffic will I be turned on to the new pavement and the old street roped off. Then will re-commence the same slow process on this side. °No wonder Bridge-street shop- keepers are groaning in bitterness of spirit. Street- making in Warrington seems to be anything but a fine art, though time-killing has well nigh reached perfection. (0011,',. ;Tr.. ,„</p>
        <p>"I received this morning," writes a correspondent in Monday's" Daily News ' " a free 'specimen' copy of one of the Harmsworth Sunday papers, accompanied by a letter from the Editor generously appraising the merits of his wares and promising to send me a further free copy. I turned to the leader and found it began with this trumpet note: '"-tHE DEBACLE.</p>
        <p>Manchester has spoken : Free Trade is at an end. We do not wish to exaggerate . . . Remembering the facts at the Manchester contest and the furious attacks of the Protectionist Press on Mr. Joynson-Hicks for his base desertion of Tariff Reform, I wondered what this speciman of Lord Northcliffe's myriad newspapers would be like when it did wish to exaggerate.' Perhaps the next free copy will be less severely restrained. As it is my rule never to buy anything from the Harmsworth factory I am grateful for this opportunity of testing its moderation without breaking my rule."</p>
        <p>* * The opponents who turned up at the Citizens Temperance League's public meeting in the National Schools on Monday evening did not spend a very happy time. They thought to cause consternation by silly interruptions but they were cornered on every point and the feeling of the meeting was so markedly in favour of the Bill that the would-be disturbers must have gone away much disconcerted, The Rev. R. E. Letheren,vvho made his first appearance on a local plat - form,is to be congratulated upon his able and convincing address. He dealt with the many questions fired at him in a tactful and skilful way and we have no doubt that the objectors, if they were so disposed, might have learnt a great deal from his address. We hope to hear Mr. Letheren championing the cause of which he is such an able advocate at many future meetings in Warrington. ,</p>
        <p>* * * We wonder if any of our Borough Councillors—or a nice plump Alderman for choice—could be persuaded to cycle from Mersey-street corner to Messrs. Fairclough's flour warehouse. The ride would only be short, but it would include Warrington Bridge and might also include the death of the rider. If there is a more dangerous piece of street-paving in Lancashire than the tramway track over the bridge, on the left going to Latchford, we should like to see it and have it photographed. Warrington Bridge is sufficiently trying to the cyclist under the best conditions, bat with pit-falls in the pavement added to the congested traffic it is positively murderous. Somebody ought to be made erztrainally responsible for such a state of things—then perhaps it would be remedied!</p>
        <p>_ * * * Miss Evelyn R. Tait, the newly-appointed matron of the Warrington Infirmary, has had a varied experience of hospital work eminently calculated to fit her for Ithat important post. She is a daughter of the late Sir Peter Tait and received her training at the Liverpool Royal Infirmary. After acting as sister at the Liver- pool Skin and Cancer Hospital she joined the Army Nursing Service Reserve and served in South Africa for two and a half years on the staff of No. 7 General IHospital stationed at Estcourt and Pretoria. She holds two medals for that campaign. In 1903 she was appointed sister of the men's medical ward at the Sheffield Royal Hospital and remained there two years. She then became night superintendent of the West London Hospital and in 1907 was promoted assistant matron, which position she at present holds</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events..</p>
        <p>- ln connection with the Warrington Band of Hope Union, a grand May festival will be held in the Parr Hall, on Wednesday and Thursday next. It will include Ye Olde Village Green, Crowning of the May Queen, Grand Character Procession, Plaiting the May Pole, Morris Dancers, Action Songs, Fancy Dancers isrc. Choruses by the Band of Hope Juvenile Choir will also be given. On Wednesday Mrs. James Dodds (President of the Union) will preside, whilst on Thurs- day the chair will be taken by Councillor Ilarrop (ex Lord Mayor of Manchester.) On Monday evening a meeting under the auspices of the Warrington Women's Suffrage Society will be held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street,when the principal speaker will be Mrs. Alan Bright, of Liver- pool. A special service will be held in St. Peter's Church to-morrow (Sunday) at 10.30, when the Mayor will unveil a memorial tablet to the late Alderman John Roberts, who, for upwards of twenty-five years was a churchwarden at this church. The Mayor will be accompanied by the members of the Corporation and the Vicar (the Rev. B. Percival) will be the preacher. The anniversary services in connection with Silver- street Sunday School will be held to-morrow (Sunday), in Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, when the preacher both morning and evening, will be the Rev. J. Grange Bennett In the afternoon a special choral service will be given by the members of the choir and children, and an address will be given by Mr. W. Hickman. The Rev. John Yonge will be the preacher at Wycliffe Congregational Church to-morrow, morning and evening. Mr. Oswald Garside.</p>
        <p>_ Our readers will be interested to learn that our local artist, Mr. Oswald Garside, has this year two pictures in the Royal Academy, and also has had one accepted by the Paris Salon for exhibition purposee. Latchford Motorist Fined.</p>
        <p>At Oswestry on Thursday Mr. Thomas M. Broadbent, The !lollies, Latchford, was charged with driving a motor-car to the danger of the public at Gobowen on September 9. The summons was returnable for last October sessions, but in consequence of the serious illness of the defendant the case had been adjourned. His advocate now stated that he was still too ill to appear. Police Sergeant Middleton said that upon pulling the defendant up and intimating that he would report the case Mr. Broadbent observed that the magistrates would surely fine him £5, and he would rather give it to the witness. A fine of five guineas was imposed.</p>
        <p>PITBLIO BENEFIT—IINDBR THIII CLOCK TOR MIST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRIL—AdVt.</p>
        <p>Infirmary and Dispensary. At a meeting of the Board of Management, under the presidency of Mr. W. Peter Rylands, Evelyn R. Tait, assistant matron, of the West London Hospital, was unanimously appointed matron of the Warrington Institution. There were originally 71 candidates for the post. Miss Tait, who will enter upon her duties in about a month, will succeed Miss Watkin, who has officiated as matron here for many yr, ' CLOCK</p>
        <p>rears,</p>
        <p>PUBLIO BENIUTIT.—IINDER TUB BOOTS IN LANO&amp;SHIRE.—Advt.</p>
