      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>Warrington:P.S.ll. A well attended meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. I)Society was held on Sunday in the Co-operative Hall, gankey-street. Councillor Hawthorn, who presided, ave an address on P.S.A. work, strongly advising the Oung men present to live tin to the true standard of urity and nobility of purpose. An address was elivered by Mr. W. Light on " The Christian life and a bearing on present day ambition and vain glory," rayer was offered by the President. The lesson was ead by Mr. Haywood. The members of the orchestra added much to the success of the meeting. Presentations to Mr. J. Orrin. At the close of Sunday evening's service a presents- was made in the schoolroom of Legh-street Church t Mr. J. H. Corria who sails to-day on board tae" Mauritania "en route for Kansas, U.S.A. The : Present took the form of a dressing case with a suitably inscribed silver plate attached. Mr. T. B. Parkes spoke, as an old member of the choir, of Mr. Col rin's valuable services and expressed regret at his intended departure, wishing him God-speed to his new home. 31r. Spruce in making the presentatioa also emphasised his long; and faithful services and, related an inter- esting incident in the early part of Mr. Carrin's connection with the choir and the commencement of his career as a soloist, on which occasion he stepped into the breach made by an absent soloist. In con- cluding Mr. Sprees trusted the little token of their regard would serve to keep him reminded of his con- nection with Lesit-street choir.—Mr. Corrin thanked the choir and friends for their beautiful gift but said no present was needed to remind him of Legh-street the happy associations of which he could never forget. Re appealed to the choir to rally round their conductor in the time to come. Mr. Bissell also spoke a few Words of encouragement and advice. The proceedings were closed by singing " God be with you till we !fleet again."— On Thursday evening Mr. Corrin was given a very hearty send-off by his fellow-members of the House and Ship Painters' Society, who also pre- sented hii . with a handsome gold watch bearing a suitable inscription. Bro. Graham (President), spoke in glowing terms of Mr. Corrin's 12 years' work for tile Society, and Bro. F. Bate made the presentation.. boring the evening an excellent musical programme 17as sustained by Messrs. Willett, Corrin, Spruce, ' :Gough, W. Bennett, H. Pitchford, S. Payns, and J. Pollard. •</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Independent Order of Oddfellows. At a social gathering in connection with the Travellers' Rest " Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows Manchester Unity, held at the Mulberry Tree Hotel' on Saturday evening, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and Mr. Joseph Shillcock were initiated as hon. members. There was a large attendance of Oddfellows from various lodges in the district, and Pray. Past Grand Master William Holbrook was voted to the chair. Among others present were Prov. Grand Master Lot Brooks, Prov. Deputy Grand )daster G. F. Freeman, Past Prov. Grand Masters James Massey (hon. lecture master), Frank Webster, ,OS., Councillor T. S. Steel, T. P. Bradshaw, Alban Boyle, Jos. Taylor, Gilbert Riley, R. H. Halford and Norbury, Past Grands Barker, w. Weave, J a akey, T. Downes, T. P. Forbes, H. Lawrence, A. nay, J. Smith, T. Horrobin, A. E. Spann, Fred 'Webster, A. Pemberton, R. Straw, W. Houghton, J. Willett, W. E. H. King, T. Reddish, and A. Marsh ; nIC'G''B W. H. Smalley and J. Hogan, and Bro. ouncilior Nield.—The initiation ceremony was Vpressively performed by the Hon. Lecture raster, and Bros. Sir Gilbert Greenall and J. Thl"eeck took their seats amidst loud applause. e health of the pewly-initiated members were drunk 4 the -proposition of P.P.G.M. Bradshaw and Bro. lr Gilbert Greenall and Bro. Shillcock responded. ro. T. S. Steel proposed the toast of " The Warring- to District " and Bro. Frank Webster responded. tßro. Councillor Nield, asked to speak, said it seemed o him that Odd fellowship and friendly societies in Reneral constituted true Socialism. It was Socialism 'hich no one could object to Socialism which aPPealed to all. During the evening songs were Ixcellently rendered by Bros. J.Willett, A. Pemberton Bro MacCiachlan ; Bro. Massey gave a recitation, and 'ire. Fred Webster was the accompanist. Bro. Free- -IQ4II carried out the arrangements of the gathering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>– -4), MOTHER AND CHILD KILLED. Shocking Accident at Glazebrook.</head>
        <p>- -4), MOTHER AND CHILD KILLED. Shocking Accident at Glazebrook.</p>
        <p>WasGlazebrook station, on the Cheshire Lines Railway, tile scene of a shocking accident on Thursday InCl:rni pg. Mrs. Hindley, of Lowton St. Mary's, was g°!ng to Warrington Infirmary to visit her mother, who 13 111, and was taking her child with her. She was she (Tossing the line when an express train came along,and and the child were killed. Hindlev was the wife of Andrew Hindley, of qewton-road, Lowton, a Leigh Co-operative Society's ai,"°Prtian. The child with her was Maggie, ten years of "ge. tr They were crossing the line behind a standing ain ; when they were knocked down. Nlrs. Hindley's tilter, who was crossing the station bridge, witnessed Vie accident. The Inquest, MR. BRIGHOUSE HAS A LIVELY TIME.</p>
      </div>
