      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODISTS. DISTRICT SYNOD AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODISTS. DISTRICT SYNOD AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Primitive Methodists of the Liverpool District are this week-end holding their Synod at Warrington. About 70 representatives and visitors are taking part in the proceedings, and they represent circuits comprising a large area. Warrington has long been closely identified with Primitive Methodism, and the name of Lorenzo Dow is linked with the religious life of the town. It is worth noting that the church membership at Warrington has more than doubled during the last thirty-five years, so that the growth of the society has at least kept pace with the growth of the population of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>OPENING SERVICE.</head>
        <p>OPENING SERVICE.</p>
        <p>The opening service was held in Legh-street Church on Thursday evening and was conducted by the Rev. T. K. Upright, who represents Birkeehead District No. 1. An address was delivered by the Rev. A. J. Wigley, general committee delegate. Mr. WioLgv dealt principally with prayer. Jesus Christ was our instructor, for we had on record His own wonderful examples. Jesus Christ was asked by the disciples how they should pray, and He gave them instructions. The Lord's prayer was beautiful in its simplicity, and they did right to use it. They were not compelled to say it as Jesus Christ said it, for St. Matthew said " Pray ye in like manner." The prayer was full of supplication and direction. Dealing with selfishness Mr. Wright said it was too frequent in prayer. , They must not pray for their own ends but for a pure heart. He did not question the sincerity of their prayers, but by selfish praying they were making a great mistake. He questioned whether they always hal pure motives. There were, of course, legitimate needs of personal character, as recognised in the second part of the Lord's prayer; but they were holy and unselfish. Christians ought to be able to stake anything for the word of Jesus Christ. They could not get the answer to their prayer all at once. They must not lose heart and cease praying as many had done. The reason they lost heart was simply hecause they lost faith in God. Selfish- ness should have no place whatever in their prayers, but God's glory should have their first consideration. CIVIC RECEPTION. Last evening the delegates and their hosts and hostesses were received at Bold-street Wesleyan School by the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst) who extended to the visitors a very hearty welcome. The Rev. Sidney Pitt (superintendent) and several of the local Wesleyan ministers were present. Light refreshments were provided and afterwards the , formal welcome took place. The Rev. J. B. BUGLISS said they were all exceed- ingly pleased to have the Mayor and Mayoress with them. (Applause.) There was a time when a function of that kind would have been impossible so far as Primitive Methodism was concerned, but now they had become a respectable people. (Laughter.) Since he (Mr. Baglass) came to Warrington he had not heard one adverse criticism of the Mayor—in fact all agreed that he had filled his office with conspicuous ability, rare dignity and impartiality. (Applause.) All movements which had for their object the material and moral betterment of the people had the sympathy of the Mayor and Mayoress. he was pleased to find that the Mayoress had turned her attention to the infant life of the town and that she and other ladies had come for- ward to try and put a stop to the extreme mortalty amongst the children. (Applause.) The MAYOR heartily welcomed the delegates to the town and expressed the hope that their meetings would be blessed with success. He would like to see all the churches united and working together in one bond of fellowship with the ooe object of reaching those who at present stood outside the churches. He wished they would follow the example of the Salvation Army and , visit the poor people in the slums. He trusted the efforts which were being put forward to reduce infantile mortality would be crowned with success. They hoped shortly to have a visit from the Mayor of Huddersfield who took such a prominent part in dealing with the question. (Applause). The Rev. ,J. G. BARKEY of Southport, in moving a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress said such a reception was very fitting as the communal authority and the churches both had at heart the well-being of 'the people. They appreciated the Mayor's generosity all the more when they remembered that he belonged to the Established Church. There were few ways in which they could break down narrow ideas and prejudies better than by coming into personal contact. ',Applause). Alderman ROBERTS, Of Clitheroe, seconded and the vote was heartily accorded.</p>
      </div>
