      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 2, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 2, 1908.</p>
        <p>time overshadowing all others,"—and Jarrow and Colne had loudly heralded alike the advent and power of Demos—no longer unwashed, illiterate, and inarticulate, but roused by education and armed with the vote. To-day the two great world forces were Christianity with its fine history, splendid ideals and beneficent power—and democracy with its new consciousness of power and its large expectations. The question was : Were the two powers to be allies or foes? Reform must come. Was it to be on Christian or Agnostic lines, by a natural evolution or by revolutionary unrest and shock. Some alienation , could not be denied, when 70 per cent, of the working classes practically neglected the Church and her services and a small but active party of democrats promoted a virulent campaign against the Church, while some Christian men looked suspiciously on all attempts to induce the Church to take part in the solving of social problems. It was part of the duty of the Social Service Onion to strive to avert any divison or strife between 'Christianity and Democracy—the ideal to be aimed at being in Flenderson's phrase "to Christianise democracy and to democratise the Church." There were many reasons why the Church should actively promote Social Reform. Democracy was the child of Christianity, springing largely out of the spiritual revivals associated with the names of Wesley, Bourne and Clowes, Christianity was concerned with all that concerns the whole man, and does not invalidate citizenship, nor free from its obligations. Many workers were in both camps and could not keep their ecouomic thinking and their Christian thinking in separate compartments ; whilst it could not be forgotten that some of their Church mem hers were amongst the over-worked, under-paid badly- housed class that social service sought to redeem. Primitive IVlnthodism had a unique position in com- bining the ardent social reformer and labour leader with the local preacher and Church official. The need for social redress could not be denied, when in England practically one million people were supported by Poor- Law, and 32 per cent, lived below the poverty line of a guinea per family per week, under conditions fatal to perfect physical and moral health ; while the sweater ground the face of the poor, making possible such a heart-rending exhibition as that promoted by the "Daily News," taking his toll of flesh and blood and morals, unscathed either by law or public opinion ; and while the workman eager for employment sought in vain for work to provide for himself and his dependents. All was not well, and to quote Dr. Paton, "the congre- gations that meet in our places of worship dare not imitate the example of the Priest and Levite and pass by the wounded and pillaged traveller without attempting to bind up his wounds." The duty of the Church in Social Service was surely to , insist on justice as the basis of the settlement of Social questions, and neither exped iency,charity nor palliatives, taking the place of rendering to every man, master, and servant his just right. Modern problems had emerged since New Testament times, but the principles for their solution were to be fcund there injustice, brotherhood, and the golden rule. To be able to give judgment as to what was the just solution,systematic study was necessary —and practically all the churches were realising this,and already in the Anglican, Wesleyan and now in Primitive Methodist churches, earnest reformers were banding in , Unions for study first, than for intelligent and Christian action. No ready-made solution was to be had, for the questions were intricate and over-lapping. Poor Law Reform, housing, land, unemployment, criminology, trusts and syndicates, sweating, the living wage, were only some of the problems that were aching for solution —and the marvellous patience and self-control of the common people were written large on every page of their sad economic laistory.Should we not in our Church work and Social Service Unions help to heal their sorrows and assuage their pains, in the Spirit of Jesus, and through His immortal teaohing ? lie urged his hearers to join the parent Union, establish local branches, and aid the work of this youngest phase of their many-sided church activity. WELCOME FROM THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>The delegates were then heartily welcomed by Mr C. E. Parker (president), and the Rev. John Sponge on behalf of the Free Church Council. The Rev. W. SPEDDING acknowledged the greeting.</p>
        <p>qualities, one of which is a special thorn proof tyre which practically guarantees immunity from puncture. An extra heavy tyre is built for heavy riders and heavy roads—in fact the rider should be able to get just what he wants from Messrs. Bates' factory. The roadster tyres we have in use are exceptionally well- rubbered, with a tread which gives every promise of wearing well. It is the fault with so-many otherwise good tyres nowadays that they are thin in the tread which is of course the essential business part. Messrs Bates covers are not skimped in rubber of the best quality, and will well repay inspection. We recorn-1 mend those of our readers who happen to be particulat, in their choice of a tyre to write direct to Messrs. Bates for sections of their tyres, which will be sent post free. The tyres themselves can be obtained, locally at reasonable prices.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Cycling Club successfully opened the season onVaturday with an outing to Warburton. In spite of the cold weather a goodly number of members turned out, some riding and others walking. At the Saracen's Head tea was partaken of, and games of bowls were indulged in, after which a concert was held, the captain, Sergeant Major Delmage, presiding. Songs were rendered by Messrs. Poole, Hassan and Clay.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the club jonrneyed to Knutsford for the May festival and to-day they will ride to Acton Bridge when a bowling match will be played.</p>
        <p>We have received the Warrington Cycling club's fixture card for 1908 which is more attractive than ever and given fine weather the season promises to be the most successful in the history of the club. A varied programme of runs and other fixtures has been arranged, including inter-club run with the Manchester Wheelers, President's and Ladies' day (when the club will again be the guests of the Presi- dent at his dolightful home at Beeston), fishing com- petition, bowling mains and tours and week-end Jaunts through the most attractive country within measurable distance. The committee have again secured the bowling green at Sankey Chapel House, for the exclusive use of the members and their friends on Wednesday afternoons and evenings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>FORTHCOMING MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>FORTHCOMING MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>This (Saturday) evening there will be an open-air service at Bridge-foot followed by a public meeting in Bold-street Wesleyan Church. To-morrow (Sunday), there will be numerous services, including one at 6.30 p.m. in the Parr Hall, followed by a meeting for praise, prayer and testimony. On Monday evening there will be a public Ordination Service in Bold-street Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>" THE EMERALD ISLE."</head>
        <p>" THE EMERALD ISLE."</p>
        <p>Comic Opera Produced by the Crosfield Operatic Society.</p>
        <p>The success which the Crosfield Amateur Operatic Society achieved on its first public appearance twelve months ago has inspired the members to attempt more ambitious things, with the result that on Thursday evening the charming comic opera " The Emerald Isle" (Hood, Sullivan and German) was presented at the Parr Hall. The Society is to be complimented upon its courage and enterprise in thus extending its operations and also upon the fact that the performances will benefit two such deserving institutions as the Infirmary and District Nursing Association, between which the gross receipts are to be divided. The attendance on PUBLIC MEETING. Thursday, though not as large as one might have . expected, was encouraging and the appreciation most -- marked. The opera cannot fail to pleise, for it is full ADDRESSES ON TEMPERANCE AND !of sprightly melody, and delightful humour, with a SOCIAL REFORM. ; romantic region in Erin's Isle as its attractive setting. We have the anomaly of the chief of a rebel clan wooing Following the reception a public meeting was held in the daughter of the Lord Lieutenant and as may readily Bold-street Wesleyan Church, and was largely attended, b. e imagined some curious situations arise, only to end Mr. R. Wilkinson (Birkenhead) presided and Mr. J. in the traditional happy way. It would be too much H. Whate (Liverpool) occupied the vice-chair, to expect the performance to be free from The CHAIRMAN said that no subjects were so interest- , ing or of such vast importance as those which they had blemishes. There was a little , stiffness on the part of some of the principals, and in the to consider that night—temperance and social service ensemble the requisite animation and freedom were not union work—whether as affecting the individual or the always sufficiently realized, but when one considers the multitude, the home or the town, the social or the strictly amateur constitution of the society and the fact national life. Touching upon the 