        <p>There was just a glimmer of interest in the Lanca- shire Junior Shield Competition Final played at Wigan on Saturday. Warrington "A" were expected to become the first holders of the trophy seeing that they had half-a-dozen first team players available in Fish, Taylor, Brooks, Lloyd, Naylor and P. Boardman. This was exuected to form the nucleus of a powerful side, but the team cut up in p rinful fashion. Wigan hAd practically only lodd and Miller of the first team doing duty, but tffey outclassed the Warrington com- bination in every department. Our forwards appeared to have spines of chewed string, judging from the way they were beaten for possession of the ball, and the backs played mostly like novices with no icloa, of combination, Fish did good work at times, but had few opportunities, and it is better to draw a veil over the display of the rest. They could neither field, tackle, nor run.</p>
        <p>A duet was pleasingly rendered by Miss Bridge and Mr. J. H. Corrin.</p>
        <p>Wigan "A " were exceptionally smart all round and would have put paid to the account of many a premier team on Saturday's form. Their running and passing was speedy and clean, thongh they were certainly helped by the slackness of the referee in respect of forward transfers and knocking-on. They were perhaps hardly good enough for the 19 points scored, but Warrington were certainly had enough to merit the heavy defeat. Todd played a beautiful game for the Wiganers and was head and shoulders above the rest of the field. It V! ill be a great loss to Northern Union football if he goes back to New Zealand and stays there. But if money can tempt him he will stay at Wigan.</p>
        <p>I would just remind my readers in conclusion that the final for the'Works Competition will ho played at Wilderspool to-day at 3.30 An excellent game should be witnessed between Crosfield's and Rylands% with slight odds on " wire."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Downing—Dick.</p>
        <p>The marriage was solemnized at St. Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath, on Monday morning, of Mr. Henry Thomas Downing, son of the late Mr. William Down- ing, of Quinton, near Birmingham, and Miss Jessie Booth Dick, daughter of the late Mr. William Dick, of Bewsey-road, who for many years was employed at the Bewsey Offices of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. H. V. Pigot (Rector of Grappenhall), who had kindly consented to officiate in the absence of the Vicar of St. Thomas's (Rev. James Collins). The bride, who was given away by her brother-in-law (Mr. Thomas R. Westbrook), was ' tastefully attired in a brown tailor-made costume with blue silk blouse and hat to match. She was attended by Miss Beatrice Downing (sister of the bridegroom) who wore a mole coloured costume with cream hat trimmed with black ostrich feathers. In lieu of the usual bouquets, the bride and bridesmaid carried ivory I backed prayer and hymn books, the gift of the bride- I groom. Mr. William McClew, of Warrington (brother- I in-law of the bride) discharged the duties of best man. After the ceremony, the wedding breakfast was served at the King's Cafe, Bridge-street, Warrington. Later in the day Mr. and Mrs. Downing left for Quinton, near Birmingham, the bride's travelling dress being a blue costume with hat to match, and ostrich boa. They were the recipients of numerous handsome presents.</p>
        <p>Hallaway--Gerrard. An interesting wedding was solemnized on Wednesday at St. Cuthbert's, the old Parish Church of Carlisle, when Mr. A. T. Hallaway, deputy town clerk of War- rington, and third son of Mr. John Hallaway of Carlisle was married to Miss Frances Dovell Gerrard, only daughter of the late Mr. William Gerrard, of Rapid Bay, South Australia. The bridegroom is well-known in Carlisle, particularly in Rugby Football circles,being an old playing member of the Town Club. The officiat- ing clergy were the Rev. Richard Bower, Canon of Carlisle Cathedral, and the Rev. Nigel E. Stones, The bride who was given away by her step-father (Mr. N. S. Parminter, of Barnstaple, Devon), looked charm- ing in a gown onvory white satin Duchesse, handsomely embroidered with silver and crystal bugles and clusters of raised chiffon flowers, the bodice being elaborately embroidered to match the skirt. Long lines of silver ran from waist to hem in front,and also having a specially graceful effect upon the back of the bodice. The hand- some Court train of satin Duchesse caught on the shoulders with clusters of orange-blossom and silver, each corner being adorned with a true lovers' knot of silver tissue and an Empire half wreath of orange blossom. She carried a beautiful shower bouquet, consisting of white orchids and lilies of the valley,the gift of Mr.Aldernian Bolton,ex-Mayor of Warrington. She also wore a pearl and sapphire pendant, the gift of the bridegroom. The bride was accompanied by two bridesmaids, Miss Madge Hallaway and MiSS, Gladys Parminter' whose dresses were of sky blue eolienne trimmed with white Greek key pattern Russian braid with lace sleeves. They wore gold bracelets, the gifts of the bridegroom, and carried lovely bouquets. Mr. Dan iel Jackson, Deputy Town Clerk of Southport, acted as best man, and Mr. Stanley B. Pearson, of the Cumberland Rugby Union, was grooms- man. Mr. Theodore Waldron M.A., presided at the organ, and the service was /idly choral. After the ceremony a reception was held at the Crown and Mitre Hotel, Carlisle. The bride and bridegroom left early in the afternoon for the South of England, where the honeymoon will be spent. The bride's travelling dress was a light tailor-made costume with pink and grey hat to match and a grey ostrich feather boa. There were numerous handsome presents received by the happy couple.</p>
        <p>On Monday the officials of the Warrington Corpora- tion presented Mr. Hallaway with a wedding gift, which took the form of a handsome case of cutlery suitably inscribed.—The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), in making the presentation, spoke highly of Mr. Hallaway's work at Warrington, and wished him and his future wife every happiness.—Mr. Hallaway acknowledged the present in suitable terms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. ,</p>
        <p>. By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>To-day sees the end of Rugby Football for four months, and the interval is little enough. Hardly anyone will feel sorry that the curtain has at last been rung down, for as far as the Warrington's Club's doings have been concerned things have been very tame for the past few weeks. The Cup defeat practi- cally extinguished the flame of local enthusiasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Business of the Next Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly Meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening at the Town Hall at 6 o'clock, when the following among other business will be transacted. STREET IMPROVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk has received the sanction of the Local Government Board for the borrowing of £22,201 and £1,836 for street improvements. THE IRREGULARITIES IN THE SURVEYOR'S DEPARTMENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>AN AUDIT DEPARTNIENT TO BE FOR‘IED.</head>
        <p>AN AUDIT DEPARTNIENT TO BE FOR‘IED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance and Estate Committee, the following report of the Borough Treasurer on the subject of the internal audit of the accounts of the Corporation was submitted and approved, and ordered to be submitted to the General Purposes Committee : " Report arising out of the irregularities in the Borough Surveyor's Department with suggestions for an Audit Department. Arising out of the above-named report one naturally asks how a re-occurrence Can be prevented. I have given the matter considerable thought and would sug- gest that all stock and stores accounts of the Highway Department be kept in my office, that the stock-taking be done jointly by a representative of the department concerned, and one or more of my staff ; that time sheets be introduced upon which should be given the time worked ; where, and nature of the work; that a requisition should be sent to the storekeeper for materials to be used on the several jobs, and a strict record kept of the returns ; also that the work should be certified as having been done by one of the office staff.—Many of the improvements named in the fore- going paragraph could be made to apply to the other departments which have stock and stores, and a niodifl- cation of it could, in accordance with your committee's expressed wish, be made to apply to the Gas Depart- ment.—Members of my staff have always audited the other departmental books where any cash is received, the Markets, Cemetery, Police Office, Tramways, all in fact except the Gas Works, and as these departments have grown considerably it is impossible under the present arrangements either to do justice to the work or to amplify the check as above indicated. Our financial operations are now so considerable, and the departments dealing in some form or other with cash are so numerous that I feel it is essential that I should be in a position to control and audit all the financial details of the Corporation in a way which I have found impossible up to the present. The recent irregularities are such as would not be discovered by any superficial system of audit nor by the usual examina- tion of the Elective Auditors, for as you will have gathered the entries were not brought into the financial accounts but (when they have been recorded at all) were kept within the departmental storekeeper's books. I suggest that your Committee should give , me authority to make such alterations and additions kto my staff as would enable me to arrangla I for an Audit Department. The Audit Clerk or Clerks would be empowered to report all matters arising out of his investigations which in his opinion calls for notice, and he would suggest the introduction or alteration of such books of account in use in any department as may be necessary to maintain the efficiency of the audit. Any items of importance I should in due course lay before you or the Committee more directly concerned. I propose with your sanction to appoint Mr. Cannel], one of my clerks, to be the Audit Clerk. He has had the necessary training, and has all the qualifications for the office. Considering the responsibility of the office I would propose that his salary be increased. I would then re-arrange the general work of my department so that he could be set free to perform these special duties. By the suggestion to take over the Stocks and Stores Accounts of the different departments it will save a clerk being appointed in the place of Mr. Brierley, and may eventually release one from some of the other departments.</p>
        <p>(Signed) JOHN FAIRHURST.</p>
        <p>THE ASSISTANT MEDICAL OFFICE.</p>
        <p>The sub-committee appointed to consider the applica- tions received for the position of Assistant Medical Officer of Health reported that in their opinion two Assistant Medical Officers were unnecessary, and that the work of school inspection could be performed by the Resident Medical Officer at the Aikin-street Hospital, whilst the Medical Officer would take charge of the infectious cases, and that they recommended thst the appointment should be again advertised, and that the salary be incre,tsed to the sum of £l5O per annum. Resolved, that the Education Committee be requested to confer with this committee on the matter, and that the Town Clerk arrange an early meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MARKET GATE WIDENING.</head>
        <p>MARKET GATE WIDENING.</p>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage Committee has resolved that Bridge-street, as widened to larket Gate,be paved with setts and the widened portion of Market Gate with Karri Wood Blocks.</p>
        <p>The tender of Messrs. .fillars, Karri, and Jarrah Co., for 40,000 Kerni wood blocks was accepted on April 27th.</p>
        <p>ASSISTANT MARREN INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>Forty-one applications for the appointment of Assistant Markets Inspector and Inspector of Weights and Measures were laid before the Markets Committee. Resloved that the consideration thereof be deferred and that the Town Clerk be requested to re-advertise for local applicants who are prepared to pass the Board of Trade examination within six in ouths of appointment.</p>
        <p>TETE TERRITORIAL FORCES: - A letter was read from Lord Stanley asking the Corporation to grant facilities to enable members of the official staff and workmen who belong to the Territorial Forces to attend camp. The consideration of the matter was deferred to the not meeting of the General Purposes Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>There is an attractive show at the Hippodrome this week and large audiences have been the rule. Miss Morrie Forde, the famous chorus singer, is making her first visit to Warrington and a successful debut it is. Her songs have fairly captivated the crowd, and nothing more tuneful or amusing has been heard in Wareington for some time than " Girls study your Cookery Book," end" People say Susannah's got money." Florrie Fordo is, moreover, a superbly gowned lady and this adds to her attractiveness, especially in the eyes of the fair sex who usually patronise the Hippodrome so largely. Another exceptionally good turn is that by Tom Grave, a Lanca- shire lad who leads the way in " looping "sensations on a cycle. Tom Grave pedals round the inside of a huge revolving illuminated wheel, and when he finally Foes over the dead centre head-downwards the spectacle is thrilling. There is no " fake "about this show. It is one essentially of skill and nerve. The final oppor- tunity of seeiL.g it is to-night at 7 and 9 p.m.</p>
        <p>CONTINUOUS NEGLECT OF THE EYES is the most fruittul source of serious eye trouble. If you cannot read the small print in the newspaper ONE EYE AT a TIME 12 inchesaway not further,JOHNSTON WATSON, EYESIGHT SPECIALIST, should he con- sulted at once.-8 I Bridge St., Warrington. Low Charges. Satisfaction Guaranteed.--Advt.</p>
      </div>
