      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LOCAL WILL.</p>
        <p>The will of the late Mr. William Fisher, of Churchfield House, Halton-road, Runcorn, Cheshire, has been proved at £22,918. RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, when there were present :—Messrs. Pilling, Wright, Baxter, Spruce, Roylance, Morris, Collins, Antwiss, Woodward, Chad- wick, Bolshaw, flaming, C. E. Linaker, jun., Bolton W. Harrison, Mynekyme, Booth, Jones, Whitlow, Maudsley, Broome, Mounfield, Talbot, Alderman Norman, G. F. Ashton (clerk), J. Farrington (nuisance inspector), J. Diggle and W. Swinton (surveyors). Messrs. Pilling and Wright were re-appointed chairman and vice-chairman respectively. Mr. Reginald Potts, clerk to the Cheshire County Council, wrote saying he was desired to ask tke Rural Council to submit the name of a gentleman acquainted with the needs of the rural district and who would be prepared to serve upon the Small Holdings and Allot- ments Committee if appointed. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Barter moved at Mr. Woodward be the gentle- man to represent the Council, which was unanimously agreed to. .</p>
        <p>-The whole of the committees were re-appointed with the exception of the Highways Committee to which Mr. Bolshaw's name was added. Mr: Booth was appointed in the place of Mr. Bell on the Isolation Hospital Committee.</p>
        <p>Council passed a formal resolution agreeing to pay £25 per annum towards the salary and expenses of , an additional inspector for Widnes under section 14 of the Alkali &amp;c. Works Regulation Act 1906. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The fortnightly meeting of this Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday when there were present :--Messrs. Baxter, Spruce, Roylance, Morris, Collins, Antwiss, Woodward, Chadwick, Bolshaw, Haining, C. E. Linaker, jun., Pilling, Wright, Bolton,W. Harrison, Mynekyme, E. Warburton, Booth, Jones, , Whitlow, Maudsley, Broome, Mounfield, Talbot, Alder- , man Norman, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E Hough (deputy clerk), W. R. DePrez, T. Cooke and J. Pearson (relieving officers), and T. Rollinson (master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF CHAIRMAN AND Vs ICE-CFIAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF CHAIRMAN AND Vs ICE-CFIAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward moved that Mr. T. Baxter be re-appointed chairman of the Board for the ensuing year. Mr. Wright seconded and the resolution was carried unanimously. The Chairman proposed that Mr. Spruce be ire-appointed vice-chairman. Mr. Jones seconded and this was also unanimously carried.</p>
        <p>The list of attendances then came up for considera- tion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Morris said that ?Ir. Bell had been struck off the Board he having put in only nine attendances. Mr. Houghton who had made only four was still on the Board. If Mr. Bell was struck off, why not Mr. Houghton? The Clerk : That is for the Board to decide. As far as Mr. Houghton is concerned, he has not been absent fora period of six months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF COMMITTEES.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce moved that the whole of the Assessment Committee be re-appointed. Mr. Collins moved that Mr. Morris's name be added to that committee. Mr. Morris said he did not desire to stand.</p>
        <p>Mr. Haining said that Runcorn was not sufficiently represented on the committee, and he seconded Mr. Collins' resolution.</p>
        <p>Owing to Mr. Moms not desiring to go on that com- mittee, Mr. Collins withdrew the resolution.</p>
        <p>The ;attendances of the members of the Assessment Committee were read, and are es follows :--Messrs. Chadwick, 9 ; Bolshaw, 10 ; Taylor, 4 ; Beater, 12 ; Linaker, 10 ; Woodward; ; Jones, 5 ; Norman, 7 ; Wright, 13 f • Harrison, 9 ; and Antwis 10.</p>
        <p>„ The committee was re-appointed. The Relief Supervision Committee was re-elected. . . .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Boston was added to the Ladies' VisTtii-i.g-a.om- mittee, the rest of the committee being re-appointod. The Parliamentary Committee was re-appointed.</p>
        <p>THE WINE CELLAR.. Mr. Woodward said a rather alarming statement had been made with regard to the wine cellar, and the exact state ought to be ascertained. The Clerk said the master reported that the stores were entirely empty. (Laughter.) There was not a single bottle of any kind of intoxicating liquor in the stores. (Laughter). Mr. Antwiss : Somebody has been having a good time. (Laughter). Mr. Morris: The wines are not kept in the cellar, but on the grqund floor. I have inspected them myself. Mr. Wright : Have you sampled them ? (Loud laughter.) Mr. Nlorris : No I have not.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mynekyrue : When I was there it was more like a millionaire's wine cellar. (Laughter.) The Clerk (after consultation wtih the newly- appointed master.)—The master has misunderstood what stores you referred to. He has gone to ascertain the exact amount. (Laughter). The Master now reported that there were 5 bottles of brandy, 4 bottles of wine, 21 bottles of gin, and oue , gallon of whisky. r Alderman Norman : Wtio was it made that libellous statement? (Laughter.) . - . _ . -</p>
        <p>, o Mr. Myuekyne : What I have said I have said. (Laughter.) When I was there it was simply full of spirits. That is about nine months ago. Alderman Norman : I move that the Clerk issue a writ at once.</p>
        <p>• The incident closed amid great laughter. Messrs. Baxter and Spruce were appointed representa- tives (An the Association of Poor Law Unions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WARRINGTON PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of this Society was held at the headquarters, the Old Academy, on Tuesday even- ing. Mr. Smithson in the chair. The TREASURER, gr. Garside, read the statement of accounts which showed that the subscriptions amounted to ET 159. They had a balance in band of .£l. Cs. 10d. The expense of the lectures amounted to £1 7s. 7d. and they owed £6 17s. Sd. The accounts were adopted. Mr. GARSIDE moved that the Society be simply carried on as far as it can be with the use of the read- ing and the dark rooms only for the present. Mr. PODMORE seconded, and the resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>Mr. SMITHSON said Mr. Taylor had acted as president of the society for two years, and if Mr. Taylor would again accept the office he would propose him. Mr. Taylor declined in stand, and moved that Mr. ' Brown be appointed president. Mr. GARSIDE seconded and the resolution was carried. The following were elected vice-presidents : Messrs. Hardintr, senior, A. Bennett, E. .1. Flail, F. V. L. Mathias, W. Winstanley, P. Dalton, J. Fairhurst and F. G. Taylcir. The following were appointed the Committee : Messrs. Halloway, Davies, Bishop, Smithson, Dolan, Podmore, Mrs. Lowe, and Miss Harding ; Mr. Owen was appointed lanternist • Mr. Taylor, auditor ; Mr. Garside, treasurer ; and Mr. Smithson secretary (pro tem.) The CHAIRMAN said that in order to stimulate the work of the Society Mr. Taylor had presented for the president's competition, a guinea, which had been won by Mr. 11 Bond. The committee had also presented two bronze placques which had been won by Messrs. Podmoro and Bishop. Mr. Taylor then presented the prizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN PRIZE OP:MR.—The above choir held their third annual concert on Thursday evening in the Wesleyan schoolreem, Mr. W. H. Jenkins presiding over a large attendance. The artistes engaged were Misses L. Fair- hurst, E. Jones, 1. Miller ; Messrs. H. Hughes, H. Plumpton, R. Miller and Master Watts violin soloist, and Mr. E. Summers humorist ; Miss Miller and Miss Watts. The choir under the able conductorship of Mr. F. E. Penketh, sustained their reputation by rendering several pieces irt fine style. Encores were numerous during the evening, and Miss Fairhurst's interpretation of " Annie Laurie " was a great success, 11 the well deserved recall being responded to with" Home Sweet Home,"given with beautiful taste and expression. Mr. H. Hughes' rendering of "Star of Bethlehem" met with approval and was encored. He has a rich tenor voice and bids fair to become a very successful singer. Mr. ' H. Plumpton possesses a good bass voice, and his sing- ing of " Four jolly sailors " was loudly encored. In response be gave A friar of orders grey." Miss M. Miller was recalled for her rendering of " Life's 1 Lullaby." Mr. R. Miller sang " Asleep in the deep " I with good effect. The quartette "In absence," by 11. Williams, H. Cliffe, G. Gould and A. E. Penketh was well rendered, and Master Watts was responsible for two cleverly executed violin solos. This young violinist is to be congratulated open his playing, which was so much appreciated that an encore was accorded. The humorist (Mr. H. Sumners) kept the audience in great merriment, and was recalled for each of his items. Miss E. Miller was an able accompanist.</p>
        <p>At the fluncorn Petty Sessions, on ) ' r Monday' before Messrs. .1 mes Pritchard (in the chair) John at 1] Littler, Percy Ely Parr, Tyldesley, was summoned for hAving driven a motor car without a iicence in Church- street, Runcorn, on April 18th. He pleaded guilty.— Sergeant Allonby said he saw the defendant, who was accompanied by a lady, drive a motor car along Church- street on the morning of the date mentioned. He i stopped the car at Messrs. Lea's motor works in Nelson- street. Witness asked him to produce his driver's Warrington Philomathic Society, licence, and he produced one which on examination The last ordinary meeting of the session was held i Proved to have expired on the 14th. Witness pointed last Wednesday in Gregory's Cafe, the Vice-president i this out to him, and he replied that he was quite (Mr. J. H. Mason); delivering his address. His i unaware that. th.-' licence had expired and he would renew it at once.—Defendant said it was usual to have subject was" The Logic of Pessimism," based upon . a notice of the expiration of the licence. He did not the novel " Ships that pass in the L;ght " After receive one, and the matter of renewal had been over- giving an epitome of the book, Mr. Mason described looked. It was a pure mistake. He bad not been asked his idea of the pessimist and btrove to demonstrate the ,to show his licence for nine month.—The Chairman . .. _ _ -.7cled as a serious one. The</p>
        <p>, _ ...... overwhelming advantages of optimism. A discussion said the offence was regarcie. i 'lowed in which the advantages of pessimism were maximum penalty was £2o.—Stiperintendent Ennion not overlooked_ A vote of thanks to the Vice-president said defendant had been convicted three times forbicycle offences.—A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed. or his address was passed with heartiness. i</p>
      </div>
