      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF FATHER LENNON.</p>
        <p>Newton people will learn with regret of the death of the Rev. Father Lennon, which tool place at his resi- dence, Upper Parliament-street, Liverpool, last week. The deceased was for about 20 years priest at SS. Mary's and John's, Crow-lane,Newton-le-Willows.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EX-CRICKETER'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>EX-CRICKETER'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Saturday morning, afters. lingering illness, of Harry Storer, formerly a member of Newton-le-Willows cricket team, at his residence, Ripley, Derbyshire, whence he went after leaving Liverpool. Whilst his demise was not unexpected, it has come as a blow upon his Lancashire football and cricket friends. Storer at one time was goalkeeper to the Liverpool club. He was the brother of the Derby- shire county cricketer, Mr. Williana Storer. The funeral took place on Wednesday at Ripley. SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. On Sunday at the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church, the Sunday School Anniversary was celebrated. The Rev. W. Spedding, ef Birkenhead was the preacher. In the afternoon, a cantata, entitled The Galilean," ! was rendered by the children and choir, under the con- ductorship of Mr. G. Edmands. The soloists were Mies Woods (soprano), Miss Stronach (contralto), Messrs. J. Parr and J. Eacbus (tenors). and A. Pendry, E. Mullins and A. Grissold (bass). Thero,was a quartette by the Misses Stronach and Dickens, and Messrs. lEachus and Grissold, and a chorus consisted of the I Misses Creed, Sproston,, Dickens and Leyland, and Messrs. Sproston, Baxter, Guilford and Drummond. The accompanists were Mrs. 3. Baxter and Mr. R. A. Robinson. The day's collections amounted to £52 13s. qd. MR. SEDDON, M.P., AT DOUGLAS. SUNDAY LECTURE ON SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., for Newton Division, on Sunday night lectured in the Grand Theatre, Douglas, on Socialism and Christianity. This was the first meeting of the kind held on a Sunday in the Isle of Man. Mr. Seddon received a most attentive hearing, ' and his speech was frequently applauded. He said that ! the island was an ideal place where Socialistic ideals might be put into practice. He referred to Mr. Hall Caine's declaration in favour of the nationalisation of the land, but added that Mr. Caine was the only man in Manxland on the road to becoming a millionaire. On Monday night Mr. Seddon lectured on Socialism and the labour question. NEWTON CONSERVATIVES.</p>
        <p>MR. ERNEST PILKINGTON ASKED TO STAND. At the annual meeting of Newton Division Conserva- tive Association on Saturday, Lord Newton made an important announcement respecting the selection of a prospective candidate for the division in place of the late Colonel Richard Pilkington' C.B. He said the names of Mr. Robert Stone and Mr. Ernest Pilkington had been brought before the Council, but Mr. Stone having declined to accept the invitation it had been decided to ask Mr. Ernest Pilkington, the eldest son of the late Coigne' Richard Pilkington, to become the prospective candidate. Mr. Pilkington is at present abroad, and a reply to the invitation has not yet been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NEWTON BETTING CASE.</head>
        <p>NEWTON BETTING CASE.</p>
        <p>At Newton-le-Willows, on Thursday, the magistrates had before them the first case under the Street Betting Act, in which Frank Dale, described as a bookmaker, was charged with loitering in Earle-street, Earlestown. The evidence was to the effect that defendant was seen to pay money to eight men whilst standing in the porch of the Griffin Hotel.—Sergeant Barrow denied I that the police were rushing the case to keep their coat clean in consequence of complaints having been made at public meetings that they were not doing their duty I with the bookmakers.—Mr. Knowles, who defended, asserted that the opinion had been expressed that unless people in Earlf•st.,wu became Non-conformists and vegetarians they were in danger of being exterminated. —Mr.Doots,r,ler. ;Le prosecution,denied the suggestion that the poise ha SCCss induenced by outside opinion.— Mr. Doots ze,ked that in view of previous convictions a fine of .2:10 he imposed. hut Mr. Knowles argued that convictions under the old Act could not be taken into consideratien —The Bench overruled that objection and fined Dale £l5 and costs. Notice of appeal was refused. —Afterwalds, Nlatthew W. Sethi( seas charged with aiding and a:)et: ing Dale. Seton was described as a bookmaker, and the evi'lence was that he called out to Dale on the ,therelbents of the police being discovered. Seton was also seen banding tsonar over, and later, I when spokes to, remarked tilt Dale could not have paid , more than three bets, and that lie (defendant) had only ' changed a cheque + for a man named Fitzgerald. It was suggested by Cite prroee!trin that Dale was in the employ of Seton.—The Bench lined defendant .215 and costs.—George Harper, of Newttm-le-Willows,a member of the Sunderland football team as het) chant pious, and Wolverhampton Wanderers, was fined 25 and £2 7s. costs for street betting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS HEALTH REPORT. The St. Helens death-rate for the past three weeks is reported to have been 141, against 184. There have only been two deaths from infectious disease, against i4swent., -ore last year. The birth-rate- is 291, against . 1. Scarlet fever is still prevalent, twenty-six new es h..ving been reported, and ten of diptheria. A BANK ON THE CANAL BANK. John Jones, an elderly hawker, of 19, Canal-bank, who had been :emended owing to his having made a reqw‘st to be tried at the quarter sessions, was brought before the St. Helena magistrates on Saturday. Jones was found ,;arrying coal from the siding of the London and North-Western Railway Company at Pocket Nook at :1.45 the previous morning. When Constable Lomas was taking him past his house he asked to be allowed to search for something. He removed some ashes at the bottom of a post in the e.inal fencing' and withdrew a bag, which wu, found to c IW.Rin 31s. 2:1. Flo said this was the i3jace white he tw,a kept Ws motley for safety. Jot-1.1.s I,lsW o,2reel to N.+ tht..it with summarily. He had been convicted in January for a similar offence, and was sent to prison for twenty-eight days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS HOSPITAL. SUGGO,STED FUUTEIER, ENI..IIWEAIENE</p>
        <p>The Mayor of St. Heletzg, Alderman H. Martin, pre- sided on Wednesday at the thirty-tlfth annual meeting of the committee and subscribers of the St. Helens Hospital. The committee reported that. the income for the year amounted to £4,813, and the expenditure to £1,603, leaving a balance of £207. The amount brought forward from last year was £BO, giving a total balance of £287. The number of patients admitted during the year was 841. and the number of days in hospital 28,581, as compared with 829 and 30,173 in 1906 and 707 and 27,877 in 1905 respectively. The Committee recorded their deep regret at the loss the hospital had sustained in the death of Colonel Richard Pilkington, C. 8., V.D.</p>
        <p>_ The Mayor moved the adoption of he 'report and balance sheet, which, he said, were most satisfactory. They had now cleared off the debt on the enlargement, and had a balance in hand of £287, but the committee were providing, and had already taken the necessary steps to provide, X-ray apparatus and a dark TOM, which would absorb a considerable amount of the balance. Another satisfactory feature was that the gentlemen in the town who, for the third time, came forward and became guarantors to the bank for the enlargement overdraft, had been relieved of their liabilities. The work of Dr. Jones, the eye and ear specialist, had been greatly appreciated by the towns- people. The penny-a-week subscribers must be thanked most warmly for the manner in which they bad expanded and extended the fund in various directions. The great benefit tbat the hospital conferred on the workingmen of the town was the result of the generous way in which the people themselves contributed, and , the patients and members of the committee were i all grateful to Miss Oates, the matron, and her staff for the splendid work they did. He read a letter from Mr. , Austin Pilkington, the chairman of the hospital, who ! had been away ill for some months, and expressed the gratification of the committee to know that Mr. Pilk- ington was improving in .health. They hoped soon to see him again at the hospital meetings.—Mr. T. Killey, J.P., seconded. The Mayor remarked that frequently the 100 i eds of the hospital had all been full during the year, and the committee would soon have to consider the advisability of erecting the additional wards which were provided for on the original plans, and by which the accommoda- tion would be brought up to 158 beds. The report was adopted. Votes of thanks to Mr. A. J. Speeder), hon. treasurer, and the Mayor closed the proceedings.</p>
        <p>MOTORIST FINED AT RUNCORN.</p>
      </div>
