      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SHIELD FINAL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " BEATEN AT WIGAN. This match aroused great interest in Wigan, while about 100 spectators aecompanied the Warrington team. There was a fine crowd of 8,000, and at the lower rates for admission the receipts were £l3O. The sides included several notable first team players who had qualified for the match through not participating in a Lancashire Cup tie. Wigan, by the aid of strong forward play, soon had the oval hovering in the vicinity of the Warrington goal, and alter Todd had made a great effort to get through, Jordan, after mulling, effected a smart recovery and forced matters at half- way. Warrington were hopeless in coping with a fine movement of the Wigan backs and Prescott completed a good try. Prescott also took the kick at goal and made a splendid effort, the ball striking the cross-bar. Wigan continued to attack, and Weave, Todd and Greenwood participated in another Passing bout. Jordan checked the movement, and then Fish received and was getting nicely away when Todd just managed to push him into touch. Fish next took operations to the Wigan line. Landor was penalized for obstruction and from the subsequent kick Prescott landed a brilliant goal. Todd again broke away, and, Miller, beating Jordan, grounded behind the posts for Prescott to annex the goal points. Greenwood added another try for Wigan. Couthard missed kicking a goal right in front of the post. Half-time : Wigan A," 13 points ; Warrington "A," nothing. Resuming, Wigan soon increased their lead. Todd followed up an initial kick, and Jordan failing to clear, Collier received the ball. He passed to Wilcock, who ran well in before he parted to Greenwood, the latter running over near the corner flag. Prescott took the kick at goal but sent the ball wide. Todd added a fifth try. In the later stages Warrington showed improved form and after Fish had shown up well Hewitt and Lawlor scored tries but neither were improved upon. Final : Wig' 1,19 pota Warrington, six points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WORKS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE WORKS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S v. L. AND N.-W RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>This semi-final for the Rugby Works Competition was played at the Wilderspool ground on Monday evening when there was a large attendance. The railway men won the toss and at first gained ground considerably. Crosfield's ran down splendidly and had it not been for Houghton the Loco's back, Crosfield's would no doubt have scored. Bradshaw (Crosfleld's) ran swiftly down the field and scored but was pulled up for a breach of the orf-side rule. Stokes afterwards scored for Cros- Lythe converting. Bradshaw broke away in brilliant style, hut when he had only the full back to pass the ball slipped from his fingers. The latter player afterwards shot at goal from the penalty area hut the ball went wide bringing half-time with the score : Crosfield's 5, L. and N.-W. 0. On resuming the Loco's broke away and after a keen struggle near the line had a penalty awarded them, Roberts kicking a splendid goal. Elwell afterwards scored a try for Crosfield's. Result : Crosfiefd's two tries,one goal (eight points) ; L. &amp; N.-W. one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>RICHNIONDS v. RYLANDS'.</p>
        <p>Inis match Wa9 played on the Vcrilderspool ground on Wednesday, when there was a large and enthusiastic attendance. Richmonds won the toss and Allen ran down the field, but just before reaching the line he was floored. Atherton (Richmonds), afterwards prevented Rylends' from scoring in splendid fashion but Floyd gave his side the lead by kicking a penalty goal. Rylands were again awarded a penalty kick which Floyd again took advantage of. Richmonds now attacked but Bylands' seemed to have all their own way, Gibbs scor- ing a splendid goal : Half-time score : Rylands three goals (six points), Richmonds nil. The second half was a very exciting one and several of the Richmonds players were hurt. Richmonds gained possession and ran down the field uith good passing and Doyley would have scored had he not slipped in touch. The hall was soon at the other end where Jackson dribbled through Richmonds hacks and scored, but Gibbs failed to convert. Result : Rylands' three goals one try (nine points). Richmonds nothing. ne " final " between Rylands' and Crosflelds' will be played onthe Wilderspool enclosure to-day, kic"k-off at 3.30</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION WORKS FINAL.</p>
        <p>The concluding stage for the Watrington F. A. Works Competition Medsjs was decided on Thursday evening oh the Latchford ground. Richmonds. by beating the " Guardian" Office, end Robertsons. by getting the better of Fletcbers, qualified far the final. In the first half the teams were evenly matched, and but for the good display of both custodians, a heavy score must have been recorded, but neither goal fell. On re-starting play was very keenly fought out, w ith Richmonds the better team, and it was evident that "Steel "could not hold out against the hot attack of the" Stoves," and a score came from a penalty. Having gained the lead, Richmonds played grand football, and further increased their score, finally winning by two goals to none. The medals were afterwards presented to vOoners end runners-up by Councillor Fulton-Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>HER 76th APPEARANCE.</head>
        <p>HER 76th APPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>Jane Johnson, Pemberton's-yard a well-known character,was charged at the Borough Court on Tuesday, with having been drunk and disorderly lu Winwick-road at 9.10 on Monday night and with breaking four panes of glass in the dwelling-house of Michael Grant, doing damage to the amount of 3s. She was further charged with assaulting Thomas Burke, Pemberton's-yard, on Saturday, and with wilfully breaking six panr^ of glass in the dwelling house of Thomas Burke, doing damage to the extent of 2s. She pleaded guilty to the first three charges and not guilty to the fourth.—P.C. Hayes said that at 9.10 on Monday night he was on duty in Silver-street when he heard a smrsh of glass in the vicinity of Winwick-road. He proceeded there snd saw prisoner, who was very drunk, break a window in the dwelling-house of Michael Grant. She was using very bad language and he took her irtto custody and locked her up.— Thomas Burke said that at about six o'clock on Saturday night prisoner came to his house in a very excited condition. She got hold of his wife by the hair and struck witness across the head with a chisel.— Mrs. Burke said prisoner threw a teapot full of boiling tea through the window..nd also a bucket and a vase.— Prisoner said she might as well say she was guilty, because they would swear she was. She struck Burke in self-defence, but she did not use the chisel.—The Chief Constable remarked that prisoner bad been before the Court 75 times for various offences.—Prisoner was committed to gaol for one month with hard labour for , the drunkenness,and two months for the wilful damage, and was ordered to find two sureties of .£lO each and one of EN herself for her good behaviour for three months, or in default go to gaol for a further three months.—As prisoner was being removed she shouted that she would" do twelve months for them" (meaning the witnesses) when she came out —Prisoner was brought back, and the Chairman (Mr. J. Rymer Young) said that in view of her extraordinary conduct she would have to go to gaol for six months instead of three in I default of findinr, snreties.—Prisoner : " I can do it, I am not feared of that."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WIFE'S 20 YEARS' SICKNESS.</head>
        <p>A WIFE'S 20 YEARS' SICKNESS.</p>
        <p>Indigestion, Dizziness, and Sleeplessness Cured by Chas. Forde's Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>Sensational proof of the great power of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans is fui oished by the case of Mrs. Louis Maple, of 196, York Road, Bedminster, Ibistol, whom they rescued from a life-long illness. " For quite 20 years" says Mrs. L. Maple, "since I was a little girl, I had been a mai yr to biliousness, indiges- tion, and lassitude. I never could keep the whole of my food down, nor had I the energy and brightness I ought to have had. Always tired and langa;d, yet I never seemed able to get refreshing sleep. Four to five years ago I tocame very much worse, and had frightful pains in the back added to my other ailments. Dizziness and faintness, and a feeling of sickness used to come over me in the street. I went very thin and fegle. I had medical adi,ice, of course, and many remedies were prescribed for me, but all proved useles.s. At last I tiled Chas. Forde's Bile Beans with excellent result.</p>
        <p>"It seems the latest development of my illness was ulceration of the stomach. I was then so constantly in great pain, I was wealy of my elistence and so tired of medicines that it was with difficulty my friends persuaded me to try Chas. Forde's Bile Beans. Though I was prejudjced against them Chas. Fordes's Bile Beans effected a marvellous complete cure, and I now enjoy health I had never dreamed of."</p>
        <p>What Chas. Forde's Bile Beans did for Mrs. Maple they can do for you Chas. Forde's Bile Beans are the lasting cure for indigestion, constipation, and such ailments as amemia, debility, dizziness, biliousnesc liver troubles, piles, pimples, blood impurities, palpi- tation, sleeplessness, headaches, neuralgia, and net yousne3s. Take care you get the original world- famed CHAS. Formes Bile Beans, price is. 11,d. and 2s. 9d., of all chemists, or direct from the Bile Bean Mfg. Co., 45, Cow Cro• s Stn. -A, London, E.C. Beware of substitutes.</p>
        <p>Local Successes at Tyldesley Elsteddiod. Local competitors were vell to the fore at the amnia' , Eisteddfod held in connection with the Tyldesley Welsh Presbyterian Church on Saturday, the following successes being achieved : Afternoon —Pianofort solo (under 14 years of age): 1, May Evans (Widnes); 2,Preston Jones (Earlestown). Children's recitation : 1. I May Evans (Widnes) ; 2, Margeif Williams (Earles- I town). Quartette : Lyric Prize Quartette Party (Warrington). Tenor solo : Mr.A.Greennugh, Wart ;og- ; ton. Evening—Ladies' challenge solo, Olive Lavv,Leigh. I Baritone solo, Waltsr Shaw, Runcorn. Duet, Miss Fairhurst, Warrington, and Mr. W. Shaw, Runcorn. Chief choral competition, " The heavens are telling," Leigh Baptist Choir.</p>
