      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday May 2, 1908.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Mr. Wilmot Lunt. Mr. Wilmot Lunt, the well-known local artist, has again been successful in having his pictures hung both lat the Royal Academy and at the Royal Institute of Water Colour Painters of London. SIX GUINEAS will purchase a fully guaranteed PREMIER CYCLE. Inspection invited.—J. S. GAR- AND, 51,53, &amp; 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Infirmary and Dispensary. At a meeting of the Board of Management, under the presidency of Mr. W. Peter Rylands, Evelyn R. Tait, assistant matron of the West London Hospital, was unanimously appointed matron of the Warrington Institution. There were originally 71 candidates for the post. Miss Tait, who will enter upon her duties in about a month, will succeed Miss Watlr'n, who has officiated as matron here for many years. Appleton Rifle Club. The Range will open for Club Shooting tc day (Saturday) and will close on Saturday, 26th Septem- ber, on which day the Donegall Bronze Badge and thei Club Medal will be awarded. The Range will be closed to the Club on Saturday, 6th June, and Satur- day, 19th September. On all other Saturdays the Range will be open from 3to 5-30 p.m., and on all Thursdays from sto 7 p.m., except in September, when the time will be from 4.30 to 6.30. There are now 96 members of the club as against 80 at the end of last season, and 33 at the end of 1906. Warrington P.S.A. A well attended meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. I:aciety was held on Sunday in the Cc-operative Hall, mkey-atreet. Councillor Hawthorn, who presided, gave an address on P.S.A. work, strongly advising the young men present to live up to the true standard of purity and nobility of purpose. An address was delivered by Mr. W. Light on " The Christian life and its bearing on present day ambition and vain glory," Prayer was offered by the President. The lesson was read by Mr. Haywood. The rneml rs of the orchestra added much to the success of the meeting. Bricklayer's Eccentricity. Samuel Allen, bricklayer, was remanded for medical ins! ction on Thursday at Warrington, charged with I ing drunk and disorderly and breaking the door at the house of Frederick Richmond, Wincvick-road.— Richmond, who is an attendant at the Winnick Asylum, where prisoner was formerly a patient, said that Allen since his discharge frequently called at his house and charged him with detaining money Allen alleged he lent him. —Richmond said there was no truth in this.—The prisoner behaved strangely in the dock, continually talking and asking irrelevant ques- tions. Compensation for the Loss of a Son. At the County Court, on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, KG., Mr. Brooks (Messrs. Chapman and Brooks, solicitors, Manchester) applied on behalf of Mrs. Ernma .Ainsworth, NC.doly, Broom Edge, Lymm, for an award under the Workmen's Comp ansation Act, the respondent being Mrs. E. J. Kelsall, Agden Brow, Lymm.---Mr. Brooks statad that the sum of £5O had b- en paid into court. The applicant's son was killed on October 3rd last year whilst following his employ .nent with a threshing machine. He asked that the sum of £5 17s. should be immediately paid to the widow, who was the sole der 'ndent, and an llowance of £1 12s. a month.— His Honour made the order accordingly. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Adv t Labourer's Fatal Fall. On Monday, Walter Coleman, 6, Back Stameord- street, was following his employment as a labourer at the Mersey White Lead Works when he lost his I lance as he was cartying something up a ladder, and fell to the ground. His skull was fractured, and he was conveyed to the Infirmary, where he died at fon.: o'clock on Tuesday morning, in the afternoon Coroner Brighouse, after holding another inquest, opened the inquiry into the cause of the death, and after hearing evidence of identification adjourned for a week. He explained that he had to give 24 hours' notice to His Majesty' q Inspector before be could hold an inquest, but he wodld issue the certificate to enable the relations to bury the deceased. Mr. Steel appeared for the relatives. Cheshire Lines Platelayers' and Workmen's Provident Society. The thirty-third annual meeting of the Cheshire Lines Platelayers' and Workmen's Provident Society, which consists of the workmen employed in the engineers' department of the Cheshire Lines Railway, was held at the works, Battersby-lane, Warrington, on Tuesday. The chair was taken by Mr. Harry Blundell, M.1.C.E., the chief engineer of the Committee, and the meeting was largely attended by officials and delegates from Lancashire and Cheshire. The Scaretary sub- mitted the annual report, which stated that the number of members was 1,118. A large sum had been expended in aid of 321 sick members, who had received assistance from the funds of the Sc aiety, and nineteen death claims had also been yaid. The report and statement of accounts baying been passed, Mr. Thomas Platt was re-elected secretary for the ensuing year, and Messrs. George Walker and R. Jones were re-elected auditors. Bicycle Thefts at Warrington. • At the Borough-Police Court, on Thursday moining Frederick Forth, a shoemaker, formerly of Waaringten was charged with stealing a bicycle from the I on Hotel yard, on January 18, the property of John Hatton. A further charge of stealing another bicycle, the property of James Preece, of Chapel-street, St. Helens, was also preferred against him. The Cnief Constable said the prisoner was sent to gaol for two months for stealing a bicycle at Birkenhead, and he was only liberated on Mooday,when he was rt areestad in connaction with the preaent charges. In regard to the theft from the Lion Hotel yard, the prosecutor left his bicycle for a few minutes,and on his retina it had disapr eared. Inquiries were made by the police, and the robbecy was traced to the prisoner,who it appeared had sold the bicycle to a woman in Latchford for 30s. In a statement to the police the prisoner also admitted haaing stolen another bicycle from St. Helens. isoner pleaded for leniency. He said he had to blame betting and drink for his downfall, and he now saw the folly of it.—The magistrates sent him to gaol for three months. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt Broke the Crockery. James Quigley, Rose and Crown-street, was charged before the Warrington magistrates on Monday with breaking 66 plates and 66 basins, also with assaulting Charles William Jenks, and also with assaulting Frances Jenks on Saturday night —Charles William Jenks said 1-1 was manager of a lodging house in Rise and Ctown-street. About 11:15 on Saturday night the prisoner came into the hanse and booked a be The bed he generally slept in was let. He went into the kitchen and said he would throw the man who was in his usual bed out. He struck the witness several blows on the face and chest and also assaulted his wife and broke two of her teeth.—Prisoner: Did you hit me on the head with a tea pot ?—Witness : No, I did not.—Mrs. Jenks corroborated her husband's statement. He told her he wanted his 4d. back and she gave it to him.--The prisoner said he asked Mr. Jenks for his 4d. and le said he would not give it to him, so he (the prisoner) broke two basins the value of his money. Jenks struck him on the head cv:th a tea pot and so prisoner broke all the crockety up.— The Chief Constable said prisoner had lac en thirty times previously convicted and the prisoner was sent to gaol for four months' hard labour. TRY GILLETTE SAFETY RAZORS for comfort in shaving.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt. Ex-Soldier's Suicide at Seventy. Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at Warrington on Tuesday afternoon on the body of William Kenyon, aged 70, a fittm's labourer, who resided at 39, Oliver- street.—Jane Kenyon, widow of the deceased, said her husband after serving twelve years in the 103rd ' Regiment was discharged in 1872 as medically unfit. Ile was invalided home from India, where be served for a number of years. He had since received a pension of 7cl. a day. After leaving the Army he was a fitter's labourer up to four years ago, when he: had a slight illness, and he had not worked since. On Saturday night he arrived home at about ten o'clock and after chatting with witness and her daughter be went to bed. Witness followed at eleven o'cloFk. Later her daughter</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PROTECTIONIST TRIUMPHS.</head>
        <p>PROTECTIONIST TRIUMPHS.</p>
        <p>AND WHY THEY ARE NEVER MENTIONED. By L. G. Chiozza Money, M P.</p>
        <p>ARTICLE X.</p>
        <p>Our friends the Tariff Reformers have apparently heard of but two foreign countries, America and Germany. You heat from them very little about France, and nothing at all about Russia, and Spain, and Portugal. Yet France, Russia, Spain and Portugal are happy Protectionist countries. Russia, Spain and Portugal, indeed, may be termed shining lights of Protection. Why, then, do not the Tariff Reformers boast of the success achieved by those countries in the world of trade ? Calcit be that it is because they are conscious that there is no success to boast of ?</p>
        <p>Object Lesson from Spain</p>
        <p>Spain took to high Protection in 1892, and the results are well worth the attention of a people who are invited to consider the old (Racy of Protection a new gospel. The tariff of 1892 was framed after due consideration by a " Tariff Commission " of experts, who undertook to adjust the taxes to the country's need. They proved to 1 3 "whole hoggers." No little duties for them ; no milk and water ten per cent., but high minimum duties calculated to provide "work for all" by shutting out foreigo products. They placed heal,/ duties on coal and coke, on machinery for factories and upon machinery for agriculture. They followed the glorious example of America and taxed most raw materials. High duties were placed on cotton, hemp and jute. They raised the food duties, and the poor were mulcted by heavy taxes on flour, on petroleum, on jam, on fish, and on most other varieties of food. Yet I doubt not the gallant Spaniards who sat on the Commission thought they were doing well for their country. They were striking at foreign products, and could not see that they were I hitting their on people.</p>
        <p>Results of the Spanish Tariff.</p>
        <p>The New Tariff worked mai vale—of a sort. In 1891, the year before the Tar, Spanish commerce was valued at £7I,C 30,000. two years of the new Tariff it fell to X,55,000,C00. Here are the facts :</p>
        <p>SPANISH TRADE AND 1H 1592 TARIFF. (In Millions of £). Imports. Exports. 1891 1892 1893 IKI 1895 1896 1898 1900 1902 105</p>
        <p>Ice figures after 1896 were affected by the Cuban war and the war with the United States and this makes them less valuable for putpczes of comparison,but even in 1905!the latest year for which I have figures)Spanish exports only reached £23,000,000. So that in 1891. 1905 a period of 14 years, Spanish exports have grown by only 3:1,000,000, while if we take only the years 13fore the loss of Cuba,the figures are desperately bad. The figures relating to Spanish commerce under Pr( tection are veey much what ours were in the first fifty years of the ninet -nth century. The reader will now understand why we do not see much of Spain on Tariff Reform le e ts. 'The figures of Portugal are equally deprebsing.</p>
        <p>The ease of France.</p>
        <p>But, it may be said, the Spanish are a backward people and it is not fair to cn.ll them in evidence. Let us turn, then, to France, another country which has indulged in Protection and whose people are certainly not backward. Let us look at the French commercial record since 185:</p>
        <p>FRENCH COMMERCE 1875-1907. (Tn Millions of Z). Imports. Exports. 18ió l4l 155 1880 2Ol 139 1885 163 123 1810 177 150 1895 1900 1905 l9l 195 1900 1907 242 222 Britain and Franca enamored. Now let us compare this small growth with that of the Uo;t3d K'ngdom : GROWTH OF BRITISH AND FRENCH COMMERCF (1875-1907). (in 'Millions of 2. Yee British French Imports Expo, ,s. Impm ts Exports. 187 h 1907 -,11 ... 426 242 ... .722 Increato in 32 years. 238 ... 203 101 ... 67</p>
        <p>That is why we see so little of France on Taill Reform leaflets. Yet France has about the same pcpulatioa as ourselves and is a much fairer subject of compailson, therefore, than Germany with 60,000,000 people or America with 80,000,000 people. Not that we need fear comparison with any count;;, as I have sn'ficiently shown in these articles, but if we must have comparison, let us have all the cards on the table. If our growth had been like that or France, one could rderstand ilissatisfaction but in 'slew of the really wonde-ful results which Britain has to show, the position of the Protec- tionist becomes guile untenable. In 1875 our exports were about 50 per cent. grea;,er than those of France. In 1007 they were about .100 per cent. greater. DC3S it not suggest itself to the minds of Tariff Reformers that if Free Trade were in ti ath a policy of run such results could not be ach;eved ? The American Trade Slump' I have jest rxeived the complete American trade reluias for Febtuary from Washington (their publica. tion is always later than in our case), and they show remediable evidences of the American slump. Tariff Reformers are ma! *ng all they can of our own trade depression, and it is true that in March our imports of raw mateiials fell greatly. The American fall, however, is greater still. Here are the latest facts from the other side : AMERICAN IMPORTS OF RAW MATERIALS. Dollars. Febt nary, 1907 February, 1908</p>
        <p>--- Fall 18,595,216</p>
        <p>This gives some idea of the nature of the latest American slump, which led the world in trade depres- sion. We could have no more conclusive proof that Protection neither steadies trade nor guarantees employment.</p>
