      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday. The-e were present :—Mrs. Simpson, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Revs. T. R. Matthews and M. Ryan, Messrs. J. Billinge, E. L. Bent, W. Gill, J. Evans, G. Mather' P. Longton, T. Ritchie, W. Flannagau, W. Houghton, R. Bolton, W. Furness, W. C. Brooks, R. Forshaw, A. Brooks, J. C. Sutton (clerk), E. J. Sutton (actilg assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools). Pending the election of Chairman, the Clerk was elected to the chair, and he remarked that he had presided on similar occasions for more than forty years. Mr. HOUGHTON said he had no doubt as to the popularity of the proposal he had to make—that the Rev. T. R.Matthews be elected Chairman for the coming year. It needed no words of his to commend Mr. Matthews because they could not gild refined gold. They knew his value—particularly to Earlestown—as a member of the Board and he had been close in his attention to the duties of vice-chairman. Mr. Thu) siGE in seconding said he was glad to have the privilege of rendering honour to one to whom it was justly due. He had been associated in work with Mr. Matthews for many years and had found him to be a thorough Christian gentleman who was always anxious to be at work—in fact he had no such word as " can't" in his vocabulary. Mr. FLANNAGAN said he had always found Mr. Matthews to be courteous and able and he was sure he would uphold the dignity of the chair. The CLERK said he had known Mr. Matthews in con- nection with the business of the Board for nearly forty years and it gave him great pleasure to put the motion. The resolution WP9 carried with applause. The Rev. T. H. MATTHEWS said he had been I requested several times to undertake the office and he I only consented now under a kind of protest, because he felt there were those on the Board who were better calculated to do the work. His chief hobby was to try and do good. He thanked them most heartily for the honour they had done him and hoped they would have a happy and agreeable year of work. Mr. B,r,LisoE proposed the election of Mr. George Mather as vice-chairman and said that gentleman had taken a deep interest in the work of the Union, as was shown by the regularity of his attendances. Mr. A. BROOKS seconded. So far as seniority went, Mr. Mather had perhaps not full right to nomination but in regard to the work he had done there was no member whose claims were superior. Mr. Bent, Miss Broadbent and Mr. Houghton sup- ported the proposition which was heartily agreed to.</p>
        <p>- - - ELECTION OF COMMITTHES. WARRIgGTON'S REPRESENTATION ON THE ASSESSMENT _ _</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE. The next business was the election of committees. Some discussion arose as to the constitution of the Assessment Committee, Mr. W. C. Brooks contending that as Warrington had a population more than twice as large as that of the remainder of the Union and considerably more than half the assessable value, it was entitled to more than four representatives on a com- mittee of twelve. He moved that Warrington should have six representatives. Mr. FURNESS seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON said that if they followed Mr. Brooks's line they would soon have no bona fide agri- culturalists on the committee. As it was, the purely agricultural part of the district was not adequately represented. _</p>
        <p>S.fr. B) LUNGE said Warrington was always fully represented at the committee meetings. After some discussion the proposition that Warring- ton should have six members was defeated. The cora- mittc 3 was elected as follows :—Rev. T. R. Matthews, Messrs. Jolley, Billinge, A. Brooks, Furness, Bent, Davies, Evans, Forshaw, Gill, Houghton, Mather. Other comm4ttees were elected as follows : Asylums Visitation Committee: Mrs. Simpson, Mrs. Wilkinson Messrs. Bolton, Backhouse. W. C. Brooks, Cook, Evans, Flannagan, Gill, Ritchie, Seton, Longton and Furness.</p>
        <p>Ho-use Committee: Miss Broadbent, Mrs. Simpson, Mrs. Wilkinson, Revs. T. R. Matthews and M. Ryan, Messrs. Billinge, Bolton, A. Brooks, W. C. Brooks, Cook, Davies, Evans' Flannagan, Forshaw, Furness, Gill, Jolley, Longton,Mather, Seton, Bent. Cottage Homes Committee: Miss Broadbent, Mrs. Sirnixon, Mrs. Wilkinson, Revs. T. R. Matthews and M. Ryan, Messi.J. G. Mather' E. L. Bent, Backhouse, Ball, Bolton, • Cook, Davies, Evans, Forshaw, Flannagan. Furness, Gill, Houghton, Jolley, Ritchie, Stanier and Seton.</p>
        <p>Ladies' Visiting Committee : Mesdames Simpson, Wilkinson, James Fairolough, Rowson, Carruthers, Pier- point, Coßingwood, Frost, J. E. Wright, Liston and Furness and Miss Broadbent.</p>
        <p>Brabazon Ladies lie-;duf;mes Atkinson, Carruthers, Fleming, Ford, Frost, Furness, Smith, Venn, Misses Broadbent, Margaret Broadbent, Bolton, Appleton, Brown, M. Fairclough and E. Tunstall. Sites for Offices Committee: The whole Board.</p>
        <p>CONFERENCE REPRESENTATIVES. It was resolved that the Board be repreaented at the North-Western and Central Poor Law Conferences,when convened by the Conference Secretaries' such repre- sentatives to be the Chairman and Vice-Chairman of the Board (or as the Board may otherwise apkoint, after receipt from the Clerk of special notice of Atch Meet- ings) and the Clerk. The representation of the Board at the meetings of the Association of Poor Law Unions were then discussed the Clerk pointing out that Mr. Jolley and himself had for several years past been appointed the two repre- entatives, the work being largely of a continuous character.</p>
        <p>Mr. FURNOSS said it was not fair that one or two Guardians should monopolise all the knowledge to be gained at these conferences. All the members should have an opportunity of attending. Mr. A. BnooKs said they wanted as a representative somebody who was in touch with the whole of Poor Law work, because it was a matter in which continuity was essential.</p>
        <p>Eventually. Mr. Jolley and the Chairman were appointed the representatives.</p>
        <p>THE RA.TES. The CLERK submitted the precepts for the ensuing half-year involving a rate of 7d. That was the lowest figure he could fix upon—in fact he was afraid it ought to have been Bd.</p>
        <p>0 WTON ELECTION. The CLERK reported that the following candidates had been nominated to fill the vacancy at Newton caused by the death of Dr. Patterson :—Mrs. Maria Elizabeth Collingwood, Mere House, Newton-le- Willows; James Henry Forster, Houghton Arms, Houghton-street, Earlestown, licensed victualler ; Gabriel Moon, Cross-lane, Earlestown, auctioneer, valuer and estate agent.</p>
        <p>EXTRA REM EN.ORAIION WANTED. Applications from the master, superintendent and relieving officers for remuneration for extra work involved in the preparation of the relief register required by the Local Government Board were referred to a committee consisting of the chairman of the Board and the cha;rmen of the relief committses.</p>
        <p>Ow day a master in a school asked a bo to give him a sentence with the word seldom In. After a bit of thinking, he said, '" Our father had two fowls, sir, and he seldom (sell'd 'em)." CLIFFORD CROKER.</p>
        <p>Soene: Railway Station. Time: 1 p.m. Beautiful Blonde (to booking-clerk): "What time please, does the next train leave WA Barnley Junction?" Booking-Clerk: "1.50." Anxious Man (just behind beautiful blonde): "Excuse me, madam—" Beautiful Blonde (haughtily): "I believe I got here first, sir." Anxious Man: "But—" Booking-Clerk: "One to Darnley?" Beautiful Blonde: "Oh, dear me, no. I want to go to Slowcumbe. Now, they told me that the train—"</p>
        <p>Booking-Clerk: "That's right. Barnley. One to Slowcumbo?" Anxious Man: "Madam, I believe—" Beautiful Blonde: "But they told me if the train was late I might not be able to catch the other. The railway companies are so dread- fully independent, you know. And then, my mother told me—" Booking-Clerk: "That's all right. Train waits. Single?" Anxious Man: "Pardon me, but—" Beautiful Blonde (still ignoring him): "What is the fare, please?"</p>
        <p>Booking-ole-rk (impatiently): "Three-and- six. Do you want a ticket or not?" Anxious Man: "I say---" Beautiful Blonde: "Will I hav6 to wait long at—what's the name? Oh, dear. I'm so dread- fully forgetful. Oh, yes. Barnley Junction?" • Booking Clerk: "Forty minutes." (The bell sounds.) "Come, hurry up." Beautiful Blonde: " Gracious." (Hands him a sovereign and gets change and ticket.) "Oh, dear, I haven't a moment to lose." (Rushes out.; Booking-Clerk: "Where to?" Anxious Man: "I'm not going anywhere." Ticket-Clerk: "'Then what do you want?" Anxious Man wearily): "Nothing. I thought perhaps that woman wanted the um- brella she left in the 'bus."</p>
        <p>Change at</p>
        <p>A SERMON. WhatscrYz.,r you find to do, Do it, boys, with all your might; Never be a little true Or a little in the right. Trifles even Lead to heaven, Trifles make the life of man, So in all things, Great and small things, Be as thorough as you can. Let no speck of falsehood dim _</p>
        <p>Spotless truth and honour bright; Who will love and honour him That says any lie is white? He that falters, Twists or alters In his tale the slightest part, May deceive me, But, believe me, He will never win my heart. Help the weak if you are strong; Love the old if you are young; Own a fault if you are wrong; If you're angry, hold your tonguit. In each duty Lies a beauty, If your eyes you do not ehwt, Just as surely And securely As the kernel in the nut. If you think a Word will please, Speak it, if it be but true; Kindness you can show with ease, Though no deed is asked from you. Words may often Soothe and soften, Gild a joy and heal a pain; • They are treasures, Yielding pleasures, It is wicked to retain.</p>
        <p>Whatever thing you find to do, Do it, then, with all your might; Let your prayers be strong arid true, Prayer, my lads, will keep you right, So in all things, Great and small things, Be a Christian and a man; And for ever, Changing never, Be as thorough as you can. ELIZABETH BREARLEL</p>
