      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>GOOD HEALTH</head>
        <p>GOOD HEALTH</p>
        <p>REGAINED AND MAINTAINED BY THE</p>
        <p>USE OF DR. MORSE'S INDIAN ROOT</p>
        <p>PILLS.</p>
        <p>• The first essential of good health is to keep the Stomach in a sound condition. It is not the quantity of food we eat, but what we digest and assimilate, that nourishes the body. When the stomach and organs of digestion and nutrition are diseased, and the food eaten is only impel- perfectly digested, thete is loss of nutrition, and the body loses strength ES a natural consequence. Not only does the system suffer from lack of nourishment, but the derangement of the organs must cause further complications. Indigestion is a most prevalent cause of Constipation, which in its turn causes Biliousness and all its attendant evils.</p>
        <p>Every ailment is an effort of Nature to get rid of some impurity in the system and the object of medical treatment is to assist Nature in doing so. It is from the little irregularities of the system that the common ailments arise. Coming evils cast their shadows before, and Nature gives many indications of the approach of these com- plaints, which, if treated at once, may be warded. off. _ _</p>
        <p>For sale by all Chemists and Stores, price 1/11 per bottle, or 6 bottles for 6/6, or will be sent by mail post prepaid by the Proprietors, The W. H. Comstock Co., Ltd., 21, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.O A free sample sent on receipt of Id. stamp</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>HARNESS THEFTS.</head>
        <p>HARNESS THEFTS.</p>
        <p>Theft of Reins.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, John William Peplow, King-street, was charged with stealing from the stables of Mr. Edward Connor a pair of horse reins valued 225. The Chief Constable said that at ten minutes past four on Saturday, April 4th, prisoner was seen acting suspiciously in the Crown-street neighbourhood by P.C. Hughes. He went into Messrs. Connor's yard and returned with something bulky underneath his coat. When prisoner saw the constable he ran away and dropped the reins. Inquiries were made and the prisoner was arrested on Sunday. When charged he replied " Yes." Mr. Arthur Browne defended. He said the prisoner joined the Army three years ago and was in the Shropshire Regiment. He was receiving a pension of £2 is. per month. He went on the spree on the 4th of April and he took the reins which were of no use to him at all. He bore a previous good character and if the magistrates convicted, the prisoner would lose his pension. He suggested to the magistrates that they bound the prisoner over to appear for judgment when called upon. He had bnly one previous conviction which was for assault many years ago. The pensions were a curse._ _ _</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said they did not wish to press the case. If the prisoner was fined it would not affect his pension a bit. The case was dismissed on payment of the costs. Mr. Browne: I wish somebody else would draw the pensions and keep them for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>THEFT OF CURB CHAIN.</head>
        <p>THEFT OF CURB CHAIN.</p>
        <p>Joseph Dale was charged with stealing from a horse's head a curb chain on the same date, the property of William Russell.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that when the prisoner was arrested the police could not find out to whom the chain belonged, hut they afterwards found it belonged to Mr. Russell. The horse was in Crown-street at the time of the theft. Prisoner was fined 20s. including costs, or 14 days' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>"MY SON."</head>
        <p>"MY SON."</p>
        <p>A BRADFORD MAN TELLS, ON OATH, HOW HIS SON WAS CURED OF RHEUNIATISNI, AFTER MANY YEARS OF SUFFERING.</p>
        <p>(COPY.)</p>
        <p>1, WILLIAM STEVENSON, of 19 Kirkstone-street, Bradford, in the County of Yorks, do solemnly and sincerely declare as follows : My son, Ernest Stevenson. has for many years been a great sufferer from Rheumatism. Whenever he was seized with an attack of this coMplaint, he was forced to throw up his work and lay in bed, often remaining in bed for weeks at a time, and throughout these periods he constantly suffered the most intense pain and agony. When in this condition, it was impossible for him to move about, or to do anything to assist himself. He screamed like a child at the slightest movement, and even the act of anyone walking across the room in which he lay was sufficient to arouse the pain and cause him the most excruciating agony. On one of his more recent attacks he was laving in bed for about six weeks, utterly helpless, and although I obtained for him the best medical advice I could, yet he was never free from pain during the whole of this long time, and it seemed that he was likely to become a chronic invalid. I almost despaired of his ever becoming fully restored to health, and was content if the Doctors could patch him up for the time, in order to afford at least* little interval between the attacks. In the month of February, 1908, he was again stricken with this malady. I was at a complete loss to know what I could do or get in order to alleviate his suffer- ings, when my eyes providentially fell upon a newspaper paragraph in which Liquid Barkola Compound, used with certain other ingredients, was recommended as a cure for Ithenmatism. Although I had but little faith after the experiences I had had with regard to' other remedies and medical advice in the past that anything could have much effect, yet I procured a bottle of the prescription which I bad seen. It was made up as follows:— Liquid Barkola Compound 1 ounce. Tincture of Gentian 1 ounce. Syrup of Rhubarb 1 ounce. Syrup of Ginger 5 ounces. The first bottle gave my son immediate relief, and seemed to entirely remove the pain. I continued to . give him the preparation until he had taken three bottles, when all effects of his illness left him, and he seemed to have regained perfect health and to be as well as he had ever been. Several weeks have since elapsed, during which time my son has pursued his usual occupation without inconvenience of any hind, and he has continued in this satisfactory condition, and to-day feels as if every trace of the dreaded Rheumatism had left his system for ever. AND I MAKE this Solemn Declaration, conscienti- ously believing the same to be true,and by virtue of the Statutory Declarations Act, 1835. DECLARED at Bradford, in the County is 23rd da of Yorks ILLIAIVI STEVENSON. thy of .April, 1908, before me,</p>
        <p>- , B. BOTTONILEy, A Commissioner to Administer Oaths. N.B.—Any Chemist or Druggist in the world can supply :he medicine which cured ERNEST STEVENSON.</p>
      </div>
