      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday May 2, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday May 2, 1908.</p>
        <p>THE MOTH AND THE CANDLE.</p>
        <p>What causes a moth td fly into a candle? There are many theories on the subject. Pro- fessor Loeb sees in filo insect's flight candle- ward only a directive effect of light similar to that which causes the flower to turn to- wards the sun, while others ascribe the effect purely to the insect's curiosity. One authority notes that only flying and swim- ming creatures are thus affected, and he ex- plains this by reminding us that such crea- tures must rely for their guidance almost wholly on sight. Hence a bright light in the night attracts them, because it gives rise to the sole external impulse that can act upon them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>THE TRAILS OF METEORS.</p>
        <p>The visible trails left by meteors as they shoot across the sky have been investigated by Professor C. C. Trowbridge, who con- cludes that they are clouds of self-luminous gas combined with very minute particles of meteoric dust. As these trains are usually seen at heights of fifty to sixty miles seldom or never above sixty-five or below forty-five miles —it appears that their formation inust depend upon encountering a certain degree of atmo- spheric density or pressure. The _trains are often visible for ten to twenty minutes, and usually contain a volume of several cubic miles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NOVEL FUEL.</head>
        <p>A NOVEL FUEL.</p>
        <p>An interesting object-lesson in the utiliea- tion of waste is afforded by the new fuel manufactured at Los Angeles, California. It is claimed that this fuel makes neither smoke nOr smell, and that two bricks of it will last a day in a stove. The basis of the fuel is the waste of the peach and apricot canneries. The fruits are stoned, and the shells being broken up, the kernels are sent to France for the manufacture of prussic acid. The broken shells are then mixed with crude petroleum waste from oilfields, shavings from planing mills, and pulp from olive oil mills. This is compressed into bricks, and used as ordinary fuel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>IN THE, DEPTHS OF THE SEA.</head>
        <p>IN THE, DEPTHS OF THE SEA.</p>
        <p>As we rea lower and lower depths, the water becom colder; the warm water, being less dense, remains at the surface. At about 12,000 ft. the temperature is little above the 'freezing-point of fresh water. Light gradually disappears, and at 1,400 ft., says Country Life, absolute darkness prevails; and as no plant can live, without light, the vegetable kingdom is unrepresented, except by some boring algm which have been dredged from a depth of over 3,000 ft. Fur. ther, there are no currents, oxygen is scarce, and uniformity of temperature prevails.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>DEEP SEA FISHES.</head>
        <p>DEEP SEA FISHES.</p>
        <p>Professor Boulenger has given an account of some of these interesting denizens of the ocean in Country Life. Much of our know- ledge of these is due to tue Valdivia expedi- tion of 1898-9, of which the report was pub- lished in 1907. In addition to their often strange forms the most striking peculiarity of these fishes is the large size, or complete absence of the eyes, and the presence of luminous organs. The so-called red sea bream of the fishmongers' shops, notable for its large eyes, is a deep sea form. It is not a bream at all, but a sort of primitive perch, belonging to the genus Beryx, which was so abundant in Cretaceous times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MUSEUM BEETLE.</head>
        <p>THE MUSEUM BEETLE.</p>
        <p>The larva of the museum beetle (anthrenus museorum), of which some account is given by Mr. A. 3. Ewart in the Ohio Naturalist, has been doing terrible damage in the National Herbarium at Melbourne. It can only be kept in check by placing the port- folios of .plants in vapour of carbon pea. phide. A remarkable point about 'these beetle grubs is that they can feed on dry materials without any supply of water And yet they are found to contain the same per- centage of water as those which feed on moister food. It is suggested that the water may be chemical in origin, and formed from the oxygen and hydrogen in the food. The grubs do not seem to have the power of con- densing water from the air. Numerous bac- teria were found in the alimentary canals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>RADIOGRAPH TEST FOR DEATH.</head>
        <p>RADIOGRAPH TEST FOR DEATH.</p>
        <p>It appears, acoording to the experiments made by M. Valliant, head of the laboratory of radiography at the Larbaisiere Hospital, that when the radiographic photograph has been taken of the internal organs of a living subject the photograph gives a confused im- pression, - the organs being indicated solely by s, vague cloudiness. But when those of the corpse are radiographed the impression is very distinct, each organ being clearly de- fined. The reason given for the cloudiness of the radiograph of the living organs is that during life there always is enough movement during the time of exposure to blur the photograph. In the case of death the move- raent of the organs and the circulation of the blood have ceased.</p>
        <p>INSENSIBILITY BY BLUE LIGHT RAYS. Dr. Radard, a Geneva dentist, after hav- ing for several years made experiments with the narcotic effect of blue rays of light, has submitted his results to the Swiss Society of Odontology. In the report he claims that a complete narcosis (stupefying effect) can be obtained if the rays of a blue electric light are brought to bear on the human eye, while all other rays of light, particularly of day- light, are kept off of it. The narcosis thus obtained is so complete that, during the same, little dental operations, such as pulling or fill- ing teeth, &amp;c., can be executed without cans- ingt the patient the least amount of pain. Th‘le the effect of the blue rays is a very strong one, ,that of violet-blue and green rays is less intensive, and yellow or red rays shew no :effect at all. The inventor is unable to give 'a definition of the cause of this remark- able discovery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>SPECTRA OF "SHOOTING STARS."</head>
        <p>SPECTRA OF "SHOOTING STARS."</p>
        <p>The sudden and unexpected appearthrica and equally sudden disappearance of meteors make it almost impcssible to utilise the spec- troscope—which has told us so much of the chemical constitution of the sun and the in- finitely remote fixed stars—to discover what elements are present in " shooting-stars " no further away than the outer fringe of our atmosphere. M. Blakjo, of the Moscow Ob— servatory, htiwevert by a rare chance, has ob- tained the spectra of two exceptionally bright meteors one from the important radiant in Perseus an account of which appeltred in the Astrophysical Journal. In the Perseid meteor helium is the predominating element, the presence of thallium accounting for its green colour; while calcium, macznesium, and potassium are among the elements whose characteristic lines are recorded in the spec- trum of the other meteor.</p>
        <p>EYE-STRAIN AND HEADACHE.</p>
        <p>Basing himself on his records of nearly 1,300 eye examinations, Dr. S. W. S. Toms claims that 90 per cent, of all those suffering from reflex or neuralgic headache have ocular defects. Over 600 of the patients 'examined were altogether unaware of their defect. Fully half the cases were of only slight refractive errors or muscular un- balance, and it is in times cases in which ciliary spasm is the direct factor in causing headache in persons whose occupation calls for near vision, that accommodative astheno- pia results. There is no apparent relation, re- marks the Family Doctor, between the se- verity of the headache and the degree of the ocular defect, and nothing especially char- acteristic, except, perhaps, the patient's non- suspicion of the cause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>BREAKING UP AN ELEMENT.</head>
        <p>BREAKING UP AN ELEMENT.</p>
        <p>Following the statement made by Sir Wil- ! liam Ramsay that a solution of copper eul- • phate in distilled water shows in the spec- troscope the characteristic red line of lithium when placed in contact with the emanation from radium,. comes the report that the German chemist Dr. Theodore Grosse has succeeded in breaking up the ele- ment platinum. He for a number of hours subjected potassium carbonate, maintained at a high temperature in a platinum vessel, to the action of an alternating current be- tween platinum electrodes. After some time, the electrodes became coated with a deposit of charcoal-coloured crystals, and gave evi- dence of baying been attacked, both the elec- trodes and the containing vessel losing weight. On extracting the melt, a brown pow- der free from carbon or potassium was ob- tainer:L. Although both the crystals ana tile powder gave solutions from which they were precipitated by hydrogen sulphide, the pt•e- -04;k2C0 og pir:tinum iiould not be deteete*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>LIGHT AND COLORATION.</head>
        <p>LIGHT AND COLORATION.</p>
        <p>An interesting phenomenon discovered "ty Marckwaldt consists of an alteration occur- ring in the colour of a body under the action of light, while the original coloration is re- established in the dark. The *substances shewing this phenomenon are very few in number, though, according to communication by Professor H. Stobbe at the Congress of German Naturalists and Physicists, the group of fulgides contains a number of substances of this kind. This phenomenon, called photo- trophy, is of especial importance, says the Scientific American, for chemical and physi- cal science, as possibly embodying an immedi- ate conversion of light waves into chemical energy. It might become extremely valuable for photography or for any other art utilising radiations of a similar nature..</p>
        <p>S'ONVICE, or THE INFINITESIMALL), SMALL.</p>
        <p>The Lancet gives some striking examples of the importance of little things. We know what infinitesimally small quantities of cer- tain substances will put an end to the great vital processes, and we know also how end- less appears to be the action of the enzymes or ferments which render food assimilable so that the same vital processes are sustained. A thirtieth part of a grain of aconitine will kill the human organism, one part of an enzyme will transform 100,000 parts ot cane sugar into invert sugar, the enzyme of malt will convert a thousand times its weight of starch into sugar, and so forth. Nor is the enormous action of infinitesimally small quantities confined to the organic or or- ganised world. Even certain materials devoid cif life are found to exert a similar action,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>Smoking Concert.</p>
        <p>Our local Parliament enjoyed a pleasant relaxation from its labours on Monday evening when a smoking concert was held at the Lion Hotel. Admirable arrange- ments had been made and the affair proved a distinct success. The Speaker (Mr. C. Plinston) presided. There was a large attendance of members and each as allowed to bring one male friend. The CHAIRMAN proposed the usual loyal toasts,which were duly honoured. He afterwards made an announce- ment that they were going to have a picnic during the summer. (Hear, hear and applause). Those in favour of that please say " Aye" (Loud " Ayes ") He believed the "Ayes " had it. (Laughter). A MEMBER : I challenge a division. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN then called upon Councillor Steel to propose the health a the visitors coupling with it the name of Councillor Fulton-Smith and expressed the hope that at that time next year he would not be a visitor but a member.. _</p>
        <p>• Councillor STEEL said that before proceeding with the toast he would like to thank the managers of the Hippodrome for their kindness in allowing one of their artistes to entertain them. (Applause.) Proceeding with the toast he said that when they had had their debates in the Co-operative Hall the visitors had always been separated from the members of the Society. That night the members were pleased to have them with them and to sit with them. (Hear, hear.) During their debates the visitors had only to listen but that night they could share with them. He hoped they would all become members of the society and next year bring their friends with them and so increase the numbers of members of the society. They were proud of the visitcrs' presence, especially of Councillor Fulton-Smith's who they knew was a man of many engagements. The toast was heartily carried. Councillor FULTON-SMITH responded. Ho said he did not know why he had been invited that night. He had taken a great interest in the debates and he con- sidered that they as a Society were doing a great deal for the development of the mental calibre of the young men of the town. Young men as a rule had got very slight notions of what was right and what was wrong in regard to public principles and it was only by such societies as theirs that they were enabled to correct any erroneous ideas and to be able to bring to their minds the correct view of what was obtaining in public opinion. To-day, be thought, consistency or inconsist- ency was a subject of praise or censure amongst public men, and debating societies such as that enabled a man to correct his conceptions in the full light of all the facts, to look upon things from both sides, and to give credit to his opponent that he was honest in his opinion. A man ought not to be blamed for his opinions. It was very hopeful to find in Warrington a company of young men like theirs prepared to go into dialectics and ; polemics. The mere question of debate he considered a subsidiary one. The great object was that each man should study for himself the great questions which affected their lived. Each man had his individual abilities and it was incumbent upon all to display that ability which was peculiarly their own. If they found out what was their particular proclivity, and followed it faithfully and well, they would do some good to the country, and they would confer benefits not only upon themselves but upon others. He thanked them very much for the way they had received the toast of the visitors. (Applause.) Mr. AWBERY, Chief Secretary for Ireland, alluded to the death of Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman, and said everybody would admit that he was sincere in all he did. lie was an amiable and distinguished men whom the country could ill-afford to lose. (Hear, hear.) Councillor MELD, Leader of the Opposition, endorsed the remarks of the Chief Secretary and said that not only was Sir Henry a distinguished statesman, but he was above all, a man. All parties sincerely regretted the loss which the country had sustained by his death. Mr. AWBERY proposed the toast of" The Speaker of the local House of Commons." He said it was a matter of great concern as to who should be the Speaker when the society was first formed, but in Mr. Plinston they had a gentleman who had held the office with dignity and with great success. (Hear, bear.) Mr. Plinston had received praise from all parts of the House and it was with the greatest pleasure that he proposed that toast, which was really the toast of the evening. (Applause.) Councillor NIELD seconded and heartily endorsed all that was said by Mr. Awbery. He had known NIT% Plinston for many years and the longer he knew him, the more he admired him. __</p>
        <p>Ceuncillor TOOLE, Leader of Nationalists, supported, and said it was a high compliment indeed that Mr. Plinston had been able to please the Nationalists. He was glad that they had had such an able Speaker and hoped Mr. Plinston would be their Speaker for many years to come. (Applause.) Councillor HALL, Socialist leader, also supported, and snid his party had been satisfied that in Mr. Plinston they had a speaker who had filled the office with dignity. The Socialists endorsed all that had been said that night. (Applause). The toast was received with musical honours, and Mr. C. PLItisTON responded. During the evening, an excellent musical programme was provided, the following being the artistes :—Messrs. J. Willett, F. Fieldhouse, C. Plinston, N. Cook, jun., W. Kilduff, and Victor Roberts (humorist). By I ki • nd permission of the management of the Hippodrome, Mr. Harry Deaton gave two very popular songs. Mr. W. H. Payton was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
