      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 2, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 2, 1909.</p>
        <p>pensation was paid by the brewer if he owned the house, to the brewer who owned the house, so what was he going to lose by it? It WAS not the present Bill but the 1904 measure that was the robbery Bill and the persons who committed the robbery were not the people of the present day, nor Mr. Asquith and his Bill, but the brewers when they took from the people what they had no right to take. (Applause.) air. Jarman then spoke fro .n personal experience of the drink domination at Barton-on-Trent and said he believed the mouth of every C'aristian 'minister there was closed by the fact that perhaps three parts of his congregation if not more, were directly interested in the breweries as they were employed there. Mr. Jamul strongly appealed to his hearers to support the Bill and do some- thing to stem the tide of evil which was sweeping everything before it. (Applause.) The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK also supported. He said he did not want to see England throttled about her neck by one trade. He did not care if it was the parsons; he would oppose them. He would have no one trade putting its hand on his throat, not even the preaching trade. (Laughter). He did not want to take the work- ingman's glass of beer. He believed the public-house properly conducted was a necessary thing in the English social system. He wanted to see the house properly ' conducted and that the man whose name was over the door should be the proprietor. (Applause). The Bill would pass, let them make no mistake about that. (Applause and a Voice: " Manchester did not say so.") If the Bill hecame law and it applied to Warrington, how many licences would be refused ?—thirteen. (A voice: ' "No. We are eight short now.") They were eight too many and the reason was because they were tied—they were 'putting money into the brewer's pocket instead of the publican's. (A Voice : " They are well conducted.") daresv they are retorted"said Mr. Cook. "I am well conducted when a policeman has his eye upon me." (Laughter.) Continuing Mr. Cook said the Bill did not in the least degree want to take away the public-house. All it said was that no trade should be the one trade in the country that should dictate to the nation what it should or should not do.(Applause.) For every £lOO worth of beer turned out, the wages paid were only 59. At Monks, Hall and Co's works, for every £lOO worth i of ! stuff turned out, the workers got £53 in wages. , (Applause.) They admitted that the trade paid the largest revenue in the country, but what did it cost us in polka, workhouses, asylums, prisons ? (A Voice : " There would still be prisons if there were no drink.") Yes there would still be, but not so many. (Applause.) The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried by a good maiarity, the dissentients numbering abont fifty. The Rev. C. HARVEY-COOK asked the supporters of I the Bill to signify their approval of the resolution by I cheering, and this they did right lustily. Not to be outdone, the opponents also had three cheers—but their effort was comparatively weak. Mr. G. WALLINGTON proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman and speakers. He said he supported the Bill heart and soot because it was a splendid measure and ought to satisfy every moderate man. The Liberal and Conservative Clubs in Warrington were not drinking clubs, but if there were rooms in the town where the main purpose was drinking alone, they should be put under the same rules as public-houses. Properly con- ducted clubs would welcome inspection. (Applause.) Mr. W. CLEGG seconded, and said the Bill was one I that every reasonable working man should be proud of. (Applause.) I The voto was heartily accorded and the meeting closed.</p>
        <p>results in this trade we are justified in saving that the tied-house system might have been specially devised for the making of drunkards. Suppose a brewing company's tenant manager knowing the condition of Ethel (13), Lucy (7), and Maggie Harris (2) refused to serve their drunken mother, this woman knows that nearly next door there is another tenant whose anxiety to keep up his sales and make a living outweighs his desire Ito shelter this poor woman from her own folly. What does the brewing manager who has to look after the turnover of say 500 licensed houses know about the condition of that " home " in Winvvick-street ? And if he knew would he, being a servant, allow such knowledge to throw his responsibility to the employers into confusion? The shareholders know nothing of the elements that go to make their dividends, in many cases have never been at a shareholder's meeting, never seen their property in licensed houses, never visited such " homes" as that of Mary Harris, never seen the uplifted right hand of the drnnken mother "striking the baby's face." The whole system is bad from first to last. The sad case of Mary Harris (unhappily by no means exceptional) has led me somewhat astray from the main point, but it presents an aspect of the case which those who are calling out confiscation and robbery" do not appear to appreciate or refuse to see.</p>
        <p>I am with Mr. Rylands in his plea for the "stability of property," that is every man being entitled to his own, and only his own, but I do not agree with him in his statement that " upon this alone can the continued prosperity of the State be maintained." Human lives, individual character, a pure motherhood, a happy healthy childhood, must be considered. Surely it is the duty of the State to remove the unneceszary facilities which endanger all these. The call is to every man who loves his country and his kind to support a Bill which, while it leaves unaffected reasonable facilities, shows a just awl generous consideration to property interests, pro- vides means for abolishing unnecessary temptations, which so many of our country man and wouieu are unable to withstand.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, April 29th, 1908.</p>
        <p>THE WESLEYAN MEETING.</p>
        <p>A fall report of the Wesleyan demonstration in Bold- street Church on Sunday last will be found on. page 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>rUbLIC V. BREWER..</head>
        <p>rUbLIC V. BREWER..</p>
        <p>LICENSING BILL ADVANCES ANOTHER STEP.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Campaign in Warrington.—Liberal Call to Arms.</head>
        <p>The Campaign in Warrington.—Liberal Call to Arms.</p>
        <p>CITIZENS' TEMPERANCE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>LIVELY MEETING AT CHURCH-STREET</p>
        <p>Opponents' Interruptions</p>
        <p>The W.trrington Citizens' Temperance League is actively pursuing its campaign in support of the Licensing Bill and another public meeting was held in the National Schools, Churchlstreet,on Nlonday evening.</p>
        <p>The room was well Ailed and the 'audience Included-a</p>
        <p>group of opponents of the measure who kept up a fusillade of interruptions, generally of an irrelevant character. So tactfully were they handled by the speakers, however, that on the whole the proceedings passed off with good humour. Mr. George Walker pre- sided and was supported by the Revs. R. E. Letheren and C. Harvey Cook, Mrs. Simpson, Mrs. Osborne, Councillor Skelland, Messrs. C. E. Parker,G. Wallington, J. Griffiths, E. England and W. Clegg. The CmurtaLta: said that was not a politieal meeting. They were met to support the Government's Licensing Bill and they felt that the objects of that measure were so I FRENZIED'APPEALS. far-reaching,touching the moral and physical well being as to the terrible effects of the red uction of public of the nation, that it demanded the support of all right- houses and there had been brought before their thinking men without respect to their political creed or , astonished minds a terrible and weird vision of cities convictions. (Hear,hear.) He was not an abstainer. They deserted, without inhabitants —that WAS a little were frequently told that the Bill was promoted by exaggerated perhaps—(Laughter and appleuse)—and of teetotal bigots and faddists. He had admitted that he not only the brewing trade but kindred trades,—the was not an abstainer and as he had an intimate acquaint- butcher, the baker and the candlestick maker,—trades ance with the conditions under which the working that were dependent on the brewery industries all show- classes laboured and had also practical experience of lug hosts of unemployed and trying to tell us that the the seamy side of our civilization, fie thought he alight whole of society, and especially the whole of industry, claim that his support was not that of a bigoted would be overwhelmed in a great and terrible catas- teetotaler or faddist. Apart from that he failed to see trophe (A Voice : " Dear me.") He caught a remark that the opinion of even the that the reduction had been going on already. Of BIGOTED TEETOTALER OR FADDIST course it had, and he wanted to know what had become was not deserving of as much consideration and might of the poor publicans—(applause)—who had thus been , not be as just and generous as that of any capitalistic deprived of their livehood. If this reduction had been I brewer, privileged parson or interested publican. going on slowly already, the Bill only proposed to (Applause.) The Bill was not a perfect Bill but it was accelerate the movement to a slightly quicker extent a very great step in the right direction. He would like and to make it a little more sure. The brewers, as a Ito have seen the Bill hit hardly at clubs. (Applause.) consequence of Mr. Balfour's Bill, had been getting rid Some within our own town deserved serious and search- of a lot of property which they were only too heartily lug examination. He would also like to have seen it glad to get rid of because it long ago ceased deal wholesale with the tied houses, the owners of to bring any profit to them. Whet he could which they knew shirked their responsibility as rate- not help admiring as a piece of consunam its skill on the 'payers. They desired that while they paid their fair part of the brewers was that they were able somehow [quota to the rates the brewers should do the same. The to turn what in every other industry would be a dead 1 licensing question was not one that could be laid on loss into a glorious gain, by being compensated for the one side. When they could see around them the loss of houses that had long been a dead loss to them; misery directly traceable to excessive drinking he (A Voice : " Who pays the compensation ? " and felt that if the nation lost this opportunity another interrupter, " Who pays the best wages ?—the of getting within its grasp again the management of brewers.") That was one of the strokes of genius in licensing affairs, we would suffer fora very long time, the whole business. The compensation certainly was It was no use saying that there was no demand for the not. taken away from the trade—it was kept within Bill. There yens a very serious demand. They had the bounds of thetrade—and that was one of the master- nothing to do but look round our towns, and study the pieces of the scheme.(A Voice : "It does not belong to the workmen in the workshops and they would see the State.") The State gave it its being because of the necessity for the sale of intoxicants being brought monopoly.(Apnlause and a Voice :"What does ale fetch under more rigorous administration. He would like to ,in for the revenue ?") Someone had asked as to which have seen in the Bill an attempt to deal with trade paid tbe best wages. He wanted to ask them what</p>
        <p>GROCERS' OFF-LICENCES. He quite admitted that Mr. Gladstone in introducing the measure had a very noble object in view. His idea was that a man's wife and children should not be forced to go to the public-house in order to obtain the supper or dinner beer. But they knew how that object had been grossly perverted and they had reason to feel that it had considerably aided the most deplorable extension of drinking on the part of women. (Applause). Mr. J. Guteerras moved :—" That this meeting heartily supports the Government's Licensing Bill, believing that its proposals will promote sobriety and diminish excessive drinking." He said it was admitted by all parties that teetotallers only numbered one in ten of the population, so that if the measure were a teetotallers' Bill it would be absolutely impossible for it to be carried. An objection commonly urged was that the Bill would not promote temperance. Those who made it, however.,had no alternative proposals to increase sobriety in this land. Mr. Griffith 4 then ' quoted some striking statistics showing that the retitle- ' tion of licences in Liverpool, Birmingham and Ireland had resulted in a marked decrease in the convictions for drunkenness. The licences in Ireland in 1902 numbered 25,252 and in 190 G they had fallen to 23,835 e reduction of 1,417. The arrests for drunkenness in 1902 totalled 91.276 and in 1909 77,262, a diminution of 14,000. In 1902 the sum of .C 102,612 was spent for the maintenance of Irish prisons ; in 130 S the amount was £93,692—a saving of nearly £9,000 in five years. (Applause.) Mr. Griffiths contended that the tied-house system was largely responsible for excessive drinking, and his arguments on this point were continually inter- rupted. Tenants, he contended, were compelled to do things which they would not do if they were free men, in order that the houses might produce sufficient return. What did the trade cost the State? (A Voice : " You will have to pay 4d. a pint in a bit and " How is</p>
        <p>this ti:ade was which i it was theatened with a slightly faster reduction than had been taking place under Mr. Balfour's Act was going to create such consternation in the business and commercial parts of the country. Fifteen breweries had been turning out during the year beer to the value of £257,000 and they employed 391 men. A similar set of trades so far as money was concerned—biscuit, boot, brick and jam trades—during the same twelve months turned out commodities to the value of £393,000 and employed 10/0 men. (Applause.) In order to turn out £1,000,000 worth of coal, 8,611 men were employed and their wages amounted to £650,000. To turn out the same worth of beer 1,795 men were employed and the wages bill amounted to £161,000. (Applause.) Fle thought "the trade" deserved its name, because it was a trade that PAID LESS WAGES in proportion to its profits and killed more employees in proportion to the number it employed than any other trade in the worlds (Applause.) For some time he was puzzled to know why that particular industry called itself Me trade. At last it came to him as the result of prolonged meditation on the subject that it called itself the trade because there was no possible adjective in the heaven above us, or in the earth around us, or (with an expres- sive gesture) beneath Us. that could be dragged up in order to qualify the trade. (Applause). Heaven above sought its dictionarini in vain. because they could not find an adjective to express their detestation of it. (A voice: "How do you know ?'' and laughter). He mast confess, said Mr. Lethereo afters pause, that he did not know. (Laughter). But he did know this : there was no word to be found in his English dictionary bad enough to express the whole gruesome results of what the trade brought about. (Applause). The tied house system was A MASTERPIECE OF SKILL AND CRAFT</p>
        <p>Churchill going on?")' The CHAIRMAN at this stage had to appeal for order. Mr. GRIFFITHS again asked what the trade cost the 1 State? (A Voice: "Nothing,") The most valuable assets of the State were order, sanity, well being and domestic peace. What did the State cost in prisons, workhouses and asylums? The Chief Constable of Nottingham a short time ago stated that 1 50 PER CENT. OF OCR CRIME WAS DUE TO DRINK. Messrs. Rowntree and Sherwell had stated in their Istandard book on the question of temperance reform , that in the years 1857 to 1897 the rate of general crime i diminished 23 per cent, but crime due to drink increased 51 per cent. Since the beginning of the twentieth century the offences against the law due to drink had not been leFs than 200,000 in a single year. He wanted j to ask that meeting of sane and sober-minded men, how !much profit would remain if the trade had to pay for the protection of society against the results of the trade ? He asked : Would the country tolerate such ;results from the conduct of any other trade ? ("No.") The greatest noise and storm beat about the financial !proposals of the Bill. It would only be a proportion of the brewers' capital that would be in danger. Legitimate profits from brewing would not the affected, but only the profits which were due to the tied house system. The trade had been talking about robbery and confiscation. I Now the word " exaction" was more frequently heard. I What was it but exaction for brewers to charge tied house tenants Gs. per barrel more for beer than they , would have to pay if they bad a free and open market? (A voice : " Good quality.") The profits which enabled the brewer to pay such fabulous prices for property accrued because the State protected the trade from free competition (Here an interrupter put in the exclama- tion" Glorious beer." Another, evidently alluding to Mr. Griffiths' argument, said " I can't see it.") Mr. Griffiths replied that there were none so blind as those who would not see. (Applause and a Voice : "Tell us !something about Manchester.") The Bill sought to recover the extra profit at the end of the time limit in the form of licence duties. The profits of the trade were of a fabulmis character and because they could afford to be outrageously extravagant in the manage- ment of their business and. throw money about in a Ireckless manner, it was no reason why we should listen 'to their whining and howling appeals. Let them manage their business with economy and care and put their house in order and fourteen years would be ample time to make provision for the security of their capital. (Applause). Here one of the opposition—evidently a thirsty soul —exclaimed : "Sup up and have another pint." The Rev. R. E. LETHEREN seconded the resolution. He said that from his point of view it seemed to him that whatever might he the colour of a man's political opinions, be could not in common honesty keep back from a Government that set itself seriously to under- take the task of temperance reform some measure of whole-hearted admiration. When Mr. Asquith broughtl the Bill before the House of Commons he knew very I well that it was not a Bill to catch votes. He knew what was the force of the traffic against which the Bill was setting itself and a Government that was</p>
        <p>well worthy of the trade. Eighty-five per cent of the public-houses in England were tied hand and foot to great brewery companies, and he had seen it pub- lished that the free publican could buy his beer for about 10s. a barrel less than the tied house man. ' (`‘ Rot.") They must. see that the brewers were not going to buy tied houses unless they could get some- thing from it. (Applause.) In addition to the illegiti- mate profits which came from such a coercion of busi- ness as that, some little time ago when there was what people called a wave of temperance the brewers, with the skill which had always marked their transactions, thought they would form their breweries into limited companies. Of comae, because the commercial aspects of the brewing business had always fascinated the minds of those who had looked upon them with admiration land astonishment, they could not wonder that there was a wild rush on the part of the public to buy as many shares in those companies as they possibly could. It was not long before the shares in the brewery com- panies were dropping just as one would drop a hot brick. (A Voice : Why ?") lie would tell them why, in the words of the financial editor of the " Times " which was not exactly a Liberal paper. (Laughter.) lie said that brewery shares were dead before the Licensing Bill was heard of, and that the speculation in tied houses which culminated ten years ago was the cause of the collapse. Now we bad the PITIFUL SPECTACLE OF THE WIDOW AND ORPHAN brought before our gaze and with the same skill we were asked to look upon those sad results of what was due to the fraud of the brewery companies and to pass the hat round and to help them in their calamity. The Bill was worthy of every man's support because it was courageous and it had not anything selfish in it from the first letter to the last. The Bill aimed at promoting temperance in a temperate way. It was not a violent Bill. It was no teetotalers' Bill. It was far too tem- perate to be a teetotallers' Bill. Teetotallers were the biggest fanatibs in the world—(laughter)—so their friends told him,' and he defied anyone to say that was an intemperate Bill. He thought as public opinion was informed about the Bill and, as the publicans looked into the Bill for themselves, it would command their support and if only they were free be believed to a man the publicans would support the I measure. (Applause.) Although the battle was a stern one and was 140 t going to be easily %von he ventured to think the v'Mtory was sure. (Applause.) Tho latest information was that it was coming nearer home. (Laughter and great applause from the opposition.) It had not come near enough yet. (Applause and a Voice : "It will do.") It was not very long since eight repre- sentatives of the brewers put their names to a sentence of this kind : that in this country a gigantic evil remained to be remedied and no sacrifice was too great to bring it about. "My friends," said Mr. Letheren, "no matter what Peckham says, or what in a fit of frenzy North-West Manchester may say—(applause from the opposition)—How any sane man could expect a con- stituency of that sort at such a crisis as this to put in Winston Churchill I don't know. We are not living in the age of New Testament miracles now. In your hearts you know there is in this country a gigantic wrong that remains to he remedied and you know that his Bill is an ho-</p>
        <p>WILLING TO RISK ITS VERY EXISTENCE on a measure of that kind demanded every honest man's support and encouragement—(applause)--because nobody could conceive that there was in the Bill to start with any party interest. If the Bill were instigated by any- thing that was selfish he thought it would be of a very different character. Nothing but a sincere determina- tion to grapple with a great moral problem could have inspired such a Bill as that and he appealed to every fair and honest man whether that was an unreasonable attitude to take with regard to it. When a Government set out to deal with the liquor traffic it found itself rushed up against bags of gold and to try to stem the traffic in drink was to find themselves face to face with the strongest monopoly that modern times knew anything at all about. They knew what wealth the traffic wielded. They knew that the inside of every public-house was a committee room on behalf of the trade, and to a large extent on behalf of the party with which the trade was more closely connected. (This statement so roused the ire of one individual that he I tried to prevent the speaker from proceeding, and was told that if he did not behave himself a policeman would be called in.) Just as the inside of every public-house was a commiteee room, continued Mr. Letheren, so the outside was at present a most beautiful picture gallery —(ldughter)—and a free advertisement hoarding. The I power that the drink monopoly wielded and the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <p>resources it could put to work were no mean enemy for I any Government to contend with, and the mere fact that they had such a Bill called for every good man's support. The Bill proposed a very gradual reduction of public-houses--a reduction which he thought they would all feel could not go on too quickly. (A Voice: "They have been reduced already.") " The public- house a public benefit " was the statement on oae of the productions they had seen staring them in the face on the various hoardings. He would like to ask his friends if they honestly believed the public- house was a public benefit ? (Cries of " Yes.") Did it benefit the health? (A Voice "It benefiCs your pocket.") Yes, the pocket of the brewers. (Applause.) He, however, said a public benefit and he did not think benefiting the pocket of the brewer was quite the same thing as benefiting the pockets of the public. Did the com non consent, with very few exceptions, of the whole medical faculty say the public-house was a benefit to the public health ? Was the public-house a heuefit to the mind ? (7 No.") Was it a benefit to a man's morals ? (" No.') A benefit to the municipality'? (" No ") Ask a policeman. (Laughter and applause.) I Ask the magistrate. (Applause and a Voice : "Go ' and ask the lawyer.") Was it a benefit to the home ? (Cries of "No" and " Yes.") Ask the women. He honoured that part of the Bill which was going to try I and make it a little harder for men to be tempted to take drink. (Applause.) They had all noticed on the placards</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>SECOND REAORIS of the BILL</head>
        <p>SECOND REAORIS of the BILL</p>
        <p>MR. ASQUITH FIRM.</p>
        <p>The new Premier, Mr. Asquith, moved the second reading of the Licensing Bill in the House of Commons, on Tuesday, before a crowded attendance of Members. His speech was lucid and dispassionate as befitted the occasion, but struck an uncompromising ; note. There was no hint of any weakening in the Government's policy in his speech. His words spelt a resolute determination on the part of the Govern- ment to carry the contest through to a triumphant issue, and his speech was ',Mowed by bursts of applause from the Government's supporters. He declared once more that the main objects of the Bill ; were to improve the conditions under which the liquor traffic was carried on, primarily by a redaction in the number of licences, and to recover to the State ! with due regard, but not undue regard —awl at this there were loud cheers - its dominion and its contr over the drink monopoly. He combated with great energy the fallacy that lay at the root of the objection to the Bill that an annual licence was a freehold. Exceptions, he said, were not a form of property, and he advised the Opposition to beware of comparing property in land to an annual iicence. There was a melancholy abundance of con- fused thinking on the question of monopoly value, said Mr. Asquith as he reminded the Opposition that the State had nothing to do with the monopoly in tied houses that the brewers had endeavoured to establish. We are not going to take into account, saidi Mr. Asquith, competition that is fratricidal in intention ! and suicidal in effect. But the Government were pre- ! pared to give fair recognition to the expectation of the ! renewal of the licence, and it was for that reason that ! the time limit of fourteen years had been proposed, in order to p,ive the prudent trader time to insure against the loss which might come from a withdrawal of the monopoly value. The question was whether the State had a right to resume, after a certain time, the monopoly which she had herself created. If the Opposition denied this, were they prepared to assert that the Act of 1001 gave the publican —" no, not the publican," said Mr. Asquith, correcting himself, "but the brewer who had speculated in tied houses' —a right to the licence? On the question of the time-limit Mr, Asquith was willing to listen to argu- ment as to its exact length. But he defended the fourteen years as a generous allowance. On behalf of the Opposition Mr. Cave moved— " That this House declines to proceed further with a measure which while failing to promote the cause of temperance, violates the minciples of equity." Mr. Evelyn Cecil seconded the amendment. The. debate was continued on Wednesday and Thursday, and then adjourned until Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>LIBERAL CALL TO ARMS. Campaign in Support of the Bill</head>
        <p>THE LIBERAL CALL TO ARMS. Campaign in Support of the Bill</p>
        <p>In response to the circular letter issued by the Presi- dent (Mr. C. E. Parker) and published in last week's "Examiner," there was a large attendance of members at a special meeting of the Warrington Liberal Associa- tion held at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening to consider a plan of campaign in support of the Licensing Bill. Mr. C. E. Parker presided, and amongst others present were the Revs. C. Harvey Cook and J. L. Milne, ; Councillors Skelland, Dickenson, Wilkinson, Hawthorn, Cunnington, and Locker, and Messrs. E. J. Guest, T. Pemberton, W. H. Sheri:urn, T. Locker, E. England, J. I Jarman, W. Madden, W. Clegg, E. V. Walker, J. Morton G. Walker, P. O'Brien, E. Wynne Jones, F. Nlaginn, 6. Wallington, B. Farrar, John Upaon, W. Furness, and Joseph Howes, ;inn., (Liberal agent). THE LICENSING BILL. ANOTHER PARR HALL DEMONSTRATION. The CHAIRMAN said he trusted the members would acknowledge that he was justified in calling the Association together at such a crisis in national affairs. The doubt that there might be as to whether there was sufficient driving force of popular feeling to carry the Bill through rendered it very desirable that every Liberal Association should confer and devise the best methods of crystalising the enthusiasm which undoubtedly existed and giving it such an impetus that it would carry the Bill into permanent form as an Act of Parliament. He felt that the fault—if fault there be —in regard to the Warrington Liberal Association was not that they had been called together too often or unnecessarily but that they had not met quite often enough. It would be to the welfare of the party if when an occasion like the present arose they could have an interchange of views so that they could thereby further the interests they had at heart. He rejoiced in the enthusiasm which was evident on all sides in regard to the Licensing Bill. It bad, he believed, been evoked mainly by the bold and courageous measure which the Government had brought forward, because they ad been brave enough to challenge one of the strongest monopolies in the country. There was nothing like a bold fighting policy to arouse the enthusiasm of the Liberal party. He thought the enthusiasm of the party was to some extent due to the action of the other side. (Hear, bear). They had started on a course, exaggerating almost to the limit of falsehood and employing unworthy methods—dark green tickets and others— (laughter)—until every right thinking man came to the conclusion that if they had no better arguments to bring forward and no better way of oppoaing the Bill, there must be something very weak in their opposition and very strong in the Bill. (Applause). The object of calling that meeting was to consolidate the enthu- siasm which undoubtedly existed and maintain it until the Bill became law. It was necessary that a Rehting fund should be formed and that further meetings should be held in support of the measure. Mr. PARK Rlt'S suggestion was enthusiastically received and it was decided to hold ward meetings and also a mass meeting in the Parr Hall at an early date. The following committee was elected to carry out the arrangements:—Rev. C. Harvey-Cook, Councillors J. Hawthorn. J. T. Locker, J. Wilkinson, Messrs. C. E. Parker, William Madden, B. Farrar, 0. Walker, F. Maginn, J. Jarman, C. E. W. Nurse, J. Jeffrey, T. Wallington, E. Wynne Jones, J. Derbyshire, W. Clegg T. Pemberton and P. Peacock. A fighting fend was formed, a good start being forth- coming in subscriptions of .£lO each from Mr. A. H. Crosfleld M.P., and Mr. C. E. Parker. Before the close of the meeting a substantial sum was promised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Tied House Evil. BREWERS WHO PREFERRED POT-HOUSES TO TEA-GARDENS. PUBLICANS VICTINIISED.</head>
        <p>The Tied House Evil. BREWERS WHO PREFERRED POT-HOUSES TO TEA-GARDENS. PUBLICANS VICTINIISED.</p>
        <p>- - - The following letter appeared in the " Westminster Gazette " on Wednesday : Sir,—Two cases which have come to my personal knowledge may be, as I am assured they are, typical of the ways of the owners of tied houses and their tenants. One case occurred at a favourite stopping-place for cyclists and others, about twenty miles south-east of London ; the other at a much-frequented spot in one of the southern counties. In both cases the brewers (different firms) had bought up the licences at a somewhat exaggerated price, in view of the character of the houses and of the business. In both cases a tenant with a small capital was found, who was ready to improve the business. Both men succeeded in so doing by making their houses attractive to trippers and tourists, by providing tea, coffee, and other light refreshments. The success in each case emboldened the tenants to spending their own money in extending the sccommodation. The landlord-brewers offered no objection in either case, but in the following year complaints were made in each case that the tenants were not selling enough beer and spirits. this warning was repeated after a short interval, and in the third year of their (annual) tenancy the licensees In both cases were given notice to quit, without any compensation for the outlay made by them, on the ground that it was their "business to sell beer and spirits, and not to allow the use of the premises as tea- gardens." In both cases I made inquiries from their local constabulary as to the character borne by the two licensees, and of the nature of their l'usiness. In both cases the report was to the same effect ; that under the managements these former " pot-houses " had become real places of refreshment; that they had been con- ducted in every respect most satisfactorily. and that the return to the older state of things was much to be regretted.—l am, sir, your obedient servrnt, L. G. R.</p>
        <p>_ If you are not as well as you would like to be try a tonic. H. M. Ashton's Life Tonic is the ideal tonic, Is, per bottle from lI.M. Astuotl,6l, Sankey. it., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>WORK OF THE TEMPERANCE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE WORK OF THE TEMPERANCE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Inspiration for Future Effort. The Warrington Citizens"renaperance League is now established on a firm basis and gives promise of playing a prominent part in the life of the town of Warrington. Though only formed on Nlarch Orb last, the League has already done excellent work in educating the towns- people in the provisions of the new Licensing Bill by public meetings, posters, and leaflets. It cannot be too often emphasized that the League is entirely undenomina- tional and non-political ; and though a Temperance Leagne it is not a teetotal organisation, but one which . .</p>
        <p>auns„ at enrolling every irtii;idual in 'the town under the banner of licensing reform and a true temperance advancement. A special meeting of the General Council of the League was held in the Winniarleigh Cafe on Wednes- day evening under the presidency of nr. James Dodds. There was a large attendance. A comprehensive report on the work already accomplished was presented by the hon. secretaries, Nfessrs. G. A. Leach and H. Woolley. The report detailed the appointment of President, chairman and vice-chairman of executive, various sub- committees and the work they had undertaken. The different meetings held under the auspices of the League were commented upon, and the report concluded with an appeal for extended membershio of the League in the following words: "We appeal for your support by becoming members to-night and asking others to become members. And by so doing let us units in build- ing up an organization which shall be for the general uplifting of the people—au organization which has come to stay ! Let us make it so strong and so reitl that in the future it shall justify its name by the actual work it shall accomplish, and thereby purify and uplif; our civic and national life." T;10 reoort was warmly applauded. The Hoy. TRE &amp;SCRIM (Nit.. Dodds) presented a financial statement vhich showed that over £lOO had been raised of the suggested £l5O camp lign fund, and after paying expenses up to date there was still a con- siderable balance in hand. A very helpful discussion followed on the lines on which the League's work should be actively carried out. A continuance of the present methods was agreed upon. and additional meetings suggested. An invitation was sent to the Wycliffe Church, asking that a united meet- ing in support of the Licensing Bill should be held there following a Sunday evenin,, service at an early date. The rules were revised and confirmed, and arrange-</p>
        <p>ments made for securing a League membership of-at least 1,000, the minimum subscription to be Is. per annum. The name of Mr. D. Hodge was added to the Executive Committee. A very enthusiastic meeting concluded in a practical way—numerous subscriptions to the funds being offered. Mr. C. E. Parker and Tied-House Tenants. Reply to Mr. W. P. Rylands. In the course of a reply to correspondence with Mr. W. P. Rylands, Mr. C. E. Parker, J.P., writes : Mr. Rylands states his belief (which we hear in almost every speech delivered by opponents of the Bill) that this Bill will result in " an increase in drinking." If it does as Mr. Rylands believes, it will naturally increase the profits of the brewer, it will increase employment as far as the trade is concerned—although it will reduce employment in evety other direction. But does Mr. Rylands not see the ridiculous position he lands his friends in? At every tied house we have seen posters proclaiming the number of people this Rill will throw out of employment varying from two millions to a quarter of a million, and yet Mr. Rylands and many others are toiling us that it will increase liquor con- sumption. Would it not be well if the trade and their friends'were to elect on which stool they were going to sit ? Why kill their own case with contradictions ? It takes all the interest out of a controversy when the trade and its allies rake their own position by cross fire. I am quite willing to admit that Mr. Rylands is as anxious for sobriety as I am. I also willing to allow that licensees fre- quently make personal sacrifices, affecting and endanger. their own livehood, by striving to save the class of individuals who habitually take too mnch from the injurious effects of their own folly. What I would desire to point out to Mr. Rylands is that the present system is perhaps the worst possible that anyone could conceive to produce that sonriety and moderation which we both desire to see. Mr. Rylands says that " the tied house system may be bad, but it is one grown up on lines well understood." I do not question this ; the important point is not the manner of its growth but the results it is now producing, the fruits it Is now bearing under present conditions and laws. We have either licensees as tenants, or individuals as managers, who are debited with the beer they have to sell at a good desl more than the licensee of a free house has to pay. If I am wrong in this Mr. Rylands Call correct me. The tenant can only pay this extra cost and make a living for himself and family by selling a con- siderable quantity. If a manager does not sell enough he is replaced by one who Can keep up the sales. If brewers and their shareholders individually came personally into contact with the customers who drink to excess I doubt not they would discourage such. Bat the system eliminates the personal element ; the brew- ing industry like other trades is run to pay, treated as a business affair in which personal consideration for individual oases finds no place. The cash nexus is the determining bond between shareholder, brewer, and If w"- knowledge and contact existed'</p>
        <p>- - th.. _ is an honest attemet to do something towards servant, If personal ._ [edge .on remedying it. Therefore I do hope that every honest ; the part of the brewer and his shareholders it would man, every good man, every man who loves his country probably result in restraints being enforced, but in the will do his share towards helpine the Government which , case of the tenant and manager it places his own has brought forward such a Bill." (Loud applause). intereet, and his duty as a servant, in direct antagonism Mr. J. JARMAN in supporting explained that he was ; to any restriction he might otherwise seek to exercise not present in any official capacity. He had no right to ; upon those who drink to their hurt. speak on behalf of the Co-operative movement—he Does anyone for a moment think that any of the share- wished he had—but he was there as an individual to holders in the Companies that produce beer for War. speak in what he believed to be a right and a just ; rington, if they had accompanied Mr. Cox of the cause. (Applause.) He supported the Bill because he N.S.P.C.C. to the wretched home, now broken up, of honestly and firmly believed it would make for temper- Mary Harris, and which is described in the same issue once. If he did not believe that, it would not be which contains Mr. Rylands' letter, would not at once worth his while to have anything to do with it and it say" We want no dividends out of the beer that has would not be worth the while of the brewers to oppose wrecked this home." And yet his the servants of these it. (Applause.) He asked them to support the Bill Companies who are supplying this woman with the because of the tremendous need there was for such a thing that has robbed her of all that is tender in woman- measure. It would restore the control of the magis- hood, deadened the mother heart, sent her for three trates which ought never to have been taken away. He years to an inebriates' home, the father to jail, and the hoped the clause would be made imperative which had children to the workhouse, all at the public for it its object the keiping of children out of the public- expense. And yet this woman apart from house. (Applause.) When he listened to such language drink is described as "hardworking and industrious." as be had heard that night from the lips of one man at It is in part out of the sale of drink to such that the that meeting, it made him hope to God that they would dividends come. Ido not wish to say hard and bitter keep children eut'of the public house. (Applause.) He things about brewers and their shareholders ; I depre- also believed that behind a bar was no place for any cate and avoid all such. I recognize clearly as Mr. respectable woman. He would like to see the club Itylands points out that this system has grown up clauses strengthened. (Hear, hear.) Why was there so almost unconsciously ; it is part of the industrial much talk of robbery and confiscation ? The corn- development common to all trades. But viewing its</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LIBERALS AND THE LATE PREMIER. VALEDICTORY SPEECHES.</head>
        <p>LIBERALS AND THE LATE PREMIER. VALEDICTORY SPEECHES.</p>
