 to the conutry,ana Mr. Asquith deserved their heartiest backing. They were also glad to know that the next Budget would in all probability contain a fulfirnent of the long expressed desire of the country for old-age pensions. When that great reform was accomplished they would have reason to feel even more thankful to the late Chancellor of the Exchequer and the present Prime Minister. They hoped the Government would be able to do a tremendous amount of real and substantial work</p>
