      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>CLUB,</p>
        <p>REVIEW OF SEASON 1907-8.</p>
        <p>The football season which concludes to-day has been a c)mpa:atively uneventful one for the Warrington Club. Though they have maintained their position in the front rank of Northern Uraion organizations, they have failed to add a single trophy to the honours gained in past seasons. Half a dozen Cups have been competed for but not one has fallen to the prowess of the" Wiiepullers," and they have also had to surrender the Northern Union Cup gained amid such scenes of glory a year ago. It must not he concluded from this summary of deeds not accomplished that Warrington have had an unsuccessful season. They have done fairly well in nearly every competition, but they have disappointed their admirers by failing ta stay the course with the usual reliability. A team which has won 23 out of 37 matches cannot be said to have done badly. The worst that can be urged against Warring- ton is that they might and cold have done better. The great effort of the season's tournaments was made to retain possession of the Northern Union Cup. Warrington fancied their chances here, and there as no doubt that they made a bold bid for success. If luck had been with them they might have accom- plished their object, for the victories over Halifax and Runcorn proved the team to have lost little of its cup-tie prowess. The third round at Broughton proved fatal,but Warrington would never have had to bow the knee to the Rangers if one of the best for- wards in the team had not been crippled early in the struggle. Warrington could not be blamed for losing after this misfortune. Their performances in the League are open to more criticism. At one time they possessed an excellent coance of figuring in the first four, but the same old lack of resolution was evident in some of the away games, and the Club had to be content with the seventh position. It has always been a just complaint that the Warrington men do not enter into the Competition for the League Champion- ship seriously. If they laid themselves out in the determined fashion so characteristic- of teams like _</p>
