      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"An Unwedded Wife."</p>
        <p>A " play of human emotion " with this title has; drawn large audiences to the popular parts of the P.oyal Court Theatre this week. We do not think much of the play itself, which is full of sensationalism and impossible situations, but high praise can justly be accorded to Miss Ethel Van Praagh who presents a powerful study of the title part. Affecting as her emotionalism is, it is not over-done and her rendering throughout is marked by fine dramatic power com- bined with due restraint. The pathetic scene at the death-bed of little Reggie in-which the much wronged mother's grief finds vent is certaialy the best feature of the play. Mr. C. Ladge-Percy in the thankless part of Philip Blakestone is sufficiently villainous to earn the execration of the "gods " and Mr. R. Wilton Bedford gives a highly creditable portrayal of "Kit." The humour, chiefly of the broad type, is supplied by Mr. Will Casey as the irrepressible Napoleon B. Bloomer and Miss Jessie Gillette as the equally livelr! Caraway Crumpet. The play is elaborately mountedr.l the scene depicting the ruins of St. Kilda 'a Abbey being particularly fine. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. Next week Mr. W. Alexander Dallow will present the successful spectacular American-lndian play !' The world's great snare." The drama is produced by a powerful company, with massive and gorgeous scenery and effects and should prove a great attrac- tion. Exceptionally fine scenes depict the beautiful Indian village, the burning cabin and the celebrated black canyon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>ROYAL THEATRE OF VARIETIES.</head>
        <p>ROYAL THEATRE OF VARIETIES.</p>
        <p>A Local Contralto.</p>
        <p>The management of the above theatre announce a special attraction next week, Madame Annie Hallwood, a well-known Warrington contralto, having been engaged. Madame llallwood is a most accomplished vocalist, having been principal with William Lea and Joseph Carter's star companies, DeJong and Cross'a Manchester concerts, Stafford, Yorks and Portsmouth. Philharmonics, Liverpool Philharmonic and Hope Hall concerts, Southport and Harrogate Spa concerts, St. Francis Xavier's choir, Salisbury-street. Liverpool, Unitarian Chapel (late Charles Beard), Renshaw-street,. Liverpool, and Cork Philharmonic concerts.</p>
